import{t as e}from"./index-DeR-qv96.js";var t={meta:e,chapters:JSON.parse(`[{"number":1,"title":"Marseilles—The Arrival","volume":1,"summary":"On 24 February 1815 the ship Pharaon sails into Marseilles under its young mate, Edmond Dantès, whose captain died at sea. The owner, M. Morrel, promises to make him captain, and the supercargo Danglars watches him with jealous hatred.","details":["The three-masted merchant ship Pharaon arrives at Marseilles, steered in by a capable nineteen-year-old mate, Edmond Dantès. The shipowner, M. Morrel, rows out to meet her and learns that Captain Leclere died of brain fever on the voyage. The cargo is safe, and Dantès took command himself.","Danglars, the ship's supercargo (the officer in charge of the cargo and accounts), is about twenty-six. He fawns on his bosses, bullies those below him, and the crew dislikes him as much as they love Dantès. He tries to turn Morrel against Dantès, complaining that Dantès lost a day and a half by stopping at the island of Elba. Dantès explains that the dying captain asked him to carry a packet to Marshal Bertrand there. While on Elba he even spoke with the exiled emperor Napoleon, who remembered Morrel's uncle Policar. Morrel quietly approves, but warns that the visit could cause trouble if it became known. Danglars also hints that Leclere gave Dantès a letter, which shows he was eavesdropping at the captain's door.","Morrel invites Dantès to dinner, but Dantès turns him down: he wants to see his father first, and then his fiancée, Mercédès, at the Catalan village. He asks for leave to get married and then travel to Paris. Morrel says he means to make him captain, if he can win over his partner. Asked about Danglars, Dantès admits they quarrelled once (he even proposed settling it on the island of Monte Cristo) but praises his work. As Dantès goes ashore, Morrel and Danglars both watch him leave, with very different expressions."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","pierre-morrel","danglars","captain-leclere","napoleon","marshal-bertrand","louis-dantes","mercedes"]},{"number":2,"title":"Father and Son","volume":1,"summary":"Dantès finds his elderly father half-starved: the old man paid off Edmond's debt to their neighbour Caderousse and lived on almost nothing. The envious Caderousse visits, then reports to Danglars, and the two go off to spy on Dantès and Mercédès.","details":["Dantès hurries to his father's small upstairs room in Marseilles. The old man nearly faints for joy. Edmond soon learns that the cupboards are empty. Their neighbour Caderousse demanded repayment of a 140-franc debt Edmond owed him, so his father paid it out of the 200 francs Edmond had left him and lived on sixty francs for three months. Edmond empties his pockets onto the table and promises his father a better life once he is captain.","Caderousse, a tailor of about twenty-five, comes to congratulate Edmond. His friendly words cover envy, and he eyes the money greedily. He teases Edmond for refusing Morrel's dinner invitation and needles him about Mercédès having plenty of admirers. Dantès answers calmly that she will stay faithful, and leaves for the Catalan village.","Outside, Caderousse meets Danglars, who has been waiting for him. He complains that Dantès is 'insolent' about his promotion, and Danglars mutters that if they choose, Dantès may end up even lower than he is now. Caderousse mentions that Mercédès is always seen with a tall, fierce young Catalan she calls her cousin. Sensing a useful rival, Danglars takes Caderousse to La Réserve, an inn near the Catalan village, to drink and watch what happens."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","louis-dantes","gaspard-caderousse","danglars","pierre-morrel","mercedes"]},{"number":3,"title":"The Catalans","volume":1,"summary":"In the Catalan village, Mercédès firmly refuses her cousin Fernand, who loves her. Dantès arrives and she forces the two men to shake hands. Fernand runs off and is drawn into drinking with Danglars and Caderousse, and Danglars begins to see a way to ruin Dantès.","details":["The Catalans are a close-knit community of Spanish origin living on a point of land outside Marseilles. There, the beautiful young Mercédès turns down yet another proposal from her cousin Fernand, a fisherman. She is a poor orphan and loves him only as a brother, because her heart belongs to Edmond Dantès. When Fernand hints that the sailor may be faithless or dead, she says that if Edmond dies, she will die too.","At that moment Edmond calls from outside and the lovers embrace. Dantès sees Fernand's threatening face, and Fernand's hand goes to his knife. Mercédès says that if any harm comes to Edmond she will throw herself from a cliff, and with a commanding look she makes Fernand shake Edmond's hand. Fernand then rushes out in despair.","Caderousse and Danglars call him into their arbour at the inn. The drunken Caderousse mocks him as a rejected lover while Danglars watches closely. When the happy couple pass by, Dantès announces that the wedding feast will be the next day or the day after, and invites them all, Fernand included. He adds that he must go to Paris afterwards to carry out Captain Leclere's last commission. Danglars realises this means the letter from Elba, and an idea forms in his mind."],"characters":["mercedes","fernand-mondego","edmond-dantes","danglars","gaspard-caderousse"]},{"number":4,"title":"Conspiracy","volume":1,"summary":"Danglars eggs Fernand on, then writes an anonymous letter with his left hand denouncing Dantès as a Bonapartist agent. He pretends it is a joke and throws it away, but Fernand secretly picks it up.","details":["Danglars works on Fernand, who admits he would kill Dantès except that Mercédès has sworn to die if Edmond does. Danglars suggests there is no need for murder: prison walls separate lovers as well as a tombstone. The very drunk Caderousse keeps protesting that Dantès is his friend and must not be harmed, so Danglars keeps refilling his glass.","Calling for pen, ink and paper, Danglars points out that Dantès stopped at Elba, where Napoleon was in exile. He writes a denunciation with his left hand so the handwriting cannot be traced, addressed to the king's attorney (the royal prosecutor). It claims that Dantès carried a letter from Murat to 'the usurper' Napoleon, and from Napoleon to the Bonapartist committee in Paris, and that proof will be found on him, at his father's, or in his cabin.","Caderousse, grasping what this means, calls it an infamous shame. Danglars crumples the paper and tosses it into a corner of the arbour, saying it was only a joke. As Danglars leads the staggering Caderousse home, he looks back and sees Fernand pick up the letter, pocket it, and hurry towards the city. Satisfied, Danglars tells himself that the plan will now work on its own."],"characters":["danglars","fernand-mondego","gaspard-caderousse","edmond-dantes","mercedes"]},{"number":5,"title":"The Marriage Feast","volume":1,"summary":"At the joyful feast, Dantès announces that he and Mercédès will marry that very afternoon. Before they can leave, soldiers arrive and arrest him. Morrel returns with the news that the charge is Bonapartist conspiracy, and Danglars frightens Caderousse into silence.","details":["The feast is held at La Réserve. Morrel himself attends, which the crew takes as proof that Dantès will be captain. Old Dantès is dressed in his finest. Mercédès seats Fernand beside her as a 'brother', which torments him. Dantès announces that, thanks to Morrel's help, the mayor will marry them at half past two that very day. He will then go to Paris and hold a second celebration on his return.","As the party sets off, a magistrate arrives with soldiers and arrests Dantès 'in the name of the law', giving no reason. Dantès calmly says it must be a mistake and is driven away while Mercédès cries out to him. Fernand slips out and returns pale. Caderousse now remembers the letter and accuses Danglars, who insists he threw it away and blames Fernand. To explain the arrest, Danglars suggests some smuggled goods were found aboard.","Morrel comes back looking grave: Dantès is charged with being a Bonapartist agent. Caderousse wants to confess, but Danglars warns him that anyone who defends Dantès may be treated as his accomplice, and selfish fear wins. Fernand takes Mercédès home. Morrel then hands Danglars temporary command of the Pharaon and goes to plead with M. de Villefort, the deputy prosecutor. Danglars privately rejoices."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","mercedes","louis-dantes","pierre-morrel","danglars","gaspard-caderousse","fernand-mondego","villefort"]},{"number":6,"title":"The Deputy Procureur du Roi","volume":1,"summary":"At the same hour, the deputy procureur du roi (the king's deputy prosecutor), Villefort, is celebrating his own betrothal to Renée de Saint-Méran among fierce royalists. We learn his father is the Bonapartist Noirtier. He is called away to deal with the denunciation of Dantès.","details":["In an aristocratic mansion in Marseilles, a very different engagement party is under way. The guests are royalists who loathe Napoleon, now exiled on Elba. The Marquis de Saint-Méran toasts King Louis XVIII. His stern wife, the marquise, reminds her future son-in-law, M. de Villefort, that his father was a Girondin (a moderate revolutionary) named Noirtier who later served Napoleon as a senator.","Villefort explains that he has disowned his father's politics and even his name: Noirtier is a Bonapartist, while he is a loyal royalist called de Villefort. The marquise reminds him that the family has vouched for his loyalty to the king, so he must be especially hard on any conspirator. Villefort boasts about the death sentences he has won in court. His gentle fiancée, Renée, is frightened and begs him to be merciful to those she pleads for. The Comte de Salvieux reports that the king himself praised Villefort and approved the match.","A servant calls Villefort out. He returns delighted and reads the anonymous letter accusing Edmond Dantès, which his office opened in the chief prosecutor's absence; the accused is already held at his house. Renée begs him to show mercy on the day of their betrothal. He promises to be lenient if he can, then leaves happily to do his duty."],"characters":["villefort","renee-de-saint-meran","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","comte-de-salvieux","noirtier","louis-xviii","napoleon","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":7,"title":"The Examination","volume":1,"summary":"Villefort questions Dantès, is convinced he is innocent, and is ready to free him, until he sees that the letter from Elba is addressed to his own father, Noirtier. He burns it and, while pretending to protect Dantès, secretly decides to keep him locked up.","details":["Gérard de Villefort, twenty-seven, rich and about to make an advantageous marriage, walks to his office. On the way Morrel begs him to free Dantès, but Villefort, a royalist, treats the shipowner coldly as a suspected Bonapartist.","Dantès is calm and open. He says he is nineteen, has no politics, and loves only his father, Morrel and Mercédès. He does not recognise the handwriting of the denunciation. He explains how the dying Captain Leclere ordered him to take a packet and a ring to the grand-marshal on Elba, who gave him a letter for someone in Paris. Villefort believes him, thinks of pleasing Renée, and tells him he may go once he hands over the letter.","Then he learns the address: M. Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, Paris. Villefort turns white and reads the letter three times, fearing that if its contents come out, his connection to Noirtier will ruin him. He asks whether Dantès has shown it to anyone, burns it in the fire, and makes Dantès swear never to mention the letter or the name. Dantès thanks him as a friend. Villefort quietly gives an order to a police agent, then sinks into a chair. He curses his father's past, then smiles: this letter, which could have ruined him, will make his fortune."],"characters":["villefort","edmond-dantes","pierre-morrel","noirtier","captain-leclere","renee-de-saint-meran","mercedes"]},{"number":8,"title":"The Château d’If","volume":1,"summary":"Instead of being released, Dantès is taken by boat at night to the Château d'If, a grim island fortress for political prisoners. He begs to see the governor and threatens a jailer, so he is thrown into an underground dungeon.","details":["Dantès is held in a cell in the Palais de Justice until about ten at night, still trusting Villefort's promise. Gendarmes then take him by carriage to the harbour and row him out to sea. He passes La Réserve, where the party continues without him, and the Catalan village, where a single light burns in Mercédès' window. Pride stops him from shouting to her.","When a gendarme finally tells him they are going to the Château d'If, Dantès is horrified: there has been no trial and no formalities. He tries to leap overboard but is pinned down at gunpoint. He is locked in a damp cell for the night.","Over the next days he refuses to eat and keeps demanding to see the governor. He bitterly regrets that he did not swim for freedom during the boat trip; he is a famous swimmer and speaks Italian and Spanish. The jailer warns that a former prisoner of the cell, an abbé who kept offering the governor a million francs for his freedom, went mad and was moved to a dungeon. Dantès offers the jailer a hundred crowns to carry a message to Mercédès. When he is refused, he threatens the jailer with a stool. Labelled a madman, he is marched down fifteen steps into a dark dungeon, already close to losing his mind."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","mercedes","villefort"]},{"number":9,"title":"The Evening of the Betrothal","volume":1,"summary":"Villefort rushes to Paris to warn the king of a Napoleonic plot and win the credit himself. He brushes off a pleading Mercédès and feels the first stab of lasting remorse. We see how each of Dantès' friends and enemies spends the night.","details":["Back at the Saint-Méran house, Villefort privately tells the marquis that urgent business calls him to Paris. He advises him to sell all his government bonds at once, a hint that he expects political upheaval. He also asks for a letter from the Comte de Salvieux that will get him straight into the king's presence. He refuses to go through the keeper of the seals, because he wants all the credit for his discovery.","At his door he finds Mercédès, who has come to ask about Edmond. Villefort tells her coldly that Dantès is a great criminal and is no longer in his hands. Alone, he is seized by remorse: he has destroyed an innocent man to protect his own ambition. Had Renée or Mercédès pleaded at that moment, he might have signed the release. But only his valet comes in, to announce the carriage, and Villefort leaves. Renée, far from pleading for Dantès, now resents the prisoner whose 'crime' has taken her fiancé away.","Mercédès spends the night in despair with Fernand kneeling at her side. Morrel's appeals to influential friends are refused, since Dantès is now branded a Bonapartist. Caderousse shuts himself in with brandy but cannot drown his conscience. Only Danglars is content, sure of his place on the Pharaon, and he sleeps soundly. Old Dantès waits in agony for news."],"characters":["villefort","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","renee-de-saint-meran","comte-de-salvieux","mercedes","fernand-mondego","pierre-morrel","gaspard-caderousse","danglars","louis-dantes","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":10,"title":"The King’s Closet at the Tuileries","volume":1,"summary":"In Paris, King Louis XVIII teases his anxious adviser Blacas and trusts his complacent police minister. Then Villefort arrives and warns that Napoleon is preparing to leave Elba.","details":["In his private study at the Tuileries palace, Louis XVIII annotates his Horace while the Duc de Blacas warns of a storm brewing in the south. The king jokes away his fears. The minister of police, Baron Dandré, reports that Napoleon is bored and possibly going mad on Elba, even telling his old soldiers to 'serve the good king'.","Blacas says that a messenger recommended by M. de Salvieux has raced from Marseilles with alarming news. When the king hears the name Villefort, he grows serious. He knows Villefort's father is Noirtier, the former Girondin and senator, but says the son is so ambitious that he would sacrifice even his father.","Villefort, still dusty from the road, is shown in. He tells the king that Napoleon is arming ships and may already have left Elba, perhaps to land in Italy or France. He claims to have learned this from a Bonapartist sailor he arrested, whom he describes as carrying a spoken message for a Paris Bonapartist whose name he 'could not extract'. He says nothing about the letter or its address. Louis XVIII is confident the coast is well guarded, but promises his gratitude. Just then the minister of police reappears at the door, pale and trembling."],"characters":["louis-xviii","duc-de-blacas","dandre","villefort","napoleon","comte-de-salvieux","noirtier","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":11,"title":"The Corsican Ogre","volume":1,"summary":"News arrives that Napoleon has landed in France. The king rewards Villefort with the Legion of Honour, but Villefort is shaken when the suspect in a general's murder sounds like his father, and then that very man turns up at his hotel.","details":["Dandré confesses that Napoleon left Elba on 26 February and landed near Antibes on 1 March, and that he is advancing by way of Gap and Sisteron. The king learns this only on 3 March, by telegraph, and is furious and humiliated. He contrasts his whole police force with the single magistrate who foresaw it. Villefort shrewdly defends the minister, earning his gratitude, partly because a desperate minister might question Dantès and uncover the truth.","The king then asks about the death of General Quesnel, a royalist who disappeared after being lured to a Bonapartist club in the Rue Saint-Jacques. The police now think he was murdered, not a suicide. They describe the suspect: about fifty, dark, with black whiskers, a blue frock-coat buttoned to the chin and the rosette of the Legion of Honour, last seen near the Rue Coq-Héron. Villefort nearly collapses, then breathes again when he hears the man escaped.","The king pointedly asks whether he has visited his father, then gives him his own Legion of Honour cross. Back at his hotel, Villefort is told that a stranger matching the police description is asking for him. The visitor walks in and greets him as 'my dear Gérard'. It is his father."],"characters":["louis-xviii","dandre","duc-de-blacas","villefort","napoleon","general-quesnel","noirtier"]},{"number":12,"title":"Father and Son","volume":1,"summary":"Noirtier, a leading Bonapartist, calmly admits his role in the club linked to General Quesnel's death and predicts Napoleon will reach Paris within weeks. He shaves off his whiskers to disguise himself and walks out past the police.","details":["Noirtier locks the doors and teases his son for being in Paris instead of at his wedding. Villefort says he came to save him. Noirtier coolly confirms that he is vice-president of the Bonapartist club at No. 53 Rue Saint-Jacques. Villefort explains that he intercepted a letter from Elba addressed to Noirtier and burned it, because it would have got his father shot and ruined his own career.","Noirtier gives his own account of General Quesnel. The club believed he was on their side and told him the whole plan for Napoleon's return. Quesnel said he was a royalist, swore an oath of silence, was 'allowed to depart free', and 'lost his way'. Noirtier insists that in politics there are no murders, only obstacles removed. He predicts that Napoleon will be at Grenoble, then Lyons, and in Paris by around the 20th, welcomed rather than resisted, and proves how good his own network is by having found his son within half an hour of his arrival.","When Villefort repeats the police description, Noirtier shaves off his black whiskers and puts on his son's brown coat and hat. He tells Gérard to go home quietly and keep his head down, promising to protect him in return. He strolls past the agents watching the street. Villefort hides his father's clothes, burns the cane, and hurries back to Marseilles, hearing on the way that Napoleon has entered Grenoble."],"characters":["noirtier","villefort","general-quesnel","napoleon","louis-xviii"]},{"number":13,"title":"The Hundred Days","volume":1,"summary":"During Napoleon's brief return to power, Villefort tricks Morrel into writing a petition that makes Dantès look like a dangerous Bonapartist, and keeps it. After Waterloo, Dantès is forgotten in prison, his enemies move on, and his father dies.","details":["Napoleon retakes the throne. Noirtier's influence protects Villefort's post, and Villefort postpones his marriage until he sees which side will win. With Bonapartists now in favour, Morrel asks what has become of Dantès. Villefort coolly pretends to look him up in the register, claims he was sent off to some distant fortress, and explains that some prisoners are made to vanish without records. He then 'helps' Morrel write a petition to the minister. It praises Dantès as one of the most active agents of Napoleon's return, and Villefort certifies it.","Instead of sending it, Villefort keeps the petition, which would be damning if the king returned. After Waterloo, Louis XVIII is restored. Villefort becomes king's prosecutor at Toulouse and marries Renée de Saint-Méran. Dantès stays in his dungeon, forgotten by everyone.","Danglars, afraid Dantès might return for revenge, gets a recommendation from Morrel and goes to Madrid to work for a Spanish merchant, and nothing more is heard of him. Fernand broods over plans to kill Dantès and himself, then is called up in Napoleon's last conscription. Mercédès, grateful for his kindness, sends him off calling him her brother, which gives him hope. Caderousse, married and older, is sent to the frontier. Old Dantès loses all hope when Napoleon falls and dies in Mercédès' arms five months after his son's arrest. Morrel bravely pays for the funeral, even though helping a 'Bonapartist's' father is dangerous."],"characters":["villefort","noirtier","napoleon","louis-xviii","pierre-morrel","edmond-dantes","renee-de-saint-meran","marquis-de-saint-meran","danglars","fernand-mondego","mercedes","gaspard-caderousse","louis-dantes"]},{"number":14,"title":"The Two Prisoners","volume":1,"summary":"In July 1816 a prison inspector visits the dungeons. Dantès humbly begs for a trial, and the inspector promises to look into it. In another cell, the 'mad' Abbé Faria offers millions for his freedom. A note in Dantès' file dooms his hopes.","details":["A year after the king's return, an inspector-general of prisons tours the Château d'If with the governor. He is shown the 'dangerous' prisoner who once attacked the turnkey Antoine. Dantès, now subdued, asks only to know his crime and be tried. He was arrested on 28 February 1815, and it is now 30 July 1816: seventeen months, which to him feel like seventeen ages. He names Villefort as the man who arrested him and calls him kind. Moved, the inspector promises to examine his case.","Next they visit No. 27, the Abbé Faria, an Italian priest imprisoned since 1811 and thought to have gone mad in 1813. He is found drawing geometry on the floor of his cell. Faria says he was secretary to Cardinal Spada for twenty years and was arrested at Piombino. He dreamed of a united Italy. He asks to speak privately about a huge treasure and offers five, then six, millions for his liberty, promising to stay in prison while they check. The officials laugh it off as the familiar madness.","The inspector finds a note in Dantès' file, written in a different hand and added after he was locked up: 'Violent Bonapartist; took an active part in the return from Elba.' It echoes the petition Villefort kept. The inspector writes 'Nothing to be done.' Dantès, newly hopeful, starts marking the days on the wall, but months pass with no change. A new governor arrives who knows prisoners only by number, and Dantès becomes simply 'number 34'."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","abbe-faria","villefort"]},{"number":15,"title":"Number 34 and Number 27","volume":1,"summary":"After years of despair, Dantès tries to starve himself to death, until he hears someone digging behind his wall. He starts tunnelling towards the sound, and finally the other prisoner, No. 27, breaks through into his cell.","details":["Dantès goes through every stage of a prisoner's suffering: pride, doubt, pleading, prayer, then rage against the unknown enemies who wrote the letter. He finally decides to die by starving himself, secretly throwing his food out of the window. Nearly four years in, close to death, he hears a scraping sound inside the wall.","Hope returns. Dantès eats again, knocks to test whether the noise is a workman or a prisoner, and concludes from the cautious silence that it is a fellow captive. With a shard of a broken jug and then the iron handle of the soup saucepan, which he tricks the jailer into leaving, he digs out a stone. He reflects that he has wasted six years in prison when he could have been working like this.","When a beam blocks his way and he cries out to God in despair, a voice answers from below. The stranger has been in prison since 1811 and has never heard of Napoleon's fall. He explains that he was digging towards the sea but miscalculated and reached the wrong wall. He gives his name only as 'No. 27' and is wary, but is reassured by Dantès' age and sincerity. Dantès, who was nineteen at his arrest and is now not quite twenty-six, begs not to be abandoned, offering to love him like a father. The next day the floor gives way and an old man climbs up into his cell."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","abbe-faria","mercedes","louis-dantes","villefort"]},{"number":16,"title":"A Learned Italian","volume":1,"summary":"The newcomer is the Abbé Faria, a brilliant Italian scholar. He has made his own tools, pens, ink and paper, and has written a whole book in prison. His escape plan has failed, but his determination inspires Dantès to suggest a new one.","details":["The stranger is small, about sixty, with white hair and a long black beard. He shows Dantès a chisel made from a bed clamp, which he used to dig the fifty-foot tunnel. Climbing onto Dantès' shoulders, he sees that the window looks onto a gallery patrolled by soldiers, so escape through this cell is impossible. He introduces himself as the Abbé Faria, imprisoned since 1811. He was jailed for dreaming of uniting Italy and was betrayed by a ruler he trusted. He admits he is the 'mad priest' of the Château d'If.","Faria accepts the failure as God's will, but his example fires Dantès with fresh courage: if this old man could do so much, a young, strong swimmer could do more. Dantès proposes cutting a side tunnel into the gallery and killing the sentry. Faria refuses to shed blood. He prefers to wait patiently for a lucky chance, as in history's famous escapes.","Dantès is amazed to learn what Faria has done alone in his cell. He has written a treatise on a united Italian monarchy on strips of linen made smooth by a chemical process. He makes pens from fish cartilage and ink from soot and wine, and writes in his own blood for important notes. He knows some 150 books by heart and speaks five modern languages. Eager to see it all, Dantès follows him into the tunnel."],"characters":["abbe-faria","edmond-dantes","napoleon"]},{"number":17,"title":"The Abbé’s Chamber","volume":1,"summary":"Faria shows Dantès that Danglars and Fernand denounced him and that Villefort buried him to protect his father, Noirtier. Dantès swears revenge. Faria educates him, and they dig an escape tunnel until a fit leaves Faria paralysed.","details":["In his own cell Faria tells the time by the sun, and shows his linen manuscript, his fish-bone pens, his knives, a lamp made from melted fat, and a rope ladder. Dantès tells his story. Faria applies a simple rule: look for whoever gains from the crime. Danglars wanted the captaincy, overheard Captain Leclere and saw the letter. Fernand wanted Mercédès. Dantès remembers seeing them with Caderousse and pen, ink and paper at the inn the day before his wedding. Faria writes with his left hand to show how the accusation's handwriting was disguised.","Then Faria questions him about the deputy prosecutor who burned the letter. Hearing the names Noirtier and Villefort, he laughs and explains that Noirtier was Villefort's father. Stunned, Dantès goes back to his cell and swears a solemn oath of vengeance. Faria regrets having planted that passion in him.","Dantès asks to be taught, and Faria teaches him mathematics, history, science and languages. Within a year Dantès is a new man, cultivated and polished. Faria finally agrees to an escape plan as long as the sentry is harmed only as a last resort. For fifteen months they tunnel beneath the gallery.","On the eve of escape, Faria is struck by a seizure. Following his instructions, Dantès gives him drops of a red remedy, and he revives. But his right arm and leg are paralysed. He warns that a third attack, like the ones that killed his father and grandfather, will kill him. He urges Dantès to flee alone, but Dantès swears never to leave him while he lives."],"characters":["abbe-faria","edmond-dantes","danglars","fernand-mondego","gaspard-caderousse","villefort","noirtier","captain-leclere","mercedes","louis-dantes"]},{"number":18,"title":"The Treasure","volume":1,"summary":"Faria reveals his secret: a half-burnt letter from 1498 in which Cardinal Spada describes a vast treasure hidden in the caves of the island of Monte Cristo. Faria names Dantès as his heir and calls him his son.","details":["Faria shows Dantès a scorched paper and says it is his treasure, half of which now belongs to Dantès. Dantès fears the illness has made Faria truly mad and avoids him. That evening the paralysed abbé drags himself through the tunnel to tell his story.","Faria served the last Count of Spada, a descendant of a noble Roman family once said to be fabulously rich. In 1498, Pope Alexander VI and his son Cæsar Borgia made Cardinal Cæsar Spada and Cardinal Rospigliosi buy their posts, then invited them to dinner and poisoned them in order to seize their wealth. But Spada's fortune was never found. His will left his nephew only his papers and his gold-cornered breviary (prayer book). For three centuries the family searched in vain.","When the last count died, he left Faria his library and the breviary. In 1807, lighting a candle with an old bookmark from the breviary, Faria saw writing appear in the heat: invisible ink. With a second fragment he reconstructed the message. Spada had buried all his gold, jewels and gems, nearly two million Roman crowns, in the caves of the little island of Monte Cristo, to be found by raising the twentieth rock from a small creek to the east, in the furthest corner of the second opening. Faria was arrested as he set out to find it. The family is now extinct, so the treasure is his to give. Dantès protests that he is no relation, but Faria declares him 'the child of my captivity', and Dantès weeps on his neck."],"characters":["abbe-faria","edmond-dantes","cardinal-spada","count-of-spada","cesare-borgia"]},{"number":19,"title":"The Third Attack","volume":1,"summary":"Their tunnel is blocked when the gallery is rebuilt, and Faria prepares Dantès to recover the treasure alone. One night the third attack comes. Despite the remedy, Faria dies, and Dantès overhears the jailers planning to dispose of the body that evening.","details":["Faria talks constantly of the good the fortune could do. Dantès, remembering his oath, thinks instead of the harm it could do to his enemies. Dantès knows Monte Cristo, a deserted, cone-shaped rock between Corsica and Elba, and draws a map for Faria. Then the ruined sea gallery is rebuilt and their escape route is sealed. Dantès tells Faria that his real treasure is their friendship and all the knowledge Faria has given him. Faria makes him learn Spada's letter by heart, destroys the second fragment, and coaches him on how to reach the island and search it.","One night Dantès wakes to hear Faria calling. The old man is seized by the fatal attack. He stops Dantès from calling for help, so that their secret will not be discovered, blesses him as his son, and tells him to hurry to Monte Cristo. His last words are 'Monte Cristo, forget not Monte Cristo!' Dantès gives him twelve drops, then the rest of the phial, but by dawn Faria is dead.","Hidden in the tunnel, Dantès hears the jailers mock 'the madman' and his treasure. The doctor confirms the death with a hot iron to the heel. The jailer recalls that Faria once cured his wife's illness. The governor orders the body sewn into a sack, without a mass, since the chaplain is away, and removed between ten and eleven that night. When the cell is empty, Dantès climbs out into it."],"characters":["abbe-faria","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":20,"title":"The Cemetery of the Château d’If","volume":1,"summary":"Dantès takes his dead friend's place in the burial sack, planning to escape from the grave. Instead, the jailers tie a cannonball to his feet and fling him off the cliffs into the sea.","details":["Sitting beside the sack that holds Faria's body, Dantès is tempted by suicide, then passes from despair to a fierce will to live, punish his enemies, and reward any friends. A thought strikes him: only the dead leave this place freely, so he will take the dead man's place.","Using Faria's homemade knife, he opens the sack and carries the body through the tunnel to his own bed, arranging it to look as if he is asleep with his face to the wall. He takes Faria's needle and thread, climbs into the sack naked and sews it shut from inside. He plans to slit the sack and fight his way out if he is discovered, or to dig himself out of the grave once the gravediggers leave.","The jailer's seven o'clock visit passes without trouble. Late that night two men carry the sack out into the windy night, joking that the old man is heavy. They stop, tie something heavy to his feet with a cord, and carry him up some steps while the waves crash below, joking that it is no night for a swim. They swing him, 'One! two! three!', and throw him into the air. Weighted with a thirty-six-pound cannonball, Dantès plunges into the icy sea. The sea, it turns out, is the cemetery of the Château d'If."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","abbe-faria"]},{"number":21,"title":"The Island of Tiboulen","volume":1,"summary":"Dantès cuts himself free, swims through a storm to a barren islet, and is rescued by Genoese smugglers, posing as a shipwrecked Maltese sailor. It is 28 February 1829, fourteen years to the day since his arrest, and he renews his vow of revenge.","details":["With the knife in his hand, Dantès slashes open the sack, cuts the cord holding the cannonball, and surfaces, diving again to avoid the torch-bearing gravediggers above. Steering by the Planier lighthouse, he swims for over an hour through rough sea and reaches the rocky islet of Tiboulen, where he collapses asleep. A violent storm breaks. By lightning he sees a fishing boat smash on the rocks and its crew drown.","At dawn, knowing the alarm will soon be raised at the fortress, he spots a Genoese tartan (a small Mediterranean sailing vessel) leaving Marseilles. He guesses its crew are smugglers. He puts on a drowned sailor's red cap, grabs a piece of wreckage and swims to cut across the boat's course. He is close to drowning when a sailor hauls him aboard by the hair.","He claims to be a Maltese sailor from last night's wreck. He explains his long hair and beard as a religious vow, then proves his skill by steering the boat flawlessly. The captain agrees to take him on. Jacopo, the sailor who saved him, lends him clothes. When the alarm gun fires from the Château d'If, Dantès calmly says a prisoner must have escaped and goes on sipping his rum. Jacopo tells him the date: 28 February 1829. Arrested at nineteen, Dantès is now thirty-three. He wonders what has become of Mercédès, and renews his oath of vengeance against Danglars, Fernand and Villefort as the tartan races towards Leghorn (Livorno)."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","jacopo","captain-baldi","abbe-faria","mercedes","danglars","fernand-mondego","villefort"]},{"number":22,"title":"The Smugglers","volume":1,"summary":"Dantès sails with the smugglers of La Jeune Amélie, sees his transformed face in a mirror, is wounded in a skirmish with customs officers, and teaches the loyal Jacopo navigation. At last the smugglers choose Monte Cristo as a meeting place for their next job.","details":["The master of La Jeune Amélie, a Genoese smuggler, comes to trust his skilled and tight-lipped recruit, though he never learns who Dantès really is. At Leghorn, Dantès has his hair and beard cut and sees himself for the first time in fourteen years. His open, smiling young face has become pale, stern and thoughtful, with eyes that can see in the dark and a changeable voice. No one who once knew him could recognise him. He signs on for three months only.","They run contraband to Corsica and Sardinia. On one run he passes close to Monte Cristo but has no chance to land. In a fight with customs men off the coast of Lucca, a customs officer is killed and Dantès is shot in the shoulder. He bears it calmly, and notices that the officer's death barely moves him: his heart is hardening. Jacopo, a Corsican, nurses him devotedly and indignantly refuses a share of his money, so Dantès grows fond of him and teaches him navigation and the stars, just as Faria once taught him.","After two and a half months Dantès has learned the smugglers' trade, their contacts and their secret signs, and has saved a hundred piastres. He plans to hire a boat of his own, but cannot find a way to search the island without companions. Then, at a smugglers' tavern in Leghorn, the master proposes the deserted island of Monte Cristo as a neutral place to exchange a valuable cargo. Dantès hides his joy and agrees they should sail the next night."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","jacopo","captain-baldi","abbe-faria"]},{"number":23,"title":"The Island of Monte Cristo","volume":1,"summary":"On Monte Cristo, Dantès secretly finds carved marks on the rocks, then fakes a bad fall so the smugglers will leave him behind with a gun and a pickaxe. As soon as they sail out of sight, he springs up and heads for the treasure.","details":["After a feverish night of dreams about jewelled caves, Dantès steers La Jeune Amélie to Monte Cristo. The crew call him 'the Maltese' and obey him almost like a commander, and the old master sees him as his natural successor. He is the first to leap ashore. He is shaken when Jacopo says there are no caves on the island, but decides the entrances must have been blocked up.","The next day, pretending to hunt wild goats, he sends Jacopo back with a kid for the cooks. Exploring alone, he finds marks carved by human hands on the rocks. They lead to a large round boulder, then seem to stop. Looking down at his comrades, he reflects that they will happily risk their lives for a few piastres, while he hopes for far more.","Running back when they signal that the meal is ready, he slips and falls down a slope. He claims to be in agony with a hurt knee and, the master suspects, broken ribs, and refuses to be moved. The master, Captain Baldi, must sail to deliver his cargo. Jacopo offers to give up his share and stay to nurse him, but Dantès insists on being left alone with biscuits, a gun, powder and a pickaxe, asking them to send a fishing boat or come back for him in a week.","He watches the tartan sail away, marvelling that he has found true friendship among such men. Once it is out of sight, Dantès leaps up as nimbly as a goat, takes his gun and pickaxe, and hurries to the rock where the marks ended, crying, like the fisherman in Faria's Arabian tale, 'Open Sesame!'"],"characters":["edmond-dantes","jacopo","captain-baldi","cardinal-spada","abbe-faria"]},{"number":24,"title":"The Secret Cave","volume":1,"summary":"Alone on Monte Cristo, Dantès follows Faria’s clues, blasts away a great rock, and makes his way through two hidden grottoes to Cardinal Spada’s chest, overflowing with gold coins, gold bars, and jewels.","details":["Left behind by the smugglers after pretending to be injured, Dantès first climbs to the island’s highest point to make sure their ships have gone. He works out that the notches cut in the rocks lead to a hidden creek where Cardinal Spada could have landed in secret. A huge boulder puzzles him until he realizes it was never lifted into place: it was slid down a slope and wedged. Using the gunpowder his friend Jacopo left him, he blows out the wedge, levers the rock into the sea, and finds a flagstone with an iron ring, covering steps that lead underground.","Bracing himself for disappointment, he imagines that Cesare Borgia got there first, but goes down anyway with a wry “Perhaps!” The first grotto, lit through cracks in the rock, is empty. Remembering the words of the will, “in the farthest angle of the second opening,” he taps the walls, finds a patch of stucco painted to look like granite, and, after a moment when doubt nearly makes him faint, breaks through into a second, darker cave.","Digging in its corner, he strikes an oak chest bound with steel and engraved with the Spada coat of arms that Faria had so often drawn for him. He forces it open: one compartment holds gold coins, one bars of gold, one diamonds, pearls, and rubies. Half mad with joy, he runs wildly about the rocks, then kneels and prays. He counts a thousand gold ingots, twenty-five thousand old crowns, and handfuls of gems, then sleeps across the mouth of the cave with his gun beside him, a night of joy and terror."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","abbe-faria","jacopo","cardinal-spada"]},{"number":25,"title":"The Unknown","volume":1,"summary":"Dantès hides the treasure, sells a few gems, buys a yacht, and learns that his father is dead and Mercédès has vanished. Using the name Lord Wilmore, he returns unrecognized to Marseilles, buys his father’s old house, and visits the Catalans.","details":["Dantès carefully disguises the cave entrance and, when the smugglers return six days later, pretends he is still lame. At Leghorn (Livorno) he sells four small diamonds to a dealer, then gives Jacopo a brand-new boat and money, explaining that he has just inherited a fortune from an uncle. He sends Jacopo to Marseilles to find out what has become of an old man named Louis Dantès and a young Catalan woman named Mercédès.","At Genoa, Dantès buys a fast little yacht that had been built for an Englishman, paying well over its price, and has the builder fit a secret three-part closet at the head of his bed. He sails alone to Monte Cristo, moves the whole treasure into the hidden closet, and spends a week learning how the boat handles. When Jacopo arrives, the news is grim: old Dantès is dead, and Mercédès has disappeared.","Confident that his face has changed beyond recognition, Dantès sails boldly into Marseilles on an English passport. A sailor from his old ship, the Pharaon, talks with him without recognizing him and is rewarded handsomely for his honesty. Dantès visits his father’s old rooms, now rented by a newly married couple, and weeps where the old man died. He learns that Caderousse, the former tailor downstairs, now keeps an inn near Beaucaire. Under the name on his passport, Lord Wilmore, he buys the house for far more than it is worth, spends an hour in the Catalan village asking about people long dead or gone, gives the family he questioned a new fishing boat, and rides out of Marseilles."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","jacopo","louis-dantes","mercedes","gaspard-caderousse"]},{"number":26,"title":"The Pont du Gard Inn","volume":1,"summary":"Caderousse, now a struggling innkeeper with a sickly wife, is visited by an Italian priest who says Dantès died in prison and left a diamond to be shared among his friends. Caderousse reveals that old Dantès starved to death.","details":["Caderousse and his wife, Madeleine, nicknamed La Carconte, keep a failing roadside inn between Beaucaire and Bellegarde; a new canal has carried off their trade. She is pale, shaking with marsh fever, and constantly complaining; he answers that it is God’s will. One scorching noon a priest arrives on horseback, speaking with a strong Italian accent. He studies Caderousse closely and makes sure he is the former tailor of the Allées de Meilhan in Marseilles.","The abbé asks about a young sailor named Dantès. Caderousse claims to have been his close friend and seems genuinely upset to hear that he died a broken man in prison. The abbé explains that a rich fellow prisoner gave Dantès a diamond worth fifty thousand francs, and that Dantès asked for it to be sold and shared among his father, his fiancée Mercédès, and three friends: Caderousse, Danglars, and Fernand. Asked how old Dantès died, Caderousse says the doctors called it a stomach illness, but the truth is he starved. The abbé leaps up, badly shaken.","La Carconte, listening from the stairs, warns her husband to keep quiet, because Fernand and Danglars are now rich and powerful. Caderousse hints that the two were traitors, not friends. When the abbé calmly says he will simply find them and give them their shares, greed wins: Caderousse bolts the door and sits down to tell the whole story, while the priest places himself in the shadows to listen."],"characters":["gaspard-caderousse","carconte","edmond-dantes","louis-dantes","mercedes","fernand-mondego","danglars","pierre-morrel"]},{"number":27,"title":"The Story","volume":1,"summary":"Caderousse tells the abbé everything: old Dantès’s slow death, the denunciation written by Danglars and posted by Fernand, Morrel’s near ruin, and how Danglars became a rich baron and Fernand the Count de Morcerf, husband of Mercédès. The abbé gives him the diamond.","details":["Once promised secrecy, Caderousse describes old Dantès’s last months: pacing all night, refusing to leave home in case Edmond came back, quietly selling his belongings, and finally, using a doctor’s diet as an excuse, refusing all food until he died nine days later, blessing his son. Mercédès and M. Morrel tried to help; Morrel left a red silk purse that paid the old man’s debts and funeral. Then Caderousse names the culprits: Danglars, driven by ambition, wrote the denunciation with his left hand at La Réserve, and Fernand, driven by love, posted it. Caderousse admits he was there, drunk, and afterwards kept silent out of fear.","Morrel, who pleaded for Dantès again and again, is now nearly ruined, with every hope riding on the Pharaon. Danglars made a fortune supplying the army in Spain, married well, and is now Baron Danglars, a Paris millionaire. Fernand deserted to the enemy with a general in 1815, rose in the army, won the title Count de Morcerf in the Spanish war, served Ali Pasha in Greece, and is now a lieutenant-general with a grand house in Paris.","Mercédès, alone after Edmond vanished and his father died, eventually married Fernand in the very church where she was to have married Edmond. She educated herself, has a son, Albert, and is now a great lady, yet Caderousse believes she is unhappy; she once secretly dropped him a purse. Villefort married Mademoiselle de Saint-Méran and left Marseilles. The abbé gives Caderousse the diamond in exchange for Morrel’s red purse and rides away. La Carconte suspects the stone is fake, so Caderousse rushes off to have it valued at the Beaucaire fair."],"characters":["gaspard-caderousse","carconte","edmond-dantes","louis-dantes","mercedes","fernand-mondego","danglars","pierre-morrel","villefort","renee-de-saint-meran","albert-de-morcerf","abbe-faria","ali-tebelin"]},{"number":28,"title":"The Prison Register","volume":2,"summary":"Posing as an English clerk of the Roman bank Thomson & French, Dantès buys M. de Boville’s endangered loan to Morrel and, in return, examines the prison records, secretly pocketing Danglars’s denunciation and discovering Villefort’s role in his imprisonment.","details":["The day after the visit to Caderousse, a man of about thirty-two with an English manner and accent calls on the mayor of Marseilles. He says he is chief clerk of Thomson & French, a banking house in Rome, and is worried about money lent to Morrel & Son. The mayor, himself a creditor, praises Morrel’s honesty and sends him to M. de Boville, the inspector of prisons, who has two hundred thousand francs, his daughter’s dowry, invested with Morrel. Boville is in despair: Morrel has just told him he cannot pay unless the Pharaon arrives by the 15th.","The Englishman offers to buy the debt at its full value, in cash. The only “commission” he asks is a look at the prison registers: he says he was taught in Rome by an abbé named Faria and wants to know how he died. Boville happily tells the story, including how a dangerous Bonapartist named Edmond Dantès dug a tunnel to Faria’s cell, took the dead man’s place in his burial sack, and was thrown into the sea with a cannonball tied to his feet. Boville roars with laughter, and the Englishman laughs too. Officially, Dantès is dead.","Left alone with the files, the Englishman reads Dantès’s dossier: the accusation, the record of his examination (which never mentions Noirtier), and Morrel’s petition of April 1815, exaggerated on Villefort’s advice while Napoleon ruled and later turned into a weapon against Dantès. A note in Villefort’s handwriting orders strict solitary confinement for an “inveterate Bonapartist.” Unseen, the Englishman slips Danglars’s original denunciation into his pocket, then pays Boville and receives the transfer of the debt."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","m-de-boville","pierre-morrel","abbe-faria","villefort","danglars","noirtier"]},{"number":29,"title":"The House of Morrel & Son","volume":2,"summary":"The Thomson & French clerk visits the desperate Morrel, who owes 287,500 francs. News comes that the Pharaon has sunk; the stranger grants three months’ delay and tells Julie Morrel to obey a future letter signed “Sinbad the Sailor.”","details":["Morrel’s once-bustling firm is almost deserted. Only two employees remain: Emmanuel, a young clerk in love with Morrel’s daughter, and the one-eyed cashier Cocles, whose faith that the firm will always pay is as fixed as his arithmetic. Morrel, now fifty and white-haired, has secretly sold his wife’s and daughter’s jewels to meet last month’s bills. Everything depends on the Pharaon, long overdue from Calcutta.","The Englishman is shown up, passing Morrel’s daughter Julie on the stairs. He reveals that his firm has bought up Morrel’s bills, Boville’s included, worth 287,500 francs, and asks frankly whether Morrel can pay. Morrel admits that if the Pharaon is lost he will have to stop payment. At that moment Julie bursts in weeping: the Pharaon has sunk, though the crew was rescued by another ship. The old sailor Penelon tells of the storm, of Captain Gaumard’s courage, and of three days adrift without food; the Englishman, clearly an expert seaman, comments on the handling of the ship.","Morrel pays the sailors what he can, and they generously offer to wait for the rest. Alone with Morrel, the Englishman, moved to tears, grants three months’ grace, renewing the bills until 5 September at eleven o’clock. Morrel thanks him, privately vowing to pay or die. On the stairs the stranger tells Julie that one day she will receive a letter signed “Sinbad the Sailor,” which she must obey exactly, however strange, and hints that she will marry Emmanuel. Then he asks Penelon for a word in private."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","pierre-morrel","cocles","emmanuel-herbaut","julie-morrel","madame-morrel","penelon","captain-gaumard","m-de-boville"]},{"number":30,"title":"The Fifth of September","volume":2,"summary":"Facing bankruptcy, Morrel prepares to shoot himself at eleven. At the last moment Julie brings a red purse holding his paid bill and a diamond for her dowry, and a new Pharaon sails in. The unknown benefactor departs, turning now to vengeance.","details":["The delay lets Morrel scrape through June, July, and August, paying bills against everyone’s expectations. Captain Gaumard returns, and Morrel notices Penelon, newly dressed and oddly embarrassed, and assumes he has found a new master. Morrel’s last hope, a trip to Paris to ask Danglars, who owes his start in life to Morrel, ends in a humiliating refusal. Julie writes to her brother Maximilian, a principled young army officer nicknamed “the stoic,” to come home, and Madame Morrel sees her husband writing what looks like his will.","On the morning of 5 September, Maximilian arrives and discovers pistols under his father’s coat. Morrel shows him the books: 287,500 francs owed, about 15,000 in hand. Dying, Morrel says, he leaves an honest name; living, he would only be a bankrupt. Maximilian accepts, takes off his epaulets, and promises to live for his mother and sister and to repay every debt, starting with Thomson & French. Morrel waits alone as the clock nears eleven, the pistol in his mouth.","Meanwhile a messenger with an Italian accent hands Julie a letter from “Sinbad the Sailor” telling her to fetch a red silk purse from a fifth-floor room at 15 Allées de Meilhan, Dantès’s father’s old home. She bursts in just in time: the purse, Morrel’s own long-lost one, holds the receipted bill and a huge diamond labelled “Julie’s Dowry.” Then Emmanuel shouts that the Pharaon is entering the harbor: an exact copy of the lost ship, cargo and all, with Gaumard and Penelon aboard. Hidden in the crowd, a man with a black beard blesses Morrel, calls for Jacopo, and boards his yacht, declaring that, having rewarded the good, he will now punish the wicked."],"characters":["pierre-morrel","julie-morrel","maximilien-morrel","madame-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","cocles","penelon","captain-gaumard","danglars","edmond-dantes","jacopo"]},{"number":31,"title":"Italy: Sinbad the Sailor","volume":2,"summary":"In 1838 Franz d’Épinay, on his way to meet Albert de Morcerf for the Roman Carnival, lands on Monte Cristo, where a mysterious host calling himself “Sinbad the Sailor” entertains him in a hidden underground palace and offers him hashish.","details":["Years have passed. Early in 1838 two young Parisian aristocrats, Viscount Albert de Morcerf and Baron Franz d’Épinay, plan to see the Carnival in Rome and book rooms with Signor Pastrini at the Hôtel de Londres. While Albert visits Naples, Franz tours Elba and, after poor hunting on Pianosa, accepts his boat captain Gaetano’s suggestion to hunt wild goats on the uninhabited island of Monte Cristo, in spite of grim stories about pirates who sink ships with their crews aboard.","At night they see a fire on the shore. Gaetano swims in to scout and reports Spanish smugglers with two Corsican bandits, men he treats as fellow sailors. Franz is made welcome, and the smugglers’ chief invites him to supper, on condition that he is blindfolded. The sailors say the chief calls himself Sinbad the Sailor, owns a fast Genoese yacht, and lives in a cave more splendid than any palace.","With the blindfold removed, Franz meets a strikingly handsome man of about forty in Tunisian dress, deathly pale, in rooms full of crimson brocade, marble statues, and treasures. Franz takes the name Aladdin for the evening. Ali, a mute Nubian whom Sinbad saved from the Bey of Tunis, serves a lavish supper. Sinbad speaks of vows still to be fulfilled, of dispensing his own secret justice, and of going to Paris one day; when Franz guesses at revenge, his host laughs it off. Then Sinbad offers him hashish, and Franz sinks into a wild, sensual dream in which the statues come to life."],"characters":["franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","signor-pastrini","gaetano","edmond-dantes","ali"]},{"number":32,"title":"The Waking","volume":2,"summary":"Franz wakes in a bare grotto to find that Sinbad has sailed away. Unable to find the secret entrance, he leaves the island and travels to Rome, where he joins Albert at Signor Pastrini’s crowded hotel on the eve of Carnival.","details":["Franz wakes on a bed of heather in an ordinary-looking grotto, his head clear and the dream of the statues gone. Gaetano passes on Sinbad’s apologies: urgent business has called him to Malaga. Through his telescope Franz sees his host standing on the deck of his yacht, waving farewell, and hears a small gun fired in salute.","Franz spends hours searching the cave walls with a torch and his hunting knife, but finds no trace of the hidden door, only smoke marks left by others who have tried. He notices the yacht heading for Corsica, not Malaga, and Gaetano explains that Sinbad is landing the two Corsican bandits: he is said to fear neither God nor devil and will sail far out of his way to help a hunted man. With nothing more to learn, Franz sails off, and the whole adventure begins to feel like a dream.","After finishing his business in Florence, Franz reaches Rome just before the Carnival, with the streets packed. Signor Pastrini welcomes him and Albert to their small rooms; the rest of the floor has been taken by a very rich gentleman thought to be Sicilian or Maltese. Pastrini warns that carriages and horses are impossible to find, but Albert, sure that a full purse solves everything, goes happily to bed."],"characters":["franz-depinay","gaetano","edmond-dantes","signor-pastrini","albert-de-morcerf"]},{"number":33,"title":"Roman Bandits","volume":2,"summary":"Signor Pastrini warns Franz and Albert about the bandit Luigi Vampa and tells his story: a gifted shepherd who loved Teresa, killed the bandit chief Cucumetto, and took over his band. Franz is startled to hear that Vampa once met a traveller called Sinbad the Sailor.","details":["Pastrini can find the friends a carriage only until Sunday, and windows overlooking the Corso are all taken. When Franz plans a moonlight drive outside the city walls to the Colosseum, Pastrini warns them about Luigi Vampa, a young bandit who kills prisoners whose ransom arrives late. Albert scoffs, but Franz wants to hear more; Pastrini knew Vampa as a child and was once captured and freed by him, receiving a fine watch as a gift.","Vampa was a shepherd boy on the Count of San-Felice’s farm, astonishingly quick: he taught himself to read and write, carved wood beautifully, and became a deadly shot. He grew up beside Teresa, a beautiful, vain orphan shepherdess, and they planned to marry. Pastrini also describes the brutal bandit chief Cucumetto, including the story of Carlini, a bandit who stabbed his beloved Rita to save her from the band. Vampa and Teresa once hid Cucumetto from soldiers and refused his gold, though Teresa’s eyes lit up at the sight of it.","At the count’s Carnival ball, Teresa dances with nobles alongside the count’s daughter Carmela, and Vampa burns with jealousy. That night the villa catches fire; an unknown peasant rescues Carmela, and next day Vampa presents Teresa with Carmela’s costume and jewels. While guiding a lost traveller, who gives his name as Sinbad the Sailor, Vampa hears Teresa scream: Cucumetto is carrying her off. Vampa shoots him dead, puts on his clothes, and leads Teresa to the bandits, who make him their captain. Franz, struck by the name Sinbad, decides they will drive to the Colosseum through the city streets."],"characters":["franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","signor-pastrini","luigi-vampa","teresa","cucumetto","carlini","rita","diavolaccio","carmela","count-of-san-felice","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":34,"title":"The Colosseum","volume":2,"summary":"At the Colosseum Franz overhears a cloaked man with Sinbad’s voice promise a bandit to save the condemned Peppino. At the opera he sees the same man with a beautiful Greek girl; next morning the friends call on their neighbour, the Count of Monte Cristo.","details":["In the moonlit Colosseum, Franz slips away from the guides and, hidden behind a pillar, watches a cloaked man meet a peasant from the Trastevere district, who drops in through a hole in the roof. Two men are to be executed in two days, the peasant reports: a murderer and Peppino, a shepherd whose only crime was supplying food to the peasant’s band. He plans to rescue Peppino by force; the cloaked man promises instead to buy a pardon, and will signal success by hanging the middle one of three windows he has rented on the Corso with white damask and a red cross. In return the peasant swears absolute obedience. Franz recognizes the voice as Sinbad’s.","Next evening at the Teatro Argentina, Albert, stung at having had no romantic adventures in Italy, gets Franz to introduce him to the Venetian Countess G——. In a box opposite, Franz sees a lovely woman in Greek dress with a motionless man in the shadows behind her. When the man stands, Franz recognizes Sinbad. The countess, shuddering at his pallor, calls him a vampire, “Lord Ruthven himself,” and makes Franz escort her home. Albert later reports that the man spoke modern Greek and was very elegantly dressed.","Back at the hotel, Pastrini announces that the Count of Monte Cristo, who has the rest of their floor, offers them seats in his carriage and at his windows in the Rospoli Palace, and the count’s servant brings his cards. Next morning the posted execution notice names Andrea Rondolo and Peppino, and Franz concludes that the peasant was Luigi Vampa himself. When the friends call on the count, Franz recognizes him as the man from the Colosseum, the theatre, and Monte Cristo."],"characters":["franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","luigi-vampa","peppino","countess-g","greek-girl","signor-pastrini","andrea-rondolo"]},{"number":35,"title":"La Mazzolata","volume":2,"summary":"The Count of Monte Cristo entertains Franz and Albert, sets out a chilling philosophy of revenge, and takes them to watch the executions. Peppino is pardoned at the scaffold; the murderer Andrea, raging, is clubbed and stabbed to death while the count looks on in triumph.","details":["The count greets the friends warmly, gives no sign of knowing Franz, and summons his steward Bertuccio, who looks exactly like the smuggler who led Franz into the cave on Monte Cristo, to confirm his window over the Piazza del Popolo. Reading Franz’s copy of the execution notice, the count remarks that he has heard Peppino may be pardoned.","When the talk turns to punishment, the count says he has seen the tortures of nearly every nation. With sudden hatred in his face, he argues that the guillotine is too quick a pain for someone who has caused years of suffering, such as destroying a man’s father, mother, or betrothed, and that a duel is no true revenge either; slow, deep torment should be repaid in kind, “an eye for an eye.” Albert simply enjoys the breakfast and the count’s cigars, but Franz notices that the count barely eats and keeps looking intently at Albert. The count says he will visit Paris soon, and Albert promises to return his hospitality there.","On the way, Franz sees the count’s three windows on the Corso: two hung with yellow damask and the middle one white with a red cross. The count has kept his promise. From their window they watch the procession of penitents, the executioner with his heavy mallet, and the two prisoners. As Peppino reaches the scaffold, a pardon is read out. Andrea, the murderer, goes berserk, screaming that Peppino must die with him, and the count laughs bitterly at this proof of human nature. Andrea is struck down with the mallet and his throat is cut. Franz half faints and Albert clings to the curtains, while the count stands upright “like the Avenging Angel.”"],"characters":["edmond-dantes","franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","bertuccio","peppino","andrea-rondolo","luigi-vampa"]},{"number":36,"title":"The Carnival at Rome","volume":2,"summary":"The Carnival begins. Albert flirts with a masked peasant woman who throws him violets and sends him a note arranging a meeting. The count lends the friends his carriage and box but remains an enigma to Franz. On the last night Albert vanishes with his mysterious beauty.","details":["As Franz recovers, the count is already changing into costume; Peppino, he says, slipped away in the crowd. The friends throw themselves into the Corso’s mad battle of confetti and flowers in the count’s carriage. The count leaves them at the Rospoli Palace, where Franz imagines that the figure in a blue domino (a hooded masquerade cloak) at the window is the Greek girl. Albert catches the eye of a woman in a carriage full of Roman peasants, who throws him a bunch of violets.","At the theatre Franz tells the Countess G—— that the count is a Tuscan count named after the island he bought, and she decides her vampire is just an eccentric millionaire. Over the next days the count lends the friends his carriage freely and impresses them with his knowledge of literature, painting, and chemistry. Albert’s flirtation grows: the woman lifts her mask and is charming, and Albert, sure she is a noblewoman, writes to her. Her elegant reply tells him to follow the peasant who snatches his candle at the church of San Giacomo on Tuesday evening. Franz, meanwhile, is received by Pope Gregory XVI.","To Franz the count is a puzzle out of Byron: he must know Franz recognizes him, yet he never mentions their earlier meetings. Franz admires him but does not want to be in Paris when he arrives there. On Tuesday, the last day, after the horse races, the Corso blazes with thousands of moccoletti, small candles that everyone tries to blow out. At seven, Albert, dressed as a harlequin with rose ribbons, has his candle snatched by a masked peasant woman and disappears with her toward the Via Macello. Then the bell sounds, every light goes out, and the Carnival is over."],"characters":["franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","signor-pastrini","countess-g","greek-girl","peppino"]},{"number":37,"title":"The Catacombs of Saint Sebastian","volume":2,"summary":"A ransom note reveals that Luigi Vampa has kidnapped Albert. Franz turns to the count, who, guided by Peppino, takes him to the bandits’ lair in the catacombs, where Vampa apologizes and frees Albert at once. Franz notices the count shudder as he shakes Albert’s hand.","details":["Albert does not come back. At the Duke of Bracciano’s ball Franz learns that a man has brought a letter from Albert. It asks for four thousand piastres; beneath it, a note signed Luigi Vampa says that if the money is not paid by six in the morning, Albert will die at seven. Franz is several hundred piastres short and goes to the count, who offers his gold freely. Franz suggests a better way: the count saved Peppino’s life, so surely Vampa will release Albert for him.","The count calls up the cloaked messenger, who turns out to be Peppino and falls to his knees in gratitude. He explains the trap: Vampa himself drove the peasants’ carriage, Teresa flirted with Albert with his consent, and the “peasant girl” at San Giacomo was Beppo, a fifteen-year-old boy, who led Albert into an ambush outside the walls. Ali, the mute servant from Monte Cristo, drives the count, Franz, and Peppino out along the Appian Way to the catacombs of Saint Sebastian.","Through dark tunnels they reach a burial chamber where Vampa sits reading among twenty bandits. When the guns are raised, the count calmly reproaches Vampa for breaking their agreement that the count’s friends would be respected. Vampa, mortified, threatens his own men and apologizes. Albert is found sound asleep, dreaming of a dance with the Countess G——, and accepts his release with perfect coolness, thanking the count and lighting his cigar from Vampa’s torch. Vampa tells Franz his favorite book is Caesar’s Commentaries. By two o’clock Albert is dancing at the ball, while Franz wonders why the count shuddered when he had to take Albert’s hand."],"characters":["franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","luigi-vampa","peppino","teresa","beppo","ali","signor-pastrini","duke-of-bracciano","countess-g"]},{"number":38,"title":"The Rendezvous","volume":2,"summary":"In Rome, the Count of Monte Cristo asks Albert to introduce him to Paris society, and they fix a precise appointment: 21 May, 10:30 a.m., at Albert's home. Franz, uneasy, tells Albert everything he knows about the Count, but Albert brushes his fears aside.","details":["The morning after his rescue from the bandits, Albert de Morcerf, with Franz d'Épinay, goes to thank the Count of Monte Cristo again and offers the help of his family, including his father, the Comte de Morcerf, a man of Spanish origin with influence in Paris and Madrid. The Count accepts at once: he has never seen Paris, he says, and needs someone to open the doors of fashionable society to him. Albert mentions that his father has called him home to arrange a marriage.","The Count, insisting on his punctuality, sets the meeting three months ahead: the 21st of May at half-past ten in the morning, at Albert's rooms, No. 27 Rue du Helder. Franz watches him closely, remembering the plans the Count hinted at in the grotto of Monte Cristo. When they shake hands on parting, the Count's hand feels as cold as a corpse's.","Alone with Albert, Franz breaks his silence and tells him everything: the island, the smugglers and Corsican bandits, the enchanted underground palace, the hashish, the conversation he overheard at the Colosseum between the Count and Vampa, and how the Count freed Albert. Albert explains it all away. Rich men own yachts and take titles from land they buy, Corsican bandits are just outlaws, and in any case the Count saved his life and 4,000 piastres without asking a single question. Franz gives up the argument, though he still calls the Count a most singular man.","The next day the friends part, Albert for Paris and Franz for Venice. Before leaving, Albert leaves a card for the Count with the address and date written on it."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","franz-depinay","edmond-dantes","fernand-mondego","luigi-vampa","peppino"]},{"number":39,"title":"The Guests","volume":2,"summary":"In Paris on the morning of 21 May, Albert prepares a breakfast for the Count in his elegant bachelor pavilion. His first guests arrive: Lucien Debray, a minister's private secretary, and Beauchamp, a newspaper editor.","details":["The scene moves to Paris. Albert lives in a pavilion in the courtyard of his parents' grand house, the home of the Count and Countess of Morcerf, a place clearly chosen by a loving mother who wanted to keep her son close while giving him his freedom. The narrator describes his rooms in detail: a private side door, a smoking room stocked with every kind of tobacco, and a studio crammed with musical instruments, paintings, fencing gear, armor, antiques and stuffed birds, the remains of a string of passing hobbies.","At a quarter to ten his valet Germain brings the post. Albert accepts a seat in Madame Danglars' opera box and asks for a liqueur set from his mother, telling her he will introduce someone to her that afternoon. He admits he does not fully trust the Count to keep the appointment, but means to be punctual himself.","Lucien Debray arrives first, a cool, fashionable young man who is private secretary to the Minister of the Interior. He has been up all night writing dispatches about Spanish affairs, and mentions that the banker M. Danglars somehow learned the news early and made a million on the stock exchange. He also says he dined the night before at M. de Villefort's. Beauchamp, a journalist on an opposition paper, comes next, and the two trade friendly political insults while Albert waits for his last two guests."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","fernand-mondego","mercedes","germain","hermine-danglars","lucien-debray","beauchamp","danglars","villefort","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":40,"title":"The Breakfast","volume":2,"summary":"Château-Renaud arrives with Captain Maximilian Morrel, who saved his life in Algeria. As the clock strikes half-past ten the Count of Monte Cristo appears, dazzles the company, reacts with visible emotion to Morrel, and reveals he already has a house in Paris.","details":["While they wait, the guests gossip about Albert's expected engagement to Eugénie Danglars, daughter of the newly made Baron Danglars. Château-Renaud, a nobleman of old family, arrives with Maximilian Morrel, a captain of Spahis (French cavalry in Algeria). Morrel saved Château-Renaud from Arab horsemen on the 5th of September, a date he honors each year as the anniversary of the day his father was miraculously saved. Albert then tells his own story of being kidnapped in Rome and set free by the Count of Monte Cristo. The others laugh at it, though Morrel remarks that Monte Cristo is a small island he has heard old sailors such as Penelon talk about.","On the stroke of half-past ten the Count enters without a sound. He greets everyone politely and coolly, but at Morrel's name he steps forward and colors, and his voice trembles as he praises Morrel's noble heart. At table he eats little, shows off an emerald box of opium-and-hashish pills, mentions gifts of emeralds to the Sultan and the Pope, explains how he once captured Vampa and let him go, and declares he protects no society that does not protect him.","The Count says he has a credit with Baron Danglars through several banks, among them Thomson & French of Rome. Morrel starts at that name: this firm once did his family a great service and has always denied it. Each guest suggests a district for the Count to live in, and Morrel offers rooms at his sister Julie's house, where she lives happily with her husband Emmanuel Herbaut. The Count declines. His mute Nubian servant Ali has already bought and furnished a house on the Champs-Élysées, his steward Bertuccio has taken boxes at the theatres, and he mentions a Greek slave. Morrel leaves his card, and the Count promises to visit."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","lucien-debray","chateau-renaud","maximilien-morrel","edmond-dantes","eugenie-danglars","danglars","franz-depinay","luigi-vampa","peppino","penelon","countess-g","pierre-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","ali","bertuccio","greek-girl","germain"]},{"number":41,"title":"The Presentation","volume":2,"summary":"Albert shows the Count his rooms and introduces him to his parents. The Count goes pale before a portrait of Albert's mother in Catalan dress; the Countess is Mercédès. Afterwards she questions Albert anxiously about the Count and warns him to be careful.","details":["Albert takes the Count around his rooms and finds that his guest knows the date and origin of every object and the painter of every picture. In the bedroom the Count stops dead before a portrait of a dark-eyed young woman in the dress of a Catalan fisherwoman, gazing at the sea. He turns pale and breathes hard, then calmly calls her a charming mistress. Albert explains that it is his mother. His father dislikes the portrait, and his mother rarely looks at it without crying.","In the main house the Count admires the Morcerf coat of arms with hidden irony and meets the Count of Morcerf, a general and peer of France who looks older than his years and whose worn face seems to the Count to tell a story of secret troubles. Morcerf explains that he left the army after the July Revolution of 1830 and went into politics. The Count flatters him lavishly. Then the Countess appears in the doorway, pale and motionless. The narrator names her: Mercédès. She thanks the Count for saving her son, and he, even paler than she is, bows deeply. He declines her invitation to stay and leaves in a splendid new carriage, noticing as he goes that the curtain of her window moves.","Albert finds his mother lying in a darkened room, smelling salts beside her, blaming the strong scent of the flowers. She asks whether Monte Cristo is a real name, how old the Count is (thirty-five, Albert thinks, which seems to shock her), and whether he is really what he appears to be. She urges Albert to be prudent, then asks how his father received the guest. Albert thinks she has fallen asleep and tiptoes out, concluding that if his mother noticed the Count, he must be remarkable."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","mercedes","fernand-mondego","bertuccio","franz-depinay","chateau-renaud"]},{"number":42,"title":"Monsieur Bertuccio","volume":2,"summary":"At his new house on the Champs-Élysées, the Count buys a country house at Auteuil without looking at it. His steward Bertuccio turns pale at the address, and the Count insists that Bertuccio come with him to see it that same evening.","details":["The Count arrives at the house Ali has chosen for him on the Champs-Élysées, where Ali and his steward Bertuccio are waiting. Bertuccio reports that the Count's new visiting cards have been engraved and the first one taken to Baron Danglars. A notary is waiting to complete the sale of a country house the Count wants to buy.","The Count claims not to know where the house is. When the notary says Auteuil, just outside Paris, Bertuccio goes pale and suggests several better places instead. The Count signs anyway, pays more than the asking price, and sends the delighted notary away.","Alone, the Count unlocks a private notebook he keeps on a key round his neck and checks the address in the deed against his notes: 'Auteuil, Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28; it is indeed the same.' He wonders aloud whether he can trust a confession 'extorted by religious or physical terror', and says that within an hour he will know everything. He then asks Bertuccio whether he knows the area around Paris. Bertuccio, trembling, says no, and is horrified to learn that he must come to Auteuil at once. The steward, who has never dared to disobey, follows his master into the carriage."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","ali","bertuccio","danglars"]},{"number":43,"title":"The House at Auteuil","volume":2,"summary":"At the Auteuil house, which once belonged to the Marquis de Saint-Méran, the terrified Bertuccio admits that he committed a killing in its garden as an act of revenge, and that his victim was M. de Villefort.","details":["Bertuccio crosses himself in the Corsican way and grows more frightened as they drive into Auteuil. The caretaker tells them the house's former owner was the Marquis de Saint-Méran, a loyal Bourbon supporter whose only daughter married M. de Villefort, once the king's attorney at Nîmes, and died twenty-one years ago. Bertuccio has to lean on the wall to stay upright.","The Count makes Bertuccio light the way through the house and down a private staircase into the garden. There Bertuccio freezes and begs his master to move away from a certain spot, the place where 'he fell'. The Count coldly threatens to send him to an asylum or dismiss him. He reminds Bertuccio that he came to him with a letter of recommendation from the Abbé Busoni, who heard his confession in prison at Nîmes, and he says he will hold the abbé responsible.","Desperate not to lose his position, Bertuccio admits that he committed a vendetta (a Corsican blood revenge) in this very garden, and that two steps from the spot is the grave in which the victim had just buried his child. He says the Count, standing in his cloak under the plane tree, reminds him of the man: M. de Villefort, the magistrate with the spotless reputation, who was, Bertuccio says, a villain. The Count sits down on a bench, humming an opera tune, and orders him to tell the whole story."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","bertuccio","marquis-de-saint-meran","renee-de-saint-meran","villefort"]},{"number":44,"title":"The Vendetta","volume":2,"summary":"Bertuccio tells his story: Villefort refused justice for his murdered brother, so he declared a vendetta, stabbed Villefort in the Auteuil garden, and saved the baby Villefort was burying. Years later, hiding at Caderousse's inn, he watched Caderousse sell the abbé's diamond to a jeweller.","details":["In 1815 Bertuccio's elder brother, a Corsican officer of Napoleon's army, was murdered in Nîmes during the royalist massacres. Bertuccio begged the king's attorney there, Villefort, to find the killers and to grant the widow, Assunta, a pension. Villefort dismissed the dead man as a soldier of 'the usurper' and threatened to throw Bertuccio out, so Bertuccio declared a vendetta against him. He followed Villefort to Versailles and discovered his secret visits to the Auteuil house, where a young, pregnant woman known only as 'the Baroness' was living.","One stormy September night Bertuccio hid in the garden and saw Villefort come out with a spade and bury a box. He stabbed him and dug up the box, thinking it held treasure. Inside was a newborn baby, nearly suffocated. Bertuccio revived it and left it at the foundling hospital in Paris, keeping half of its linen, which was marked with an H and an N under a baron's coronet. Assunta later reclaimed the boy and named him Benedetto. He grew up charming but wicked, stealing and lying, and once taunted Bertuccio that he was not his father.","In June 1829, while running from customs officers, Bertuccio hid in a shed beside Gaspard Caderousse's inn on the Beaucaire road. Through the wall he watched Caderousse and his wife, La Carconte, sell a diamond to a Paris jeweller named Joannes. It had been left to them, they said, by a sailor named Edmond Dantès and delivered by an Abbé Busoni. The jeweller paid 45,000 francs and set off into a storm, ignoring the couple's greedy whispers. Then a knock came: Joannes was back, asking to spend the night."],"characters":["bertuccio","edmond-dantes","israel-bertuccio","assunta","villefort","marquis-de-saint-meran","benedetto","gaspard-caderousse","carconte","joannes"]},{"number":45,"title":"The Rain of Blood","volume":2,"summary":"Bertuccio finishes his tale: the Caderousses murdered the jeweller, Caderousse fled, and Bertuccio was arrested for the crime until the Abbé Busoni came to his aid. Benedetto later tortured Assunta for money and vanished. That night Haydée arrives at the Count's Paris house.","details":["La Carconte fawned over the jeweller and put him to bed. Bertuccio dozed off and woke to a pistol shot, screams and a struggle overhead, and warm drops falling on his face through the floor. Caderousse came down covered in blood, took the diamond and the money, and fled. Bertuccio broke through to help and found La Carconte shot dead on the stairs and Joannes dying of knife wounds. Customs officers and gendarmes burst in, found Bertuccio soaked in blood, and arrested him.","In prison at Nîmes he begged the authorities to find the Abbé Busoni. After three months the abbé appeared, confirmed the diamond story, and heard Bertuccio's confession, including the Auteuil killing. Caderousse was later caught, confessed, and was sent to the galleys for life, and Bertuccio was freed. The abbé then recommended him to the Count. Back in Corsica, Bertuccio found that Benedetto and two companions had held Assunta over a fire to make her reveal where her money was hidden, then robbed the house and disappeared. She died of her burns. 'Those Villeforts are an accursed race,' Bertuccio says, and the Count murmurs agreement.","The Count tells him that his real crime was not returning the rescued baby to its mother, and that the wicked are not so easily gone: Benedetto may be alive. He sends Bertuccio away, studies the garden as a 'plan' laid out before him, and murmurs that the prosecutor may not be dead. Back on the Champs-Élysées, he moves through the house as if he has always known it, and at midnight welcomes Haydée, the beautiful young Greek woman from Italy, to her secret apartments."],"characters":["bertuccio","edmond-dantes","gaspard-caderousse","carconte","joannes","benedetto","assunta","villefort","ali","haydee"]},{"number":46,"title":"Unlimited Credit","volume":2,"summary":"Baron Danglars calls at the Count's house and is turned away. The Count has Danglars' prized horses bought out from under him, then visits the banker and crushes his doubts about an 'unlimited' credit, arranging to draw six million francs in the first year.","details":["Baron Danglars, a showy, sly-looking man past fifty with dyed hair, drives up and is told the Count is not receiving. From behind his blinds the Count studies him through a lorgnette and remarks on his 'decidedly bad countenance'. He orders Bertuccio to buy Danglars' fine horses at double the price, and reassures the anxious Ali kindly in Arabic. He tells his valet Baptistin that he must never gossip about his master, and adds that he keeps a fund for loyal servants. Ali, he says, is not a servant but a slave whose life is his. He also orders Bertuccio to find him an estate on the Normandy coast with a harbor for his corvette, and to keep his ships and relays of horses ready.","At five o'clock the Count drives to Danglars' house in the Chaussée d'Antin behind the banker's own horses. Danglars has just told his colleagues he suspects a hoax: Thomson & French of Rome have given this so-called count unlimited credit with him. In his gaudy white-and-gold drawing room he tries to patronize the Count, who mocks his titles. When Danglars grandly offers 'even a million', the Count pulls two treasury notes of 500,000 francs each from his card case and shows further letters of unlimited credit on Rothschild and Lafitte.","Danglars is overwhelmed. They settle on six million francs for the year, beginning with 500,000 the next morning. The Count says his fortune is an old family treasure only recently released to him, and adds, with a ghastly smile, that Danglars will soon know more about him. Danglars offers to present him to the Baroness, who is with Lucien Debray. The Count smiles, since he already knows the banker's domestic secrets."],"characters":["danglars","edmond-dantes","ali","bertuccio","baptistin","haydee","hermine-danglars","lucien-debray","albert-de-morcerf","franz-depinay"]},{"number":47,"title":"The Dappled Grays","volume":2,"summary":"Madame Danglars is furious that her husband sold her dappled grays, now pulling the Count's carriage. The Count returns them decorated with diamonds. Next day the horses bolt at Auteuil with Madame de Villefort and her son aboard, and Ali stops them.","details":["The Count is presented to Madame Danglars in her pink boudoir, where Debray is also present. She has heard all about the Count from Debray and Albert. Her maid then reports that her pair of dappled gray horses, promised for Madame de Villefort's drive the next day, have vanished from the stable. Danglars stammers excuses and whispers that he sold them at a huge profit, just as Debray spots the grays harnessed to the Count's carriage outside. Debray slips away before the quarrel breaks out, and the Count leaves pleased: 'The domestic peace of this family is henceforth in my hands.' Two hours later he sends the horses back with a large diamond fixed in each rosette of their harness.","At Auteuil the next day the Count asks Ali whether his lasso could stop two runaway horses, and stations him outside the house. At five o'clock the grays come racing by, out of control, carrying a young woman and a small boy. Ali lassoes one horse and grips the other by the nostrils. The Count carries the pair inside and revives the unconscious boy with a single drop of a blood-red elixir.","The lady is Héloïse de Villefort, wife of the king's attorney, and the boy is her spoiled son Edward, who goes straight for the Count's bottles of drugs and calls Ali 'too ugly' to thank. The Count warns that some of the liquids are dangerous even to smell, and notices her glance at the case. Ali drives them home behind the now sluggish horses. She writes to her friend Hermine Danglars, begging to meet the Count again, and all Paris talks of the adventure. That night M. de Villefort, in full dress, calls at the Count's house."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","danglars","hermine-danglars","lucien-debray","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","ali","eugenie-danglars","valentine-de-villefort","villefort","albert-de-morcerf","chateau-renaud","beauchamp"]},{"number":48,"title":"Ideology","volume":3,"summary":"Villefort, now a cold, powerful royal prosecutor in Paris, comes to thank the Count. They spar about justice: the Count calls himself an agent of Providence sent to reward and punish, and Villefort answers with the story of his father Noirtier, struck down by paralysis.","details":["The narrator describes M. de Villefort as he is now. He is a respected and much-feared king's attorney who has survived four revolutions, rarely pays visits, runs a stiff, correct household with his young wife and his daughter by his first marriage, and despises dreamers and theories. He has grown thin and yellow, wears gold spectacles and dresses all in black. He arrives suspecting the Count is an adventurer or an escaped criminal.","The Count meets his stiff thanks with a chilly reply and then a string of provocations. He has studied the laws of every nation and found the primitive law of retaliation closest to God's law. There are men, he says, whom God sends with a mission rather than a post, and he is one of them, a man of no country, stopped only by death. Asked his ambition, he tells a parable: Satan offered him the world, and he answered that he wished to be Providence, because nothing is finer than to reward and punish. He was made one of Providence's agents, and would make the same bargain again. He adds that he has been 'nearly mad' once.","Shaken, Villefort warns him that worse things than death exist, such as apoplexy (a stroke). He describes his father, M. Noirtier, once a fearless Jacobin and Bonapartist plotter, now paralyzed and speechless overnight and dependent on his granddaughter Valentine. Villefort calls this God's punishment of his father alone, while his children Valentine and Edward are his compensation. The Count smiles while groaning inwardly, promises to visit, and, once Villefort leaves, says: 'Enough of this poison, let me now seek the antidote.'"],"characters":["villefort","edmond-dantes","heloise-de-villefort","valentine-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","noirtier","renee-de-saint-meran","ali","bertuccio","haydee"]},{"number":49,"title":"Haydée","volume":3,"summary":"Before visiting the Morrels, the Count spends an hour with Haydée in her Eastern apartments. He tells her she is free in France and may leave him; she insists she will never leave him. He asks her to keep the secret of her birth.","details":["After Villefort's visit the Count looks forward to calling on old friends in the Rue Meslay: Maximilian, Julie and Emmanuel. To prepare himself, he first spends an hour with Haydée. She lives in rooms furnished in Eastern style, with Turkish carpets and silk divans, and has three French maids and a Greek one, all ordered to treat her like a queen. The narrator describes her: about nineteen or twenty, dressed in the costume of Epirus (a region of north-western Greece), smoking a narghile (water pipe), strikingly beautiful.","She greets him in Greek, asking why he asks permission to enter when he is her master. The Count tells her that in France she is free, free even to leave him, and that she may find someone she prefers. Haydée answers that she has loved no one but him and her father, and that her love for him is not a daughter's love: if he died, she would die too.","He gives her full freedom of the house, a carriage, and Ali and her maid Myrtho to go with her. He asks only one thing: that she keep the secret of her birth and never speak the names of her 'illustrious father' or 'ill-fated mother'. He then leaves for the Morrels', murmuring lines of Pindar about youth and love."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","haydee","ali","maximilien-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","villefort"]},{"number":50,"title":"The Morrel Family","volume":3,"summary":"The Count visits Maximilian, Julie and Emmanuel, whose happiness moves him deeply. They show him relics of their unknown benefactor, 'Sinbad the Sailor'. He suggests a 'Lord Wilmore', then leaves abruptly on hearing old Morrel died believing their rescuer was Edmond Dantès.","details":["The Morrels' house in the Rue Meslay is cheerful and comfortable. The one-eyed Cocles, now the doorkeeper, does not recognize the Count, and the old sailor Penelon is the gardener. Maximilian explains that his father died leaving 500,000 francs. Julie and her husband Emmanuel worked until they had earned 250,000 francs more, then closed the firm rather than risk the Morrel name, and now live happily on a modest income.","The Count is so moved by their contentment that he can barely speak. Julie says that God once sent them an angel. Under a glass cover they keep their dearest treasures: a silk purse, a letter signed 'Sinbad the Sailor', and the diamond given to Julie as her dowry. These are relics of the stranger who saved their father from suicide and the firm from ruin. They have never been able to thank him. Penelon once glimpsed the Englishman who visited in 1829, a clerk from Thomson & French, boarding a yacht at Trieste.","The Count, uneasy under Julie's gaze, describes the Englishman exactly, then claims he must have been a friend of his, an eccentric Lord Wilmore who did such kindnesses secretly and did not believe in gratitude. He says Wilmore has left for distant lands and will never return. Maximilian adds that their father always said 'It was no Englishman', and that his last words were 'Maximilian, it was Edmond Dantès!' The Count goes deathly pale, makes hurried excuses and leaves. Julie says his voice seemed familiar."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","maximilien-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","cocles","penelon","pierre-morrel","baptistin","chateau-renaud"]},{"number":51,"title":"Pyramus and Thisbe","volume":3,"summary":"Maximilian Morrel and Valentine de Villefort, secretly in love, meet at a boarded-up gate in her family's garden. Valentine, unhappily engaged to Franz d'Épinay, describes her lonely home life and odd reactions to Morrel's name. The Count's arrival interrupts them.","details":["Behind a grand house in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, a disused gate separates the garden from an abandoned vegetable plot. Here a young woman waits by the planks, and a tall young man in a workman's blouse slips in to meet her: Maximilian Morrel and Valentine de Villefort. (The chapter takes its title from Pyramus and Thisbe, lovers in an old legend who talked through a crack in a wall.) To meet her safely, Maximilian has rented the plot as a 'gardener' for 500 francs a year.","Valentine is afraid. She is engaged to Franz d'Épinay, and her father never changes his mind. At home she feels like an outcast: her father ignores her, and her stepmother hates her behind constant smiles, perhaps because Valentine has her own mother's fortune and will inherit from her grandparents, the Saint-Mérans, while Madame de Villefort's adored son Edward has nothing. Her only comfort is her paralyzed grandfather Noirtier, who can speak only with his eyes. Maximilian promises patience: Franz is not due back for a year.","Valentine asks whether their fathers ever quarrelled in Marseilles. When she read aloud the news of Maximilian's promotion in the Legion of Honor, her father started and sneered at the Bonapartist Morrels of 1815, and she thought M. Danglars, who was present, trembled too. Noirtier, however, signalled with his eyes that he was glad of Maximilian's honor. A maid then calls Valentine indoors: the Count of Monte Cristo has come to call. Maximilian wonders how the Count knows M. de Villefort."],"characters":["maximilien-morrel","valentine-de-villefort","franz-depinay","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","noirtier","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","renee-de-saint-meran","danglars","pierre-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":52,"title":"Toxicology","volume":3,"summary":"The Count calls on Madame de Villefort and reminds her that they once discussed poisons at Perugia. She eagerly draws him out on how poisons can kill without leaving traces, and he gives her a dangerous elixir, pleased that 'the seed sown' has found fertile soil.","details":["Villefort is out, so Madame de Villefort receives the Count with Edward, who torments a parrot and tears up an album while his mother barely scolds him. Valentine comes in, sad and recently in tears. The Count claims to remember them all from Perugia two years earlier, and Madame de Villefort finally recalls talking at length with a man in a long cloak she took for a doctor. They had discussed the famous aqua Tofana, a legendary Italian poison, and she had asked about Valentine's health. At six o'clock she sends Valentine off to give Noirtier his dinner and sends Edward after her, closing the door carefully.","Alone with the Count, she returns again and again to poison. The Count explains how Mithridates, an ancient king, made himself immune by taking small, increasing doses; how Eastern poisoners cover their tracks; and how a 'terrible chemist' he knew, the Abbé Adelmonte, poisoned a cabbage with strychnine so that, passed along a chain of animals, it left no trace except what looked like apoplexy (a stroke). She shows that she already knows a good deal of chemistry. When she raises conscience, he answers with cases of people who excused their crimes as acts of love, among them Lady Macbeth, who he says wanted a throne for her son.","She asks about the elixir that revived Edward. One drop restores life, he says, while five or six would kill and could not be tasted in a glass of wine. He offers her the recipe and leaves for the opera. She thinks he must be the Adelmonte he talks about. The Count, satisfied, reflects that 'this is a fruitful soil', and sends the prescription the next morning."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","valentine-de-villefort","villefort","noirtier","abbe-adelmonte","haydee"]},{"number":53,"title":"Robert le Diable","volume":3,"summary":"At the opera, all Paris stares at the Count and Haydée. In Madame Danglars' box the Count meets Eugénie and the Count of Morcerf, who served Ali Pasha at Yanina. Seeing Morcerf, Haydée nearly faints: this man, she says, sold her father to the Turks.","details":["At a performance of Meyerbeer's opera Robert le Diable, Albert and Château-Renaud chat loudly in the stalls. A mystery horse entered as 'Vampa' has won the Jockey Club cup, and the Countess G——, the lovely Venetian from Rome, has received the cup 'from Lord Ruthven', the vampire nickname she once gave the Count. Albert visits her and confirms that it was the Count. Madame Danglars arrives escorted by Debray (openly hinted to be her lover) with her daughter Eugénie: handsome, cold and strong-willed, gifted in music and languages, who studies with a poor young singer, Louise d'Armilly. Albert finds her beauty too severe for his taste.","The Count enters a box with Haydée, whose beauty and diamonds hold everyone's attention. In Madame Danglars' box the ladies quiz Albert about the Count. Albert then meets the Count in the corridor, who asks whether the Count of Morcerf ever comes to the opera. Later the Count visits the baroness's box, where Morcerf has now arrived. Eugénie coolly asks whether Haydée is his daughter. Madame Danglars mentions that Morcerf served Ali Tepelini, and Morcerf confirms he was inspector-general of the Pasha's troops at Yanina and owes his fortune to him.","The Count leans over the front of the box with his arm around Morcerf, just as Haydée looks across. She sees Morcerf's face next to her guardian's, cries out and falls back in her seat. The Count excuses her nerves and returns to her. Trembling, she calls Morcerf a wretch: 'he sold my father to the Turks, and the fortune he boasts of was the price of his treachery!' The Count says he heard something of this in Epirus and asks her to tell him the details some day. They leave as the fourth act begins."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","chateau-renaud","countess-g","edmond-dantes","haydee","hermine-danglars","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","lucien-debray","fernand-mondego","mercedes","danglars","ali","ali-tebelin","luigi-vampa","beauchamp","franz-depinay"]},{"number":54,"title":"A Flurry in Stocks","volume":3,"summary":"Albert confides that he dreads marrying Eugénie Danglars, a match his mother opposes. Debray squirms at a joke about tricking Madame Danglars on the stock market. The Count plans an Auteuil dinner without the Morcerfs and expects Major Cavalcanti and his son Andrea.","details":["Albert and Debray call on the Count. Albert explains that his father and Danglars made their fortunes in Spain, his father in the army and Danglars supplying it. He does not love Eugénie, and his mother, for reasons he cannot explain, strongly dislikes the match with the Danglars family. Albert faked an illness to delay a meeting, but the two-month postponement ends next week.","Debray works out how much Danglars made on Haitian bonds, and lets slip that it is really Madame Danglars who gambles on the stock exchange. Albert jokes that Debray, who receives government news before anyone else, should teach her a lesson by feeding her a false report so she loses money. Debray is visibly embarrassed. The Count notices it, says something quietly to Debray as he leaves, and Debray replies, 'Willingly, count; I accept.'","The Count plans a dinner at Auteuil on Saturday for the Danglars and Villefort families. He tactfully leaves out the Morcerfs so that the Countess will not see it as a marriage meeting, and Albert decides to take his mother to the seaside at Tréport. The Count declines Albert's invitation to dine with his mother: he is expecting Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, an Italian nobleman, and his son Andrea, a young man making his entry into Paris society with his father's millions, whom the Count has promised to look after. Albert jokingly suggests finding Andrea a rich wife such as Eugénie. He also mentions that Franz is to marry Valentine de Villefort, and that Franz's father, General d'Épinay, was assassinated in 1815 by Bonapartists. Finally the Count orders Bertuccio to redecorate the Auteuil house, but to leave the red damask bedroom and the garden exactly as they are."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","lucien-debray","edmond-dantes","hermine-danglars","danglars","eugenie-danglars","fernand-mondego","mercedes","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","beauchamp","baptistin","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","andrea-cavalcanti","franz-depinay","valentine-de-villefort","haydee","bertuccio"]},{"number":55,"title":"Major Cavalcanti","volume":3,"summary":"An elderly Italian calling himself Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti visits Monte Cristo, sent by the Abbé Busoni. The count coaches him through a ready-made story about a long-lost son, hands him the papers and money he needs, and tells him the son is waiting next door.","details":["At seven in the evening a man of about fifty-two, in an old-fashioned green coat and a cravat that looks like a noose, arrives at the count's house on the Champs-Élysées. Baptistin recognizes him from a description. He is Bartolomeo Cavalcanti, and he carries a letter from the Abbé Busoni describing him as a noble of Lucca with an income of half a million, whose one sorrow is a son stolen in childhood. A postscript gives him credit of 48,000 francs on the count.","The 'major' is comically unsure of his own story. He is not certain he was a major, is surprised to hear how rich he is, and only 'believes' his son is called Andrea. Monte Cristo smoothly supplies every detail: the boy's mother was the Marchesa Oliva Corsinari, dead these ten years, and the boy vanished at five. When the major admits he has forgotten his marriage certificate and his son's baptism record, the count produces both, saying the abbé thoughtfully sent them.","The count then advises him what to tell Parisians: the boy was simply away at school in the provinces, or, if necessary, a treacherous tutor bribed by the family's enemies carried him off. He hands over 8,000 francs on account, tells him to stop wearing his outdated costume, and informs him that his luggage, including a uniform and medals for grand occasions, is waiting at the Hôtel des Princes. Finally he announces that the son is already in the house, and goes to prepare him for the meeting."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","baptistin","andrea-cavalcanti"]},{"number":56,"title":"Andrea Cavalcanti","volume":3,"summary":"Monte Cristo meets 'Count Andrea Cavalcanti', who came with a letter from 'Sinbad the Sailor'. Left alone, Andrea and the major quickly admit they are both paid impostors, agree to play father and son, and are invited to dine at Auteuil.","details":["In the next room waits a tall, fair young man with a red beard. His letter of introduction is signed 'Sinbad the Sailor', who, the count explains, is really an eccentric rich Englishman called Lord Wilmore; Andrea admits he once met that Englishman. He recites a fluent tale of noble birth and kidnapping, but starts in terror when told his father is in Paris. The count hints that Wilmore found him in an 'unpleasant position' and that his youth was 'stormy', advises him not to tell his romantic story, and promises him 50,000 livres a year, paid through Danglars' bank, for as long as he stays in Paris.","Through a hidden peephole the count watches the reunion. The two men embrace like actors on a stage. Then Andrea examines the papers with an expert eye and asks, in Tuscan, whether forgers are no longer sent to the galleys (prison ships and labor camps) in Italy. He asks the major how much he is paid to be his father: 50,000 francs down, while Andrea gets 50,000 a year to be his son. They compare letters, the major's from the Abbé Busoni and Andrea's from Sinbad/Lord Wilmore, both ordering them to the count on 26 May. Neither understands the scheme, but they agree to play their parts.","The count returns, slips Andrea a packet of banknotes 'from his father', and invites both to dine on Saturday at his country house at Auteuil to meet Danglars. Watching them leave arm in arm, he calls them two scoundrels and says it is a pity they are not really related. Then, finding disgust worse than hatred, he goes to visit the Morrels."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","andrea-cavalcanti","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","danglars","baptistin"]},{"number":57,"title":"In the Lucern Patch","volume":3,"summary":"At their secret meeting place by the Villefort garden gate, Valentine and Maximilian discuss her dreaded engagement to Franz, her stepmother's interest in her fortune, and Maximilian's strange trust in Monte Cristo, whom Valentine distrusts.","details":["Maximilian waits at the gate beside the lucern (alfalfa) field. Valentine is late because Madame Danglars and Eugénie are visiting, and he compares the two girls as they stroll past. Alone at last, Valentine tells him Eugénie dislikes the idea of marrying Albert de Morcerf, would prefer an independent life, and almost wishes her father would lose his fortune so she could become an artist like her friend Louise d'Armilly. Maximilian brings news that Franz d'Épinay is returning to Paris, and Valentine has been summoned by Madame de Villefort about a matter concerning her fortune.","Valentine explains her stepmother's motives. A year ago Valentine proposed entering a convent, and Madame de Villefort quietly encouraged it, because Valentine is rich: she has about 50,000 livres a year from her mother and expects more from her grandparents the Saint-Mérans and from Noirtier. If she became a nun, it would all go to her father and then to little Edward. Only her paralysed grandfather's despairing look made her give up the plan.","Maximilian asks permission to confide in the Count of Monte Cristo, whom he has known barely ten days but feels oddly bound to. He believes the count arranged for him to meet the Villeforts at the coming Auteuil dinner, and even lost at cards on purpose so Maximilian could buy the horse he longed for, Médéah. Valentine says the count is too much her stepmother's friend and has never even looked at her, so Maximilian drops the idea. Before running back to the house she lets him kiss her hand through the gate."],"characters":["maximilien-morrel","valentine-de-villefort","eugenie-danglars","hermine-danglars","heloise-de-villefort","noirtier","edouard-de-villefort","edmond-dantes","franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","louise-darmilly","chateau-renaud","lucien-debray"]},{"number":58,"title":"M. Noirtier de Villefort","volume":3,"summary":"Villefort and his wife tell the paralysed Noirtier that Valentine will marry Franz d'Épinay. Furious, Noirtier lets Valentine know through his eye signals that he opposes the match, and demands a notary.","details":["Noirtier can neither move nor speak; only his eyes are alive. He closes them for 'yes', blinks repeatedly for 'no', raises them when he wants something, and shuts the right eye to call Valentine or the left to call Barrois, his devoted servant of twenty-five years. Only Valentine, Barrois and Villefort understand him, and Villefort rarely bothers to visit.","Sending Valentine and Barrois away, Villefort and Madame de Villefort announce that Valentine will marry within three months, and name the groom: Franz de Quesnel, Baron d'Épinay. Noirtier's eyes blaze, and he almost chokes with rage. Madame de Villefort mentions that Franz's father was assassinated in 1815. Villefort, who knows of an old political hatred between Noirtier and the elder d'Épinay, says pointedly that whoever was guilty of that murder should be glad to offer Valentine to the victim's son as a peace-offering. Noirtier answers with a look of contempt.","When Valentine comes in, she questions him patiently and learns he is upset about the marriage and does not like Franz. She confesses she does not love Franz either, and that her convent plan was really a way to escape the match. Noirtier signals that he can still help her. Reciting the alphabet and then running her finger down a dictionary, she discovers the word he wants: 'notary' (a lawyer who draws up wills and contracts). Villefort objects that the scene will be ridiculous, but Barrois marches off to fetch one."],"characters":["noirtier","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","valentine-de-villefort","barrois","franz-depinay","general-depinay","edouard-de-villefort"]},{"number":59,"title":"The Will","volume":3,"summary":"With Valentine as interpreter, Noirtier proves his mind is sound and dictates a will: if Valentine marries Franz d'Épinay, his 900,000 francs will go to the poor instead of to her or anyone else in the family.","details":["The notary at first refuses to act for a client who cannot speak. Valentine teaches him the yes/no signals, and through the alphabet and dictionary Noirtier spells out 'will'. The impressed notary agrees, and a second notary, already fetched by Barrois, joins him. Noirtier stops them at the exact amount of his fortune, 900,000 francs, and Barrois brings a casket holding that sum in bank scrip, proving the old man knows his affairs perfectly.","Madame de Villefort assumes the money will go to Valentine, who has nursed him for six years. Noirtier refuses, though he looks at Valentine with deep tenderness. He refuses even more fiercely to leave it to Edward, and also refuses his son. Then he fixes his eyes on Valentine's hand, and she understands: it is about her marriage. He disinherits her because of the engagement to Franz; without it, she would have been his heir.","Villefort declares that he alone decides his daughter's marriage, and she will marry Franz. Noirtier says he will leave everything to charity. The notary points out that the law protects a son's share, but Villefort says he will not contest the will, since a man in his position cannot go to court against the poor. The will is drawn up, witnessed, sealed and entrusted to the family notary, M. Deschamps. Valentine weeps, Villefort bites his lips, and Madame de Villefort cannot hide her joy."],"characters":["noirtier","valentine-de-villefort","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","barrois","edouard-de-villefort","franz-depinay","deschamps"]},{"number":60,"title":"The Telegraph","volume":3,"summary":"Monte Cristo visits the Villeforts just after the will is made. He draws out the story of Noirtier's feud with Franz's father, encourages Villefort to hurry the marriage, unsettles him by revealing the Auteuil house was Saint-Méran's, and leaves to visit a telegraph.","details":["Villefort, still smarting from losing 900,000 francs to an 'old man in his second childhood', vows that Valentine will marry Franz anyway. While Edward pours ink into the bird's water, Madame de Villefort suggests Valentine and Noirtier planned the whole thing together. Carried away, Villefort describes his father as a Jacobin (a radical revolutionary) who plotted against the Bourbon kings and never shrank from any means. The count recalls that General d'Épinay, a secret royalist, was assassinated after leaving a Bonapartist meeting, and Villefort looks at him almost with terror. Madame de Villefort explains the marriage was meant to end the old feud.","Madame de Villefort keeps returning to how unfair it is to Edward. The count advises settling the marriage the moment Franz returns, so it cannot be undone. Villefort gratefully agrees, while his wife changes color.","Monte Cristo reminds them of Saturday's dinner at his country house in Auteuil, Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28. Villefort is visibly shaken: it is the house he once sold for M. de Saint-Méran, and he would never live in it. The count teases that if Villefort fails to come, he will suspect the house has some gloomy legend, and Villefort hastily promises to attend. The count then says he is off to see a telegraph (in this era, a chain of towers passing signals by moving arms), choosing a lonely one on the Spanish line rather than an official station."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","noirtier","franz-depinay","general-depinay","valentine-de-villefort","marquis-de-saint-meran"]},{"number":61,"title":"How a Gardener May Get Rid of the Dormice that Eat His Peaches","volume":3,"summary":"Monte Cristo bribes a poor telegraph operator who loves gardening to send a false signal. The fake news that Don Carlos has returned to Spain makes Danglars dump his Spanish bonds, costing him about a million francs when the story is denied.","details":["At the tower of Montlhéry, the count finds a tiny, perfectly kept garden and its owner, a telegraph operator of fifty-five, fretting over stolen strawberries and the dormice that eat his fruit. He earns a thousand francs a year, repeats signals he does not understand, and likes it that way because it means he has no responsibility. For a moment the count fears he has found a man with no ambition, which would spoil his plans.","Up in the signal room, the count shows him banknotes: 15,000 francs, then 10,000 more, enough to buy a house with two acres of garden and live on the income. In return he must transmit three signals the count has drawn on a paper. Sweating and trembling, the man does it, while the next station frantically signals in confusion. The count swears he has wronged no one, and the man faints among his dried herbs.","Minutes later, Lucien Debray, the minister's secretary, rushes to Madame Danglars: her husband must sell his Spanish bonds, because Don Carlos (a rival claimant to the Spanish throne, held in France at Bourges) has escaped to Spain. Danglars sells at any price and loses 500,000 francs, and the newspaper Le Messager prints the story. Next morning the official Le Moniteur announces it was false, blamed on a signal misread in fog. The bonds rise, and with losses and missed gains Danglars is out about a million. The count tells Morrel he has just learned how a gardener may get rid of the dormice that eat his peaches."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","telegraph-operator","lucien-debray","hermine-danglars","danglars","maximilien-morrel"]},{"number":62,"title":"Ghosts","volume":3,"summary":"Guests arrive for Monte Cristo's dinner at Auteuil. Bertuccio, peeking in, is horrified: Madame Danglars is the pregnant woman from the garden in his story, Villefort is the man he thought he had killed, and 'Andrea Cavalcanti' is Benedetto.","details":["In a few days Bertuccio has transformed the gloomy Auteuil house: full-grown trees, a fresh lawn, a library, a conservatory, the count's dogs and birds. One room alone has been left untouched, and Bertuccio passes it in terror. The count arrives with Ali.","Morrel rides up first on Médéah, followed by the Danglars' carriage escorted by Debray and Château-Renaud. The count notices Madame Danglars slip Debray a note, and sees her cast an anxious glance over the house. She asks to buy Morrel's horse, and the count rescues him by inventing a wager. Danglars, pale after his losses, is surly and absent-mindedly strips the blossoms off an orange tree. The Cavalcanti are announced, the major in uniform covered with medals; Château-Renaud remarks that Andrea looks well dressed for the first time in his life. The count tells Danglars they are enormously rich and that the son wants a Parisian wife. Then M. and Madame de Villefort arrive, Villefort's hand trembling.","Bertuccio glances through the door and gasps. The fair lady in white with diamonds, Madame Danglars, is 'the woman of the garden' who was expecting a child, and the man she was waiting for is Villefort: 'Then I did not kill him?' The count mocks him: he must have struck badly, or dreamed it all. Then the count points out Andrea, and Bertuccio mutters, 'Benedetto? Fatality!' Benedetto is the newborn Villefort buried in this very garden and Bertuccio dug up alive, the boy his sister-in-law Assunta raised. Struggling for composure, Bertuccio announces dinner."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","bertuccio","ali","maximilien-morrel","hermine-danglars","danglars","lucien-debray","chateau-renaud","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","andrea-cavalcanti","benedetto","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","baptistin"]},{"number":63,"title":"The Dinner","volume":3,"summary":"After a lavish dinner, Monte Cristo shows his guests the old red damask bedroom and a secret staircase, then announces his workmen dug up a newborn's skeleton in a box under a garden tree. Madame Danglars faints, and Villefort secretly asks to meet her.","details":["Villefort sits beside Madame Danglars, and both are uneasy. The count serves an extravagant feast, including a sterlet from the Volga and a lamprey from Lake Fusaro, both brought alive in barrels, with live spares carried in to prove it. Château-Renaud remembers that the house belonged to M. de Saint-Méran and stood empty for years, looking like a place where a crime had been committed. Villefort gulps a glass of wine and says the house was part of Valentine's dowry, which makes Morrel turn pale.","The count leads everyone to a gloomy room hung with blood-red damask, whose faded portraits seem to say 'We have seen', and opens a hidden staircase. He imagines someone creeping down it one stormy night with a burden to hide. Madame Danglars nearly faints on Villefort's arm. When the count switches to picturing an honest mother's room, with a father carrying a sleeping child down the stairs, she faints outright. Madame de Villefort hands the count her smelling bottle, filled with the red liquid he once used on Edward, and mentions she has 'made the trial' he advised.","In the garden the count declares a crime was committed here. Stamping on the ground beneath a tree, he says his workman found the ironwork of a box containing the skeleton of a newborn infant. Villefort weakly asks who said the child was buried alive. The major innocently asks what happens to child-murderers in France; Danglars says their heads are cut off, and Villefort confirms it in a voice 'scarcely human'. The count stops there and serves coffee, but Villefort whispers to Madame Danglars that he must see her tomorrow in his office."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","villefort","hermine-danglars","danglars","heloise-de-villefort","maximilien-morrel","lucien-debray","chateau-renaud","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","andrea-cavalcanti","baptistin","marquis-de-saint-meran","valentine-de-villefort"]},{"number":64,"title":"The Beggar","volume":3,"summary":"Danglars, dazzled by Major Cavalcanti, drives him home. As Andrea leaves, a ragged man calls him 'Benedetto': it is Caderousse, a runaway from the Toulon prison, who demands a monthly allowance and rides back to Paris in Andrea's carriage.","details":["The party breaks up. Danglars, convinced the Cavalcanti are fabulously rich nobles, is charmed by the major's diamond ring and his refusal to be impressed by anything, and offers him a seat in his carriage. They agree to meet on business the next day. Andrea, who said little at dinner and was alarmed to find the king's prosecutor among the guests, goes to his tilbury (a light two-wheeled carriage).","A huge, bony hand falls on his shoulder. A sunburnt, bearded man in rags, with a red handkerchief on his head and teeth like a wolf's, asks for a ride back to Paris and calls him 'Master Benedetto'. Andrea sends his groom away and drives off with him. Once alone, he calls the man Caderousse. They parted at the Pont du Var, where Andrea claimed he was heading for Italy; Caderousse reminds him how he used to share his soup with him and call him his child. Andrea says he was brought up in Corsica.","Caderousse asks for 150 francs a month; Andrea gives him 200 and promises the same every month. Andrea explains he has 'found his father', Major Cavalcanti, through the Count of Monte Cristo, and warns Caderousse to keep away from the count. Caderousse refuses to get down: with no papers he would be arrested at the city barrier and sent back to Toulon as convict No. 106. Andrea's hand strays to a pistol, Caderousse quietly opens a Spanish knife, and both think better of it. Wearing the groom's coat and Andrea's hat, Caderousse passes the barrier as a servant, then vanishes with both. Andrea sighs that one cannot be completely happy in this world."],"characters":["andrea-cavalcanti","gaspard-caderousse","danglars","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","edmond-dantes","villefort","hermine-danglars","heloise-de-villefort"]},{"number":65,"title":"A Conjugal Scene","volume":3,"summary":"Debray escorts the shaken Madame Danglars home, but Danglars sends him away and coldly confronts his wife: she owes him a share of his 700,000-franc Spanish loss, and he has long known about her lovers, including Villefort.","details":["Debray rides to the Danglars house and, alone with the baroness, calls her Hermine and asks what upset her at the count's; she will not say. Her maid Cornélie reports that Eugénie is in bed while Louise d'Armilly plays the piano, and the baroness jokes that Eugénie will soon ask Debray for a job at the Opera. Then Danglars walks in and, with icy politeness, makes Debray leave.","Danglars flings his wife's little dog across the room and gets to the point: he has lost 700,000 francs on the Spanish loan. He lists the tips she gave him this year, about Haitian bonds, a railway grant, and Spanish affairs overheard at the minister's, each of which earned him a fortune, and from which she received a quarter, 500,000 livres in all. The latest tip, from her talk with Debray, was false. He demands a quarter of the loss, 175,000 francs, and suggests the money has gone to Debray. He says they ceased to be husband and wife four years ago, and that he has tolerated her 'music lessons' and her 'study of diplomacy with the minister's secretary' as long as she paid for them herself. He even suspects the minister and Debray of faking the telegraph to ruin him; she points out that the operator has fled.","Danglars boasts that for sixteen years he has seen every step she took, and that all her friends, 'from M. de Villefort to M. Debray', have trembled before him. At Villefort's name she turns white. He says her first husband, M. de Nargonne, died of grief or rage when he came home after nine months away to find her six months pregnant. Debray must pay his share or disappear. Danglars leaves her half-fainting, thinking of Villefort and the dinner."],"characters":["lucien-debray","hermine-danglars","danglars","cornelie","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","villefort","baron-de-nargonne"]},{"number":66,"title":"Matrimonial Projects","volume":3,"summary":"Danglars, reeling from new losses, visits Monte Cristo, who calls his wealth 'third-rate'. Danglars admits he now prefers rich Andrea Cavalcanti to Albert for Eugénie, sneers that Morcerf was once the fisherman Fernand Mondego, and on the count's hint writes to Yanina.","details":["The next day Debray does not call and Madame Danglars goes out alone. Major Cavalcanti visits Danglars to cash his draft. Danglars then drives to the Chamber of Deputies and on to Monte Cristo's, where a man dressed as an abbé walks past him into the inner rooms; the count explains it was his friend the Abbé Busoni, just arrived in Paris.","Danglars is gloomy: a Trieste banker, Jacopo Manfredi, has gone bankrupt owing him a million, on top of 700,000 francs lost in Spain, which he blames on his wife's 'dreams'. The count sorts fortunes into three classes and politely places Danglars' in the third, built on credit and easily shaken; six or seven months like this would ruin him. Danglars blusters. Talk turns to the Cavalcanti: the major has drawn 40,000 francs and opened an account of 5,000 a month for his son. The count calls the major a miser, guesses he came to find Andrea a wife, and remarks that such fathers may give millions if the son marries to their liking, perhaps a banker's daughter.","Danglars admits he would like Andrea for Eugénie, saying Albert would hardly care. He sneers that Madame de Morcerf's trip to the seaside will do her good, as it is the air she breathed in her youth, and that while he really is named Danglars, Morcerf is not: when Danglars was a clerk, Morcerf was a fisherman called Fernand Mondego. He hints at rumors from Greece. The count says he has heard the name Fernand Mondego in connection with Ali Pasha of Yanina (Ali Tepelini), and suggests Danglars ask his correspondent at Yanina what part a Frenchman of that name played in Ali's downfall. Danglars rushes off to write the letter."],"characters":["danglars","edmond-dantes","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","andrea-cavalcanti","hermine-danglars","lucien-debray","eugenie-danglars","albert-de-morcerf","fernand-mondego","mercedes","ali-tebelin","jacopo-manfredi"]},{"number":67,"title":"The Office of the King’s Attorney","volume":3,"summary":"Madame Danglars meets Villefort secretly in his office. He confesses he buried their newborn at Auteuil believing it dead, but a year later the box was gone. The child may have lived, and Monte Cristo seems to know their secret.","details":["Veiled and changing cabs, Madame Danglars slips into the Palais de Justice and is led by a private passage to Villefort's office. He bolts the door and says bitterly that his place should be on the prisoner's bench, not the judge's seat. She speaks of the dinner, of the 'poor innocent offspring of mine, which I never even kissed', and of the bones the count found. Villefort tells her the truth is worse: nothing was found, because nothing was there.","He tells the story. She gave birth in the red damask room, and the baby seemed lifeless. He put it in a chest, buried it in the garden, and was stabbed by a Corsican who had sworn a vendetta (blood feud) against him. She helped him back to the house, a duel was given as the excuse for his wound, and while he spent months recovering, she, the widow of M. de Nargonne, married Danglars. A year later he cancelled the lease, returned at night and dug everywhere, but the chest had vanished. A corpse would have been taken to a magistrate, so he concluded the child might have been alive, and the Corsican might have saved it.","At the foundling hospital he learned that on that same night, 20 September, a baby had been left wrapped in half of a fine napkin marked with half a baron's crown and the letter H, the mark on all her linen. Six months later a woman claimed the child with the other half of the napkin. His agents traced her to Châlons, then lost her. Since Monte Cristo spoke of finding remains that could not have been there, Villefort is sure the count knows their secret. He asks whether she ever told anyone, kept a diary, or talks in her sleep, and vows to find out within a week who this Count of Monte Cristo really is."],"characters":["hermine-danglars","villefort","edmond-dantes","baron-de-nargonne","bertuccio","andrea-cavalcanti","assunta","danglars"]},{"number":68,"title":"A Summer Ball","volume":3,"summary":"Albert returns from the seaside and invites Monte Cristo to his parents' summer ball, saying his mother especially wants him there. The count hints that Danglars also wants out of the engagement, then sends Bertuccio to Normandy after learning Madame Danglars visited the Palais.","details":["Madame de Morcerf and Albert return from Tréport, and Albert hurries to see the count, who greets him with his usual cool reserve. Albert hears about the Auteuil dinner; the count stresses that young Cavalcanti only 'calls himself' a count. Albert declares that Eugénie would make a charming mistress but an awful wife, and that what he really wants is a wife like his mother, at which the count turns pale. Albert has even tried, by letter, to make Franz fall for Eugénie. Franz is returning soon, summoned by Villefort to marry Valentine.","The count tells Albert to relax: Danglars would pay double to break the engagement himself, since he is taken with someone else. Albert, a little stung, invites the count to his father's summer ball on Saturday and asks him to bring the Cavalcanti. The count refuses to present Andrea, saying he hardly knows him and was only recommended by 'a good abbé, who may be deceived'. He hesitates to come at all until Albert says his mother begs him to; she has talked of nothing else for four days. The Countess G— thinks he is Lord Ruthven (a famous fictional vampire), while Madame de Morcerf takes him for a Cagliostro, a mysterious magician. He accepts, and asks Albert to bring Franz to see him.","As Albert drives away, Bertuccio reports that 'she' went to the Palais and stayed an hour and a half before going straight home. The count tells him to go and find the little estate in Normandy they had discussed, and Bertuccio leaves that evening."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","mercedes","eugenie-danglars","danglars","andrea-cavalcanti","franz-depinay","villefort","valentine-de-villefort","fernand-mondego","countess-g","bertuccio","hermine-danglars"]},{"number":69,"title":"The Inquiry","volume":3,"summary":"Villefort has the police look into Monte Cristo. The Abbé Busoni and Lord Wilmore give a supposed police agent harmless, contradictory accounts of 'M. Zaccone'. Lord Wilmore then turns out to be the count in disguise, and the 'agent' was Villefort himself.","details":["Villefort writes to M. de Boville, the former prison inspector now high in the police. The reply says the count is an intimate friend of Lord Wilmore, a rich Englishman, and known to the Abbé Busoni, a Sicilian priest famed for his good works in the East. Further reports describe the abbé's austere little house near Saint-Sulpice and Wilmore's rented lodgings; Wilmore, oddly, never speaks a word of French.","A visitor claiming to come from the prefect of police calls on the abbé at night. Wearing a monk's cowl and huge spectacles, the abbé tilts his lamp so the light falls on his visitor's face while his own stays in shadow. He says the count's real name is Zaccone, son of a rich Maltese shipbuilder and his childhood playmate; he bought his title, has a modest income, served in the navy, may be a Lutheran, and was decorated by the pope for helping Christians in the East. His only enemy is Lord Wilmore. The count had never been to France before; six months ago he asked the abbé for advice, and the abbé recommended Bartolomeo Cavalcanti. The Auteuil house is to become a lunatic asylum. The abbé politely refuses the visitor's donation for the poor.","Lord Wilmore, red-whiskered and dressed in the fashion of 1811, speaks with more spite. He says Zaccone fought for an Indian prince against the English, was captured and escaped from the prison hulks by swimming, then fought in Greece and found a silver mine in Thessaly. He is in France for railways and a new telegraph system, and is digging up Auteuil looking for a mineral spring. Wilmore claims they have fought three duels over a seduced wife, and shows a fresh scar. When the visitor leaves, Wilmore pulls off his wig, whiskers, false jaw and scar, and becomes the Count of Monte Cristo. The 'agent' who returns home is M. de Villefort, who sleeps soundly for the first time since the dinner."],"characters":["villefort","m-de-boville","edmond-dantes","bartolomeo-cavalcanti"]},{"number":70,"title":"The Ball","volume":3,"summary":"At the Morcerfs' ball, gossip spreads the police's version of Monte Cristo's past. Mercédès notices that the count refuses all food and drink under her husband's roof, and pointedly takes his arm to lead the guests into the garden.","details":["On a hot July night the Morcerf garden glows with lanterns. Villefort has told Madame Danglars she must be seen at the ball, so she comes, dazzling. Eugénie and Valentine arrive arm in arm in white. Albert jokes that Madame Danglars is the seventeenth person to ask whether the count is coming; she reports that at the Opera he tossed a ring to the dancer Elssler. Madame de Villefort confides the 'secret' the police have just discovered: the count's family name is Zaccone, he is Maltese, served in India, owns a mine in Thessaly, and plans a water-cure at Auteuil. When Albert introduces Morrel, Madame de Villefort is cold to him, but across the room Valentine raises her bouquet of forget-me-nots to her lips.","Every eye turns as the count enters. He and Madame de Morcerf exchange only a silent bow. Albert amuses him with sharp portraits of pompous academicians and peers. Danglars joins them, and the count mentions that a Frankfurt bank has just failed; Danglars, pale, has already paid out 200,000 francs on their drafts and begs him not to mention it in front of young Cavalcanti.","Mercédès watches the count refuse every ice and drink, and asks Albert to press him; he still refuses. She tells her son the count has never been willing to eat under M. de Morcerf's roof, and concludes his abstinence is deliberate. Hearing he finds the room stifling, she has the blinds thrown open, then invites her husband's guests into the garden and asks the count for his arm. He almost staggers, and the look they exchange seems to her to last a century."],"characters":["mercedes","albert-de-morcerf","hermine-danglars","villefort","eugenie-danglars","valentine-de-villefort","heloise-de-villefort","maximilien-morrel","edmond-dantes","fernand-mondego","danglars","andrea-cavalcanti","haydee","franz-depinay"]},{"number":71,"title":"Bread and Salt","volume":3,"summary":"Alone in the conservatory, Mercédès keeps offering Monte Cristo fruit, which he refuses, and questions him about his past. He speaks of a girl he once loved who married another. Then news arrives that the Marquis de Saint-Méran has died.","details":["Mercédès leads the count to the greenhouse and offers him Muscatel grapes; he says he never eats them. She offers a peach; he refuses again. She reminds him of the Arab custom by which those who share bread and salt under one roof become friends forever. He replies that in France eternal friendships are as rare as that custom. When she asks, gripping his arm, whether they are friends, he goes deathly pale, then flushes, and answers, 'Certainly, we are friends; why should we not be?'","She asks if he has suffered; he has, deeply. Is he married? No: the young woman seen with him at the Opera is a slave he bought in Constantinople, a prince's daughter he has adopted, having no one else to love. He says that 'at Malta' he loved a young girl and was about to marry her when war carried him away; when he came back, she was married. He has forgiven her, and claims he does not hate those who separated them. When he refuses the grapes again, she flings them into the bushes: 'Inflexible man!'","Albert runs in with bad news. Villefort has come to fetch his wife and daughter: Madame de Saint-Méran has just reached Paris with word that the marquis died on the road from Marseilles, while coming to hurry on Valentine's marriage to Franz, and Valentine fainted. Albert makes a careless joke, and his mother joins his hand with the count's, asking again if they are friends. The count replies only that he is her most respectful servant, and she leaves in tears. Morrel leaves the ball soon after Valentine."],"characters":["mercedes","edmond-dantes","haydee","albert-de-morcerf","villefort","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","valentine-de-villefort","franz-depinay","maximilien-morrel","heloise-de-villefort"]},{"number":72,"title":"Madame de Saint-Méran","volume":3,"summary":"The grieving Marquise de Saint-Méran arrives with news of her husband's sudden death. Feverish, she insists Valentine marry Franz at once, describes a white figure touching her glass in the night, and sends for a notary. Dr. d'Avrigny is called.","details":["Alone in his study, Villefort goes over his secret list of enemies and decides none of them would have waited so long to strike. Perhaps, he thinks, the Corsican told the story to some priest, but what interest could Monte Cristo have in it? His mother-in-law bursts in: M. de Saint-Méran is dead. Unwell but determined to see Valentine, he took his usual lozenges on the road from Marseilles, fell into an unnaturally deep sleep, cried out and died. A doctor called it apoplexy (a stroke). She has had his body sent on to the family vault.","Villefort fetches his family from the ball. Valentine sits with her grandmother, who keeps a bottle of orangeade, her usual drink, by her bed, and then visits Noirtier, who comforts her with his eyes.","Next morning the marquise is feverish and insists on seeing Villefort. She wants the marriage to Franz, son of the royalist general killed before Napoleon's return, hurried on because she feels she has little time left; she reproaches Villefort for forgetting her daughter Renée. She says that in the night, with her eyes closed, she saw a white figure enter through the door from Madame de Villefort's dressing room and heard her glass being moved, and she believes it was her husband's spirit. She drinks a glass of orangeade and demands a notary. Valentine thinks of confessing her love for Maximilian, but knows her proud grandmother despises anyone not noble. While the notary is with the marquise, Valentine meets Dr. d'Avrigny, an old family friend, who finds the symptoms strange. She walks to the garden gate and hears Maximilian calling her."],"characters":["villefort","marquise-de-saint-meran","marquis-de-saint-meran","valentine-de-villefort","barrois","noirtier","heloise-de-villefort","franz-depinay","general-depinay","renee-de-saint-meran","dr-davrigny","maximilien-morrel"]},{"number":73,"title":"The Promise","volume":3,"summary":"Valentine promises to run away with Maximilian rather than sign her marriage contract, but on the chosen night her grandmother dies. Hiding in the garden, Maximilian overhears Dr. d'Avrigny tell Villefort she was poisoned. Noirtier then swears he will stop the marriage himself.","details":["Maximilian tells Valentine that Franz has just arrived in Paris. She refuses to defy her father and her dying grandmother, and turns down his offer to take her to his sister's and marry her. He calmly announces he will kill himself the day she marries, and she gives in: if they force her to sign, she will come to him and they will flee. She swears it by her mother. Her letter arrives: the contract will be signed at nine that evening, so he should meet her at the gate at a quarter to nine.","That night he waits with ladders and a carriage, but Valentine never comes and the house stays dark. He climbs the wall and hides as Villefort and Dr. d'Avrigny walk into the garden. Madame de Saint-Méran is dead. The doctor says her convulsions looked like tetanus, but they are also the symptoms of vegetable poison, and he is convinced she died of brucine or strychnine. Villefort admits that only Valentine, her heir, gains by her death. The doctor wonders whether Barrois could have given her by mistake some of the brucine prescribed for Noirtier. Villefort begs him to avoid a scandal. D'Avrigny agrees to keep silent, but warns him to watch carefully, since the evil may not stop here.","Maximilian slips into the house and finds Valentine praying beside the body; Franz arrived just as her grandmother was dying. Morrel says he heard the news from servants, keeping the doctor's words to himself. Valentine leads him to Noirtier and declares she loves him. Noirtier rejects both of Morrel's plans, running away or provoking Franz to a duel: help will come from Noirtier himself, and the contract will not be signed. Morrel swears to wait for his decision and goes home."],"characters":["maximilien-morrel","valentine-de-villefort","franz-depinay","albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","marquise-de-saint-meran","villefort","dr-davrigny","noirtier","barrois","heloise-de-villefort","deschamps"]},{"number":74,"title":"The Villefort Family Vault","volume":4,"summary":"The Marquis and Marquise de Saint-Méran are buried together at Père-Lachaise. Straight afterwards Villefort hurries Franz into signing the marriage contract, until Barrois announces that Noirtier demands to speak with Franz at once.","details":["Two days later a double funeral takes place, the marquis's coffin having arrived from the south, and both are laid in the Villefort vault at Père-Lachaise cemetery beside their daughter Renée. In a mourning coach, Beauchamp, Debray, Château-Renaud and Albert discuss the sudden deaths; Beauchamp doubts that grief could cause a stroke in so slight a woman. They note that Valentine, and so Franz, will inherit a fortune, doubled when 'the old Jacobin' Noirtier dies. No one suspects the doctor's secret. At the cemetery Albert introduces Morrel to Franz, and Morrel, bound by his oath, bows politely, though it pains him to hear that Franz has seen Valentine.","Villefort takes Franz home and, citing the marquise's dying wish, proposes signing the contract that very day. Valentine will spend her three months of mourning on her new estate of Saint-Méran, where a quiet civil wedding can be held in a few days. Franz agrees and goes to fetch his witnesses, Albert and Château-Renaud. Valentine is stunned, and Madame de Villefort sits pale in the shadows, clutching Edward.","The notary first tells Franz that Noirtier has disinherited Valentine because of this marriage. The will is legally void, since Noirtier gave away more than the law allows, but Villefort says he will never challenge it while he lives. Franz answers nobly that he seeks happiness, not money. Villefort assures him Noirtier's mind is so weak that he has forgotten Franz's name. At that moment Barrois appears: M. Noirtier wishes to speak immediately to M. Franz de Quesnel, Baron d'Épinay, and if refused, he will have himself carried into the drawing room. Over Villefort's objections, Franz insists on going, and follows the joyful Valentine downstairs."],"characters":["marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","renee-de-saint-meran","beauchamp","lucien-debray","chateau-renaud","albert-de-morcerf","franz-depinay","maximilien-morrel","villefort","valentine-de-villefort","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","barrois","noirtier","deschamps","dr-davrigny"]},{"number":75,"title":"A Signed Statement","volume":4,"summary":"Noirtier has Franz read a hidden document about his father's death in 1815: General d'Épinay was not murdered but killed in a fair duel after refusing to join a Bonapartist plot. Asked who killed him, Noirtier spells out the answer: himself.","details":["Villefort whispers to Valentine that she is forbidden to understand anything that might delay the marriage. Through his eyes, Noirtier guides her to a key, an old writing desk, and a secret compartment that Barrois opens. Inside is a packet tied with black string, addressed to a General Durand, and Noirtier wants Franz to read it.","It is a report of a meeting of a Bonapartist club in the Rue Saint-Jacques on 5 February 1815, the night Franz's father disappeared. General de Quesnel had been recommended by a letter from Elba as loyal to Napoleon, despite being made a baron by Louis XVIII, and was brought blindfolded to the meeting. The letter spoke of Napoleon's return and promised more news on the arrival of the Pharaon, Morrel's ship. The general refused to join, declaring himself a royalist, and said that keeping their secret would make him an accomplice. Threatened, he swore an oath of silence. But in the carriage home he kept insulting them, so the club's president stopped at the Quai des Ormes and challenged him. By lantern light on icy steps, with a short sword hidden in his cane, the president fought him. The general fell twice and died; the witnesses threw his body into the river. The president had been wounded three times. The three witnesses signed the statement to prove it was an honorable duel, not murder.","Franz begs to know the president's name. Villefort insists it cannot be known, while Valentine, who has often seen two scars on her grandfather's right arm, draws back. Using the dictionary, Franz goes through the letter M until Noirtier stops him at 'myself'. 'You killed my father?' 'Yes.' Franz collapses into a chair, and Villefort flees the room, tempted to smother what life remains in the terrible old man."],"characters":["noirtier","villefort","valentine-de-villefort","franz-depinay","barrois","general-depinay","napoleon"]},{"number":76,"title":"Progress of Cavalcanti the Younger","volume":4,"summary":"Andrea Cavalcanti, now accepted in Paris society, courts Eugénie with Danglars' encouragement while Albert looks on unbothered. Danglars asks Monte Cristo to press the Morcerfs for a decision, then receives news from Greece about 'Fernand and Yanina'.","details":["The 'major' has gone back to the gaming tables of Lucca, leaving Andrea his papers. In two weeks Andrea has become a fashionable figure, called count and rumored to be heir to riches buried in the quarries of Saravezza. When Monte Cristo calls on the Danglars, the baroness, still nervous of him since Auteuil, is soon charmed again. Andrea sighs and gazes at Eugénie, who coldly escapes to her music room with her singing teacher Louise d'Armilly. Danglars sends Andrea in after them, and the count smiles to hear him sing a Corsican song, which reminds him of Benedetto.","Madame Danglars praises her husband's calm over a loss at Milan; the count notes that Danglars now hides his losses, and advises her to build a fortune of her own, which makes her blush. She tells him Franz broke off his engagement to Valentine yesterday, for unknown reasons, and that Villefort is taking it 'like a philosopher'. Danglars keeps calling Andrea 'Prince Cavalcanti'.","Albert arrives and hears that Eugénie is at the piano with Cavalcanti. He takes it with mocking good humor, which annoys Danglars. Aside, Danglars tells the count he will give his daughter only to a man who loves her, complains of 'obscurity' in the Morcerfs' past, teases the count about the proud Mercédès taking his arm at the ball, and asks him to make Albert's father either set a wedding date or break off. Called away, Danglars returns agitated: his courier from Greece has come, and there is 'a whole history connected with the names Fernand and Yanina'. He asks the count to take Albert away and send the father to him. The count turns aside to hide a look of pity, and leaves with Albert."],"characters":["andrea-cavalcanti","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","edmond-dantes","hermine-danglars","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","danglars","villefort","franz-depinay","valentine-de-villefort","albert-de-morcerf","fernand-mondego","mercedes","lucien-debray"]},{"number":77,"title":"Haydée","volume":4,"summary":"Monte Cristo introduces Albert to Haydée, who turns out to be the daughter of Ali Pasha of Yanina. She tells how her father was betrayed and killed, and how she and her mother were sold as slaves.","details":["Driving home from the Danglars’ house, Albert complains that Andrea Cavalcanti is courting Eugénie Danglars and that the family is cold to him. The count says Danglars has asked him to push the Count of Morcerf, Albert’s father, to settle the marriage. At the count’s house Albert hears music from a guzla (a Balkan stringed instrument) and learns that the player is Haydée, a slave the count bought in Constantinople. She is also the daughter of Ali Tepelini, Pasha of Yanina, the ruler Albert’s father served when he made his fortune.","Albert begs to meet her. The count agrees on two conditions: Albert must tell no one, and he must never tell Haydée that his father served hers. Speaking Greek, which Albert cannot follow, the count tells Haydée to describe her father’s fate but to name neither the traitor nor the treason.","Speaking Italian, Haydée remembers her early childhood in Yanina. Then came a night flight to a kiosk (a pavilion) on the lake. There the loyal soldier Selim guarded a cavern full of gold and gunpowder. If Ali sent his dagger, Selim was to blow everything up. If Ali sent his ring, he was to put out the match. Ali had sent a trusted French officer to the sultan to negotiate. Soon a messenger arrived with the ring and news of a pardon. Selim put out the match and was stabbed at once by soldiers of Kourchid, the sultan’s commander, and Ali was killed in the fighting that followed. Twice Albert asks the French officer’s name, and twice Haydée says she cannot remember it.","Kourchid handed Haydée and her mother Vasiliki to the man who had done most to bring about Ali’s death. He sold them to slave merchants. Vasiliki dropped dead in Constantinople when she saw Ali’s head displayed over the city gate. Haydée was sold to an Armenian and later to the Sultan Mahmoud, and the count bought her from the sultan with an emerald. Albert leaves bewildered."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","haydee","ali-tebelin","vasiliki","selim","kourchid","ali","baptistin","andrea-cavalcanti","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","danglars","lucien-debray","fernand-mondego"]},{"number":78,"title":"We hear From Yanina","volume":4,"summary":"Franz breaks off his engagement to Valentine, and Noirtier makes her his heir. Danglars stalls Morcerf’s marriage proposal. Beauchamp’s newspaper reports that a French officer named Fernand betrayed Yanina, and Albert challenges Beauchamp to a duel.","details":["After Noirtier’s revelation about Franz’s father, Franz writes to Villefort that the marriage is impossible. Madame de Villefort tells the waiting guests that Noirtier has had a fit. Valentine meets Maximilian at the garden gate and tells him “We are saved,” but she keeps the secret. Madame de Villefort then asks Noirtier to give his fortune back to Valentine. He agrees, and the next day he makes a new will leaving everything to Valentine, on condition that she never leaves him.","The Count of Morcerf puts on his general’s uniform and formally asks Danglars for Eugénie’s hand for Albert. Danglars coldly says he must think it over and refuses to explain. He hints at “calumnies” (slanders). Morcerf leaves in a fury, and Danglars notices that Morcerf never once asked whether the objection concerned him personally. That evening Andrea Cavalcanti is the last guest to leave Danglars’ house.","The next morning Danglars smiles over a paragraph in l’Impartial, Beauchamp’s newspaper, headed “We hear from Yanina.” It says the castle of Yanina was handed to the Turks by a French officer named Fernand. Albert finds Monte Cristo at a shooting gallery, where the count has shot ordinary playing cards full of holes so precisely that they look like other cards. Albert asks him to be his second. The count suggests asking Haydée what happened, then advises Albert to see Beauchamp alone. He refuses to act as second or to give lessons, and says Albert may learn why at some future time.","Beauchamp says he did not write the paragraph and that nobody else would connect “Fernand” with the Count of Morcerf. Albert’s threats make him refuse to retract without first looking into it. He asks for three weeks, until 21 September. Then he will either retract or fight."],"characters":["franz-depinay","villefort","noirtier","heloise-de-villefort","valentine-de-villefort","maximilien-morrel","fernand-mondego","danglars","andrea-cavalcanti","albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","baptistin","ali","beauchamp","haydee","chateau-renaud"]},{"number":79,"title":"The Lemonade","volume":4,"summary":"Through Valentine, Noirtier tells Morrel they may marry once she comes of age. Then old Barrois drinks from Noirtier’s lemonade and dies in agony, and Dr. d’Avrigny proves that the lemonade was poisoned.","details":["Noirtier sends for Maximilian, and Valentine interprets for him. Noirtier plans to move to separate lodgings and take Valentine with him. If Villefort refuses, she will wait ten months until she comes of age and has her own fortune. Then, with her grandfather’s consent, she will marry Maximilian. Until then they must be patient. Barrois, Noirtier’s old servant, is hot from running an errand, and Valentine offers him a glass of Noirtier’s lemonade.","Minutes later Barrois is seized by terrible convulsions. Morrel hides, and Villefort runs for Dr. d’Avrigny, who is upstairs with little Édouard. Madame de Villefort comes down slowly. She looks first at Noirtier and then at the dying servant, asks whether he has eaten, remarks that lemonade was bad for him, and leaves, saying she cannot bear the sight of blood. Morrel slips out by a back staircase.","D’Avrigny questions Barrois and Noirtier. Barrois made the lemonade but left it in the pantry, and Valentine carried it into the room. It was also Valentine who offered him a drink. Noirtier drank some too and noticed a bitter taste, but he feels just as he does after his usual Sunday dose of medicine. Barrois has a second, worse attack and dies.","Alone with Villefort, d’Avrigny says it is the same poison that killed Madame de Saint-Méran. He drips some lemonade into syrup of violets, which turns green. Barrois has been poisoned, he declares, and he will swear to it before God and man."],"characters":["maximilien-morrel","barrois","noirtier","valentine-de-villefort","villefort","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","dr-davrigny","marquise-de-saint-meran"]},{"number":80,"title":"The Accusation","volume":4,"summary":"D’Avrigny argues that the poison was meant for Noirtier and accuses Valentine of all the deaths. Villefort refuses to give up his daughter. The doctor withdraws from the house, and the frightened servants all leave.","details":["D’Avrigny explains why Noirtier survived. For a year the doctor has been treating his paralysis with brucine, a poison in small doses, so his body is used to it. He traces a pattern behind the deaths. The Marquis and Marquise de Saint-Méran died, leaving a double fortune to inherit. Noirtier was spared while his will favored the poor, but as soon as he made a new will he was attacked. Look for whoever profits from the crime, he says.","It is Villefort who first speaks Valentine’s name. D’Avrigny presses on. She packed the Marquis’s medicines, prepared the Marquise’s drinks and carried in Noirtier’s lemonade. He denounces her to Villefort, as the king’s attorney, as the poisoner who deserves the scaffold.","Villefort falls to his knees. He will not hand his daughter to the executioner, and if she turned out to be innocent, he says, he would kill himself. D’Avrigny agrees to keep the secret but will never come to the house again. To the servants he announces that Barrois died of apoplexy (a stroke), and he quietly tells Villefort to throw away the syrup of violets.","That evening all the servants give notice, saying “death is in this house.” They praise their gentle young mistress Valentine as they go. Watching Valentine cry, Villefort glances at his wife and thinks he sees a faint, gloomy smile cross her lips."],"characters":["dr-davrigny","villefort","noirtier","valentine-de-villefort","heloise-de-villefort","barrois","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran"]},{"number":81,"title":"The Room of the Retired Baker","volume":4,"summary":"Andrea asks Danglars for Eugénie’s hand and is accepted. Caderousse demands more money from him. Andrea claims Monte Cristo is his real father, draws Caderousse a plan of the count’s house and tells him when it will be empty.","details":["Andrea tells Danglars that his father has promised him 150,000 livres a year once he marries, and that his late mother left about two million. Danglars eagerly accepts him. Andrea lets slip a vulgar “Very good, father-in-law” that betrays his low birth. He says Monte Cristo refuses on principle to arrange marriages for anyone, and he gets Danglars to cash a draft signed by the count.","Caderousse refuses his monthly 200 francs and leaves a note summoning Andrea. Disguised in his groom’s livery, Andrea visits him on the Rue Ménilmontant, where Caderousse lives as “M. Pailletin, retired baker.” Over a Provençal breakfast Caderousse calls him “my little Benedetto” and brings up the past they shared “down there,” in the galleys (prison labor). He boasts that he once knew Danglars and the Count of Morcerf, and demands more money.","Andrea raises the allowance to 500 francs. Then he confides that he believes Monte Cristo is his true father. According to Andrea, the count pays “old Cavalcanti” to pose as his father and has made a will leaving him 500,000 francs. Caderousse is dazzled by talk of the count’s wealth. Andrea draws him a plan of No. 30 Champs-Élysées, showing the secretaire (a writing desk) in the dressing room, and mentions that the count will spend tomorrow night at Auteuil.","Caderousse also takes Andrea’s diamond ring. Andrea privately thinks Caderousse won’t sell it, “not at least till the day after tomorrow.” Alone, Caderousse studies the plan and mutters that whoever hurries Benedetto’s inheritance along will not be his worst friend."],"characters":["andrea-cavalcanti","danglars","eugenie-danglars","edmond-dantes","gaspard-caderousse","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","fernand-mondego","pierre-morrel"]},{"number":82,"title":"The Burglary","volume":4,"summary":"An anonymous letter warns Monte Cristo of a burglary, so he lies in wait and catches Caderousse in the act. Disguised as the Abbé Busoni, he makes Caderousse write a letter exposing Andrea, then lets him go, and an accomplice waiting outside stabs Caderousse.","details":["At Auteuil, Bertuccio reports that the count’s house in Normandy is ready, a sloop (a small sailing ship) is waiting, and fresh horses are posted along the road to Tréport. Then Baptistin brings an anonymous letter. A man will break into the Paris house tonight to steal papers from the secretaire in the dressing room, and the count should deal with him personally, without the police. Suspecting assassins, the count sends all his servants to Auteuil and secretly returns to Paris with Ali. They wait in the dark bedroom, armed.","At a quarter to midnight a man cuts out a windowpane and climbs in, while a second man keeps watch in the street. When the burglar’s lantern lights his face, the count recognizes him. He puts a priest’s cassock and wig over the coat of mail he is wearing, steps in with a candle and greets the intruder: “Good-evening, my dear M. Caderousse.” Caderousse gasps, “The Abbé Busoni!”","Under questioning, Caderousse confesses everything. He escaped from the Toulon galleys with Benedetto, a young Corsican foundling, using a file supplied by an Englishman, Lord Wilmore. Benedetto now calls himself Andrea Cavalcanti, supposedly the count’s natural (illegitimate) son, and is about to marry Mademoiselle Danglars. When the abbé threatens to tell Danglars, Caderousse stabs him in the chest. The blade bends against the hidden mail, and the count wrenches his arm out of joint.","The count dictates a letter to Danglars: his daughter’s suitor is Benedetto, escaped convict No. 59. Caderousse signs it. He must leave by the window. If he gets home safely, the count promises to forgive him and pay him a small pension abroad. From the window the count watches the lookout run to the spot where Caderousse will climb down. As Caderousse reaches the ground, the man stabs him three times and runs, leaving him crying out for the reverend sir to help."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","bertuccio","baptistin","ali","gaspard-caderousse","andrea-cavalcanti","carconte","joannes","danglars","eugenie-danglars"]},{"number":83,"title":"The Hand of God","volume":4,"summary":"As he dies, Caderousse names Benedetto as his murderer and signs a statement. The count lectures him about God’s justice, then reveals who he really is, and Caderousse repents with his last breath.","details":["The count sends Ali for the procureur du roi (the king’s prosecutor), M. de Villefort, and for a surgeon. Caderousse names his attacker: Benedetto. Benedetto gave him the plan of the house hoping that Caderousse would kill the count, making Benedetto his heir, or that the count would kill Caderousse and remove him. Drops from a phial (a small bottle) briefly revive the dying man. The count writes out a statement, “I die, murdered by the Corsican Benedetto, my comrade in the galleys at Toulon, No. 59,” and Caderousse signs it.","The count explains that Benedetto also sent the anonymous warning, and that he himself saw the ambush coming and chose not to warn Caderousse. Instead he left him to God’s judgment. He goes back over Caderousse’s life: betraying his friend while drunk, wasting the fortune the abbé once gave him, murdering the jeweller, the galleys, his rescue by the Englishman, and now this third crime. Caderousse screams that there is no God.","The count takes off his wig. Caderousse thinks he looks like the Englishman, Lord Wilmore. The count says he is neither Busoni nor Wilmore, swears by his father’s tomb, and whispers his real name. Terrified, Caderousse begs God’s pardon and dies. “One!” says the count over the body. When the surgeon and the procureur arrive, they find the Abbé Busoni praying beside the corpse."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","gaspard-caderousse","andrea-cavalcanti","ali","villefort","carconte","joannes"]},{"number":84,"title":"Beauchamp","volume":4,"summary":"The police fail to find Benedetto, and Andrea’s engagement to Eugénie goes ahead. Beauchamp returns from Yanina with proof that Albert’s father betrayed Ali Pasha, but he generously hands Albert the papers, and Albert burns them.","details":["All Paris talks about the attempted robbery. The count says he was at Auteuil and that the Abbé Busoni happened to be staying in his house that night. Only Bertuccio turns pale whenever Benedetto’s name comes up. Villefort prepares the case, but after three weeks there is still no arrest. Meanwhile Andrea is received as Eugénie’s fiancé. Letters arrive from Major Cavalcanti approving the match, and it is agreed that Danglars will invest Andrea’s three million, although some people warn Andrea about the banker’s recent losses. Eugénie, who put up with Andrea only to get rid of Albert, now plainly dislikes him.","One morning Beauchamp appears at Albert’s house. He has spent the three weeks travelling to Yanina and back, and he shows his passport as proof. Sadly, he tells Albert that the newspaper paragraph was true. He hands over a statement signed by four leading citizens of Yanina: Colonel Fernand Mondego surrendered the castle for two million crowns. Beauchamp’s inquiries leave no doubt that this man is the Count of Morcerf. Albert collapses in tears.","Beauchamp says children cannot be blamed for their fathers’ faults, and he offers to destroy the only proofs. Albert burns every scrap and swears eternal friendship. But he is heartbroken about his father and terrified for his mother. Beauchamp warns him that it may not be over and asks pointedly about the broken Danglars engagement. To cheer Albert up, the two friends set off to visit Monte Cristo."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","bertuccio","andrea-cavalcanti","villefort","danglars","eugenie-danglars","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","fernand-mondego","ali-tebelin","mercedes"]},{"number":85,"title":"The Journey","volume":4,"summary":"Monte Cristo whisks Albert away to his seaside house in Normandy. Three days later Beauchamp’s messenger brings a newspaper that openly names the Count of Morcerf as the traitor of Yanina, and Albert rushes back to Paris.","details":["At the count’s house, Beauchamp says the matter is settled. The count is busy with papers about Cavalcanti that Danglars asked him to obtain. He insists he opposed the marriage and is forwarding the papers “like Pilate,” washing his hands of it. He mentions that Mademoiselle d’Armilly is going to Italy with letters of introduction from him. Complaining that the police keep sending him suspects in Caderousse’s murder, he invites Albert to the seaside. Albert accepts, and Beauchamp stays behind to watch the newspapers.","Albert says his mother Mercédès talks constantly about the count and calls him a noble nature, and the count sighs. They travel in the count’s carriage, with Ali driving relays of his black horses, and cover forty-eight leagues in eight hours. On the way the count remarks that he holds the power of life and death over Bertuccio.","The house near Tréport looks onto the sea, with a sloop in the creek flying the Monte Cristo arms. The friends spend three days hunting and fishing. On the third evening Albert’s valet Florentin gallops up with a letter and a newspaper from Beauchamp. Albert nearly faints. He borrows a horse and dashes off, leaving the paper behind. The count reads it. Another newspaper has printed that the French officer who surrendered Yanina and sold his benefactor to the Turks now calls himself the Count of Morcerf and sits among the peers."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","andrea-cavalcanti","danglars","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","hermine-danglars","villefort","gaspard-caderousse","mercedes","bertuccio","haydee","ali","baptistin","florentin","fernand-mondego"]},{"number":86,"title":"The Trial","volume":4,"summary":"Beauchamp tells Albert how his father was accused before the Chamber of Peers and defended himself well, until Haydée appeared with proof that he had betrayed Ali Pasha and sold her and her mother into slavery.","details":["Beauchamp learned that the article came from a government newspaper, whose editor said a man from Yanina had brought in a stack of documents. In the Chamber of Peers (the upper house of parliament), the Count of Morcerf was disliked as an upstart. He knew nothing until a peer who was his enemy raised the matter. Morcerf went deathly pale, but he demanded an immediate inquiry. A committee of twelve met that same evening, and Beauchamp watched hidden in a gallery.","Morcerf spoke well. He produced letters from Ali Pasha and Ali’s ring, and claimed he was away negotiating for Ali when the pasha died. Ali had trusted him with Vasiliki and her daughter Haydée, he said, but they disappeared. The committee was almost won over when the president read a letter from a witness who claimed to have been present at Ali’s death.","A veiled woman entered. It was Haydée. She produced her birth and baptism records and a bill of sale from the slave merchant El-Kobbir. It recorded that a French colonel named Fernand Mondego had sold her and her mother for 400,000 francs, and that the Count of Monte Cristo later bought her for an emerald. Haydée told the committee that the count was in Normandy and knew nothing of what she was doing. She had always wanted to avenge her father.","Morcerf denied knowing her. Haydée cried out that he was the man who brought the false pardon, took the ring, stabbed Selim and sold them, and she told the peers to look at the scar on his right hand. He hid the hand, said he had no reply to make, and rushed from the room. The committee unanimously found him guilty of felony, treason and conduct unworthy of the House."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","fernand-mondego","haydee","ali-tebelin","vasiliki","selim","el-kobbir","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":87,"title":"The Challenge","volume":4,"summary":"Albert swears to kill whoever ruined his father. A clue leads him and Beauchamp to Danglars, who says it was Monte Cristo who advised him to write to Yanina, so Albert turns his anger on the count.","details":["Beauchamp admires Haydée and suggests that whoever struck the blow was simply an agent of Providence. Albert wants an enemy of flesh and blood to fight. Beauchamp then reveals something he had kept back: the leading banker in Yanina told him that Danglars had written asking the same questions two weeks earlier.","At Danglars’ house they find Andrea Cavalcanti with the banker. Albert challenges Danglars and even offers to fight Andrea. Danglars calls him a mad dog. He admits writing to Yanina, saying a father has a duty to look into a suitor’s family. But he says it was Monte Cristo who suggested it, after Danglars remarked that Morcerf had made his fortune in Greece. The count saw the reply and knew Morcerf’s real name. Once the answer came, Danglars refused the marriage.","Albert puts the pieces together. The count bought Haydée, so he knew the truth. He advised Danglars to write, let Haydée tell her story in front of Albert, forbade Albert to mention his father’s name, and took Albert to Normandy just as the blow fell. Beauchamp agrees that Danglars was only a secondary player and that Albert must demand an explanation from Monte Cristo."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","haydee","danglars","andrea-cavalcanti","edmond-dantes","fernand-mondego"]},{"number":88,"title":"The Insult","volume":4,"summary":"Mercédès begs Albert to stay friends with Monte Cristo, but he insults the count publicly at the Opera. The count accepts the challenge, swears he will kill Albert, and asks Maximilian and Emmanuel to be his seconds.","details":["The count is home but sees no one, and he plans to go to the Opera, so Albert asks his friends to meet him there. He visits Mercédès, who is in bed with grief. He asks whether “M. de Morcerf” has enemies, and he reminds her that the count would eat nothing in their house, an Eastern sign of an enemy. Mercédès turns pale and begs him to keep the count’s friendship. Albert hints that she has special reasons for saying so. After he leaves, she sends a servant to follow him and gets dressed.","At the Opera, Albert, Beauchamp and Château-Renaud go to the count’s box, where Maximilian Morrel is sitting. Albert loudly demands an explanation. The count orders him out and remarks that showing off “is not becoming to everyone, M. de Morcerf,” a pointed reference to his father’s disgrace. Albert raises his glove to throw it, and Morrel catches his hand. The count takes the glove and promises to return it wrapped around a bullet.","The count tells Morrel that it was through Haydée that the Chamber learned of Morcerf’s treason, and that he will kill Albert before ten the next morning. When Morrel says Albert’s father loves him, the count flares up: “I will make him suffer.” Beauchamp comes to ask for an explanation, but the count refuses, though he lets Albert choose the weapons. They agree on pistols at eight in the Bois de Vincennes. Morrel and his brother-in-law Emmanuel agree to act as the count’s seconds."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","edmond-dantes","chateau-renaud","franz-depinay","lucien-debray","maximilien-morrel","mercedes","fernand-mondego","haydee","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut"]},{"number":89,"title":"The Night","volume":4,"summary":"Mercédès comes secretly to Monte Cristo, calls him Edmond and begs for her son’s life. He shows her the letter that sent him to prison and tells her what he suffered. Then he promises Albert will live, which means letting Albert kill him instead.","details":["The count is testing his pistols when a veiled woman rushes in. “Edmond, you will not kill my son!” It is Mercédès. She says she recognized him from his voice the moment she heard it. He replies coldly that his revenge has nothing to do with Ali Pasha. It is aimed at “the fisherman Fernand, the husband of Mercédès the Catalane.”","Mercédès blames herself for not waiting for him. He shows her the yellowed letter that Danglars wrote and Fernand posted, denouncing Edmond Dantès as a Bonapartist agent. The count took it from the prison file when he posed as a clerk of Thomson & French. He tells her of fourteen years in a dungeon of the Château d’If, so close to her, and of his father starving to death. Mercédès says she suffered too. For ten years she dreamed every night that he died being thrown from the Château d’If.","When she says she has seen the man she loved about to murder her son, the count breaks down and promises that Albert will live. But he cannot live with the public insult, so the duel will still take place, and it is his own blood that will be spilled. Mercédès trusts God, thanks him and tells him her heart has not changed. When she has gone he cries out that he was a fool not to tear his heart out on the day he resolved to take revenge."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","ali","baptistin","mercedes","albert-de-morcerf","fernand-mondego","danglars","maximilien-morrel","ali-tebelin","haydee","louis-dantes","m-de-boville"]},{"number":90,"title":"The Meeting","volume":4,"summary":"Expecting to die, Monte Cristo rewrites his will in favor of Maximilian and Haydée. But on the dueling ground Albert apologizes in front of everyone, saying the count was right to take revenge on his father.","details":["The count grieves over his ruined plans. He adds a note to his will saying that his death is by his own choice, and that Morcerf, Danglars and Villefort should know their punishment is only delayed. He finds Haydée asleep outside his door and realizes that while Mercédès remembered her son, he forgot that he had a daughter. He leaves Maximilian twenty million francs hidden in his grotto on Monte Cristo, which Bertuccio knows how to find, and hopes Maximilian will marry Haydée. The rest, about sixty million, goes to Haydée. She wakes, reads the will and tears it up, saying that if he dies she will need nothing. Then she faints. For the first time he wonders whether she loves him as more than a daughter. He copies the will out again.","Morrel and Emmanuel arrive early. Morrel failed to get swords substituted for pistols, but he has won the count the first shot at twenty paces. The count shows them his skill by shooting away the four sides of an ace of clubs. Morrel begs him to spare Albert, and the count calmly replies that Albert will kill him. At the meeting place he asks whether Morrel’s heart is free. When Morrel says he loves a young girl, the count murmurs, “Poor Haydée!”","Albert has asked all his friends to attend, so Franz and Debray turn up too. Albert arrives late and pale. Before everyone, he says he blamed the count for exposing his father, but he has since learned the count had the right. That right came not from Ali Pasha’s betrayal but from the fisherman Fernand’s treachery toward the count and the misery it caused. Albert thanks him for not being harsher and offers his hand. The witnesses are stunned, and Château-Renaud and Debray are openly scornful. The count understands that Mercédès saved her son by telling him the family secret, and he feels sure again that he is God’s instrument."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","mercedes","fernand-mondego","danglars","villefort","haydee","maximilien-morrel","pierre-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","bertuccio","ali","baptistin","albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","chateau-renaud","franz-depinay","lucien-debray","abbe-faria"]},{"number":91,"title":"Mother and Son","volume":4,"summary":"Albert’s friends coolly advise him to leave France. He and Mercédès both decide to give up the Morcerf house, name and fortune, and Monte Cristo offers Mercédès the small sum he once buried for her in Marseilles.","details":["Beauchamp and Château-Renaud praise Albert’s “generosity” with obvious irony and suggest he leave the country for a few years. Albert says he already meant to go and parts from them coldly. At home he glimpses his father’s pale face at a window. He takes only his mother’s portrait, leaves everything else in order with an inventory, and tells his valet to inform the count, his father, that he apologized to Monte Cristo. Soon afterwards his father drives away.","Albert finds his mother packing up her own things in exactly the same way. He intends to live without rank or fortune and earn his own bread. Mercédès suggests he take her father’s name, Herrera, and asks to go with him. He plans to take her to a modest lodging in the Rue des Saints-Pères.","As they leave, Bertuccio hands Albert a letter from the count. It urges Albert to spare his mother poverty. Twenty-four years ago, the count writes, he buried 150 louis (gold coins) for his betrothed under a fig tree in his father’s garden in Marseilles, and recently he dug them up untouched. Now he offers them to her. Mercédès accepts, saying he has a right to pay the dowry, which she will take with her to some convent."],"characters":["albert-de-morcerf","beauchamp","chateau-renaud","edmond-dantes","fernand-mondego","mercedes","franz-depinay","bertuccio","louis-dantes"]},{"number":92,"title":"The Suicide","volume":4,"summary":"The Count of Morcerf comes to challenge Monte Cristo to a fight to the death, and the count reveals that he is Edmond Dantès. Morcerf flees home, watches his wife and son leave him forever, and shoots himself.","details":["Riding back, the count reminds Maximilian that he has great power and will use it for those he loves. Morrel has an appointment, clearly a matter of the heart. Bertuccio reports that Mercédès and Albert are leaving their house, and the count sends him off with the letter. Haydée rushes to greet him, overjoyed. He begins to believe there might be “two Mercédès in the world” and that he could still be happy. He swears by his father’s tomb that no harm will come to him, and kisses her forehead.","Meanwhile the Count of Morcerf had secretly watched Mercédès packing and seen Albert come home unhurt, and at first he assumed his son had taken revenge. His valet then told him that Albert had apologized. Now Morcerf arrives with two swords. He says he hates Monte Cristo by instinct and wants to fight him to the death, without witnesses.","Monte Cristo lists his betrayals: deserting on the eve of Waterloo, serving as a guide and spy in Spain, and betraying and murdering Ali. Morcerf demands his real name, out of all his names: Monte Cristo in Paris, Sinbad the Sailor in Italy. The count leaves the room and returns in a sailor’s jacket and hat, reminding Morcerf of Mercédès, “my betrothed.” Morcerf backs out of the room crying, “Edmond Dantès!”","At home, Morcerf hides behind a curtain as Mercédès and Albert come down the stairs. He hears Albert say, “Courage, mother! Come, this is no longer our home!” Neither of them looks back from the hired coach. As the coach passes through the gateway, a shot rings out and smoke pours through a broken pane of his window."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","emmanuel-herbaut","maximilien-morrel","bertuccio","haydee","baptistin","fernand-mondego","albert-de-morcerf","mercedes","ali-tebelin"]},{"number":93,"title":"Valentine","volume":4,"summary":"Valentine has been taking Noirtier’s medicine on his orders. While Madame Danglars and Eugénie visit to announce Eugénie’s marriage to “Prince” Cavalcanti, she drinks some bitter water and collapses.","details":["Morrel makes one of his permitted visits to Noirtier’s room. Valentine is relieved that the duel ended peacefully. Noirtier is again planning to move out, claiming the air of the house is bad for her. She has felt unwell for two weeks, and on her grandfather’s orders she takes spoonfuls of his medicine every morning, now four. She mentions that a glass of sugared water she just drank tasted bitter, and suddenly she cannot see properly. Then Madame Danglars and Eugénie arrive, and Valentine goes upstairs to receive them.","Spelling out words with Morrel’s help, Noirtier asks for the glass and decanter from her room. Both come back empty. Valentine finished the glass as she passed through, and little Édouard emptied the decanter to make a pond for his ducks. From then on Noirtier keeps his eyes fixed on the door.","Upstairs, Madame Danglars announces Eugénie’s marriage to “Prince” Cavalcanti, the title Danglars likes to use. Eugénie says she does not care for him in the least and wants to be a free artist, though she is glad to have escaped marrying the son of a dishonored man. Valentine gently defends Albert, and Eugénie mocks his apology on the dueling ground. Valentine keeps flushing and turning pale, and Madame de Villefort sends her off to drink a glass of water.","On the way back down Valentine falls on the stairs. She insists she is fine and chatters about Eugénie’s betrothal party in three days. Then she gives a strange laugh, stiffens and goes still. Hearing the alarm, Madame de Villefort exclaims, “I told you so! Poor child!”"],"characters":["maximilien-morrel","valentine-de-villefort","noirtier","edouard-de-villefort","barrois","hermine-danglars","eugenie-danglars","heloise-de-villefort","andrea-cavalcanti","albert-de-morcerf","lucien-debray","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":94,"title":"Maximilian’s Avowal","volume":4,"summary":"Morrel rushes to Monte Cristo and confesses his love for Valentine. The count is horrified that he loves a Villefort but promises she will not die. Noirtier reveals he has protected Valentine by accustoming her to the poison, and a priest named Busoni rents the house next door.","details":["Villefort runs to d’Avrigny, crying that it is now Valentine’s turn, which surely proves she is innocent. Morrel runs to Monte Cristo, who calmly tells him that General de Morcerf has just blown his brains out. Morrel describes the conversation he once overheard in Villefort’s garden about poison. The count says he knows all about the house. If God’s justice is at work there, he says, Morrel should look away: “between the culprit and the victim I have no choice.”","Then Morrel cries out that he loves Valentine. The count roars like a wounded lion, “that daughter of an accursed race!” Within moments he masters himself and admits he had been watching the tragedy like a wicked angel, only to be bitten himself. It is noon, he says, and if Valentine is not dead yet, she will not die. He sends Morrel home to wait for news.","D’Avrigny is astonished to find Valentine alive. When Madame de Villefort comes in with tears in her eyes, Noirtier stares at her in terror, sweating. Alone with the doctor, Noirtier answers his questions. Barrois was poisoned, the poison was not meant for him, and the same hand has now struck Valentine, but she will not die. Noirtier has been giving her his brucine medicine to build up her resistance. D’Avrigny confirms that this saved her life. He tastes the new medicine Villefort brought himself and gives strict orders about Valentine’s care.","That same afternoon an Italian priest calling himself Signor Giacomo Busoni, the name the count used for his abbé disguise, leases the house next to Villefort’s. No one knows how the previous tenants came to leave, and workmen begin repairing the house that very night."],"characters":["villefort","maximilien-morrel","noirtier","valentine-de-villefort","dr-davrigny","edmond-dantes","baptistin","bertuccio","fernand-mondego","mercedes","albert-de-morcerf","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","barrois","heloise-de-villefort"]},{"number":95,"title":"Father and Daughter","volume":4,"summary":"Earlier that same morning, Eugénie told Danglars she would not marry Andrea. Danglars admitted he was close to ruin and needed the marriage to restore his credit, and she agreed on condition that she stay free.","details":["That morning, in the gilded drawing room, Eugénie calmly tells her father she will not marry Andrea Cavalcanti. She loves no one, wants to live free and alone, and points out that she is beautiful, clever and rich, and that the law does not let him force her to marry.","Dropping his mask of the indulgent father, Danglars admits the marriage is purely business. He is nearly ruined, and a banker’s credit is his life. He must soon put four million into a new railway concession. He also holds five and a half million belonging to charities, which could be withdrawn at any moment. Andrea’s three million, or even the rumor of it, would restore his credit.","Eugénie is not frightened of poverty. She could earn a fortune as a singer, she suspects her mother has already provided for herself, and she has never been loved, so she has learned to love no one. She asks him to rely on the rumor of Andrea’s fortune rather than the money itself, and to leave her completely free in her person. On those terms she agrees to sign the contract in three days, without telling him her own plans. Then she sings with Louise d’Armilly and sets out with her mother on the visits that end at the Villeforts’ house."],"characters":["danglars","eugenie-danglars","andrea-cavalcanti","hermine-danglars","edmond-dantes","louise-darmilly","heloise-de-villefort"]},{"number":96,"title":"The Contract","volume":5,"summary":"Monte Cristo refuses to stand in for Andrea’s father. At the grand contract signing he tells of a bloodstained waistcoat found with a letter to Danglars, and just as Andrea is due to sign, police arrive to arrest him for Caderousse’s murder. Andrea has vanished.","details":["On the day of the signing, Andrea calls on Monte Cristo and chatters about Danglars’ wealth and railway scheme. When Andrea credits the count with his success, the count coolly replies that he took Andrea up only on the word of Lord Wilmore and the Abbé Busoni and because of his father’s name. Personally, he says, he does not know Andrea. He refuses to stand in for Andrea’s absent father at the wedding, though he agrees to attend the signing and add his name. Andrea asks anxiously how the dowry will be handled.","That evening Danglars’ rooms are packed. Eugénie wears plain white, and Louise d’Armilly thanks the count for her letters of introduction to Italy. Madame de Villefort has come alone, since Valentine is still ill. The contract is read aloud.","As the baroness signs, she complains that Villefort was kept away by a new development in the murder case at the count’s house. The count explains. Caderousse’s missing waistcoat has been found, soaked in blood, with a letter addressed to Danglars in the pocket, and he has sent both to the king’s attorney. Andrea turns pale and edges out of the room.","The notary calls for “Prince Cavalcanti” to sign. At that moment soldiers block the doors and a police commissary asks for Andrea Cavalcanti: an escaped galley slave from Toulon, accused of murdering his former fellow prisoner Caderousse. But Andrea is gone."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","andrea-cavalcanti","danglars","eugenie-danglars","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","hermine-danglars","lucien-debray","beauchamp","chateau-renaud","heloise-de-villefort","valentine-de-villefort","louise-darmilly","villefort","gaspard-caderousse"]},{"number":97,"title":"The Departure for Belgium","volume":5,"summary":"While the guests flee the scandal, Eugénie and Louise d’Armilly carry out a long-planned escape. Eugénie cuts off her hair and dresses as a man, and using a passport obtained through Monte Cristo, they leave Paris by night.","details":["The guests leave as if fleeing the plague. Danglars gives his statement to the police, Madame Danglars hides in her boudoir, and Eugénie locks herself in her room with Louise. “I escaped the Morcerf only to fall into the Cavalcanti,” Eugénie says scornfully. She despises all men. They will leave tonight as they had planned anyway. A post-chaise (a travelling carriage) is already bought, and they have a passport made out to “M. Léon d’Armilly, artist,” to which Eugénie added “travelling with his sister.” Monte Cristo got it for her when she asked for letters to theatres in Rome and Naples.","They have 23,000 francs and as much again in jewels, and they mean to live as musicians. Eugénie puts on a full man’s costume and cheerfully cuts off her magnificent black hair. They plan to go by Brussels, up the Rhine and through Switzerland into Italy.","At midnight Eugénie, posing as a young man, bluffs her way past the sleepy porter. They collect the carriage at a laundress’s house, tell the postilion (the driver) Fontainebleau to throw off pursuit, and then turn north through the Barrière Saint-Martin. “M. Danglars no longer had a daughter.”"],"characters":["danglars","hermine-danglars","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","andrea-cavalcanti","albert-de-morcerf","lucien-debray","edmond-dantes"]},{"number":98,"title":"The Bell and Bottle Tavern","volume":5,"summary":"Andrea flees Paris with jewels stolen from Eugénie’s wedding gifts and reaches an inn at Compiègne, but gendarmes track him there. Escaping up a chimney, he tumbles into the room where Eugénie and Louise are sleeping, and he is arrested.","details":["On his way out, Andrea helped himself to the most valuable jewels from Eugénie’s wedding presents and jumped from a window. He hires a cab north with a story about catching up with a friend, and on the road a carriage carrying Eugénie and Louise passes him. He walks on, hires a horse at Chapelle-en-Serval (dropping a friend’s visiting card, so the innkeeper takes him for someone else) and reaches the Bell and Bottle inn at Compiègne at four in the morning. He asks for room No. 3, but a young man travelling with his sister has it, so he takes No. 7. His plan is to disguise himself as a woodcutter, slip across the border and sell the diamonds. He sleeps with his knife beside him.","He wakes too late. The telegraph has spread the alarm, and gendarmes (armed police) fill the courtyard and guard the only staircase. Andrea leaves a note and a pin to pay his bill, sets the door ajar and climbs up the chimney onto the roof. The brigadier lights a fire in the chimney, but Andrea is already crouching among the chimney pots. Fearing he will be spotted, he climbs down a different, smokeless chimney.","His foot slips and he crashes into room No. 3, where Eugénie and Louise are in bed. Louise screams and rings the bell. Andrea begs them to hide him, and Eugénie tells him to go back the way he came, but the brigadier spots him through the keyhole and breaks in. Eugénie urges him to kill himself. Andrea throws down his knife, saying, “one has friends,” and surrenders. He mockingly asks Eugénie whether she has a message for her father: was he not nearly her husband? The humiliated girls drive off through a jeering crowd and reach Brussels the next day. That evening Andrea is locked in the Conciergerie prison in Paris."],"characters":["andrea-cavalcanti","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","gaspard-caderousse","danglars"]},{"number":99,"title":"The Law","volume":5,"summary":"Madame Danglars begs Villefort to go easy on Andrea Cavalcanti, now exposed as the escaped convict Benedetto, to spare her family more scandal. Villefort, haunted by the deaths in his own house, refuses, and news comes that Benedetto has been arrested.","details":["After the disastrous contract-signing, Madame Danglars hurries, veiled, to the rooms of her lover Lucien Debray. He is out at his club, where friends are teasing him that he ought to marry Eugénie and her two millions himself. She waits until nearly midnight and goes home. The maid says Eugénie and Mademoiselle d'Armilly went to their room together, so she suspects nothing of their flight. Lying awake, she realizes how deep the disgrace is, and remembers that she felt no pity when Mercédès was struck by a similar blow.","She decides only Villefort can help, and plans to appeal to their old intimacy. Next morning she drives to his house, which now looks like a plague hospital: the porter suspects every visitor, and neighbours wonder aloud whether there will be another funeral today. Villefort receives her with a grief-stricken calm. She asks him to let “the impostor” escape, or at least to delay the trial. He corrects her: Andrea Cavalcanti, really Benedetto, is an assassin, a Corsican of unknown parents who was sent to the galleys (prison labour) at sixteen for forgery.","Villefort guesses what she is thinking: that people say the deaths in his house were not natural. Before a crucifix he swears that whoever the culprits are, they will die. In a bitter outburst he admits he has sinned, perhaps more deeply than others, and says every criminal he condemns proves he is not the only wicked man. A despatch arrives: Benedetto has been taken at Compiègne. Madame Danglars leaves, pale, and Villefort gloats that a murder case will make a splendid session of the assizes (the criminal court)."],"characters":["hermine-danglars","lucien-debray","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","villefort","andrea-cavalcanti","valentine-de-villefort","danglars","mercedes"]},{"number":100,"title":"The Apparition","volume":5,"summary":"As Valentine slowly recovers, a man steps through her library door one night. It is the Count of Monte Cristo, who has been watching her from the house next door and swapping her poisoned drinks for safe ones. He tells her to pretend to sleep and see who comes.","details":["Valentine is still weak and feverish. Her stepmother tells her about Eugénie's flight and Benedetto's arrest, but she hardly takes it in. By day Noirtier sits with her. In the evening Dr. d'Avrigny brings her night draught himself, and a nurse watches until she sleeps. Then the keys go to Villefort, so her room can only be reached through the rooms of Madame de Villefort and little Edward. Every morning Morrel gets news from Noirtier. Monte Cristo promised him that if Valentine lived two hours she would be saved, and four days have now passed.","At night Valentine is haunted by fevered visions. One night after eleven she sees the library door open without a sound and a man come towards her bed. When she reaches for her glass he stops her hand. He holds the glass up to the lamp, tastes a spoonful, and says in a living voice, “Now you may drink.” She recognizes the Count of Monte Cristo.","The count says he has rented the next house and has watched for four sleepless nights from behind the hidden door. Whenever her drink was dangerous, he replaced the poison with a healthy draught. He is keeping her alive for Maximilian, who has told him everything. He explains that the Saint-Mérans and Barrois were poisoned. Noirtier's long medical treatment protected him, and Noirtier has been protecting Valentine too, by making her share his bitter-tasting drinks. But a much stronger dose four days ago would have killed her. The count has seen the poisoner. Instead of naming them, he tells her to pretend to sleep and watch at midnight, then slips back behind the door."],"characters":["valentine-de-villefort","edmond-dantes","noirtier","maximilien-morrel","villefort","dr-davrigny","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","barrois","eugenie-danglars","andrea-cavalcanti"]},{"number":101,"title":"Locusta","volume":5,"summary":"Pretending to sleep, Valentine watches her stepmother, Madame de Villefort, pour poison into her glass. The count explains the motive, Valentine's fortune passing to little Edward, and gives her a pill, telling her to fear nothing even if she wakes in a tomb.","details":["The title refers to Locusta, a notorious poisoner of ancient Rome. Valentine waits, terrified. At half past midnight she hears the count scratch softly at the library door. Then the other door, the one from Edward's room, opens. Someone whispers her name twice, and she keeps breathing evenly. Peeping out, she sees a woman in a white dressing-gown emptying a phial into her glass. It is Madame de Villefort. Valentine's shudder makes her stepmother freeze and watch, but at last she empties the phial and slips away.","The count returns and tastes the drink. It is no longer brucine but a strong narcotic that would have killed her. He explains why: Valentine has 200,000 livres a year. If she dies, her father inherits, and after him his only son, Edward. That is why the Saint-Mérans died, and why Noirtier was targeted once he made Valentine his heir. He reminds her of a man in a brown cloak at Perugia whom her stepmother questioned about aqua tofana, a famous poison. Valentine would rather die than denounce her stepmother, and she worries the crimes will fall on Edward. The count says her father is not involved, though he should have been the one protecting her.","The count asks her to trust him blindly and to tell no one, not even her father. Whatever happens, even if she loses consciousness or wakes in a vault or a coffin, she must not be afraid. Thinking of Maximilian, she swallows a pea-sized pill from his little emerald box and falls asleep. He pours most of the poisoned drink into the fireplace so that it will look as if she drank it, and leaves."],"characters":["valentine-de-villefort","edmond-dantes","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","noirtier","villefort","maximilien-morrel","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran"]},{"number":102,"title":"Valentine","volume":5,"summary":"Before dawn Madame de Villefort finds Valentine cold and still. In the morning the nurse raises the alarm, Dr. d'Avrigny pronounces Valentine dead and finds poison in her glass, the servants flee, and Maximilian arrives in time to hear the word “Dead!”","details":["At about half past four, Madame de Villefort creeps in to see what her drink has done. She throws what is left in the glass into the ashes, then rinses and replaces the glass. Valentine is not breathing, her heart feels cold and motionless, and her nails are turning blue. The poisoner stands frozen by the bed, caught between fascination and remorse, until the lamp flickers out and she gropes her way back to her room.","The nurse comes at dawn, thinks Valentine is asleep, and dozes in an armchair. At eight she notices the stiff arm and screams. D'Avrigny and Villefort rush up, and the doctor declares that Valentine is dead. Villefort collapses at the bedside, and the servants all run from the “accursed house.”","Madame de Villefort arrives and forces out a few tears. Then she sees d'Avrigny studying the glass, which is somehow a third full again with the same coloured liquid she is sure she threw away. The doctor tastes it and says it is no longer brucine. A drop of nitric acid turns it blood-red. She staggers out and faints in her room. Only d'Avrigny sees her go, and he sends the nurse to help her.","Meanwhile Morrel comes to Noirtier's door as usual and finds the house empty and silent. Noirtier's terrified eyes tell him something has happened to Valentine. Morrel runs upstairs and hears d'Avrigny and Villefort say, “Dead!”"],"characters":["heloise-de-villefort","valentine-de-villefort","dr-davrigny","villefort","maximilien-morrel","noirtier","edouard-de-villefort"]},{"number":103,"title":"Maximilian","volume":5,"summary":"Maximilian carries Noirtier to Valentine's deathbed, declares himself her betrothed, and demands justice. Noirtier privately reveals the killer to Villefort, who swears a terrible revenge within three days if the secret is kept. An Italian abbé from next door watches over the body.","details":["Villefort orders the stranger out. Morrel leaves, but minutes later he carries Noirtier, chair and all, up the stairs with almost superhuman strength. Calling Noirtier “my father,” he begs him to tell them that he was Valentine's betrothed, and the old man weeps. Villefort knew nothing of the engagement. He forgives Morrel and tells him to say his last farewell.","Morrel refuses: Valentine needs an avenger, not just a priest. This is the fourth victim in four months, he says. He overheard Villefort and d'Avrigny in the garden the night Madame de Saint-Méran died, and he denounces the crime to the procureur du roi (the king's prosecutor). D'Avrigny joins him, ashamed that his silence encouraged the murderer. Noirtier signals that he knows the killer and asks to be left alone with Villefort.","A quarter of an hour later Villefort comes back livid, holding the shreds of a quill pen. He begs both men to keep the secret. Within three days, he promises, his revenge will make the boldest heart tremble, and Noirtier confirms it. D'Avrigny agrees. Morrel kisses Valentine's cold lips and rushes out in despair.","With the servants gone, d'Avrigny handles the formalities and brings the “doctor of the dead,” the official who certifies deaths, who confirms it. Villefort asks for the nearest priest, an Italian abbé who lives next door. The abbé already knows Valentine's name and has prayed for her. When Noirtier meets his eyes, he seems to read something there. Once he is alone, the abbé bolts the doors, including the one to Madame de Villefort's room."],"characters":["villefort","maximilien-morrel","noirtier","dr-davrigny","valentine-de-villefort","edmond-dantes","heloise-de-villefort","marquise-de-saint-meran"]},{"number":104,"title":"Danglars’ Signature","volume":5,"summary":"On the morning of Valentine's funeral, the count calls on Danglars and walks off with five million francs in bonds that the banker owed to the hospitals' charity fund. Unable to pay the charities, Danglars quietly prepares to flee France.","details":["The Abbé Busoni watched over Valentine's body until daybreak. Noirtier is found sleeping almost peacefully, which surprises d'Avrigny and Villefort. Villefort, who has not slept, buries himself in writing the indictment against Benedetto and leaves the funeral reception to a colourless cousin. Debray, Château-Renaud and Beauchamp gossip among the mourners. Beauchamp's paper has hinted that if four deaths had happened in anyone else's house, Villefort would have looked into them more closely. They notice that the count and Morrel are missing.","The count has gone to see Danglars. The banker complains about the year's misfortunes: Morcerf dishonoured and dead, himself made ridiculous by Benedetto, and Eugénie gone abroad. The count flatters his vast wealth. Danglars proudly shows him five bonds of a million francs each, payable on sight at the Bank of France. The count is still owed 5,100,000 francs on his credit, so he pockets the bonds and hands over a receipt he wrote in advance. Danglars panics, because the money was meant for the charity fund, but he cannot refuse without admitting weakness.","M. de Boville, now Receiver-General of the charities (the official who collects funds for the hospitals), arrives for the five million. Danglars shows him the count's receipt, drawn on Thomson & French of Rome, and asks him to come back tomorrow at noon. Boville mentions that Mercédès and Albert have given their whole fortune to the hospitals rather than keep money “so guiltily acquired.” Danglars claims Eugénie has gone to a strict convent with a nun. Once Boville leaves, Danglars calls him a fool, pockets the receipt, gathers about 50,000 francs, burns some papers, writes a letter to his wife, and checks that his passport is still valid."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","danglars","m-de-boville","villefort","dr-davrigny","noirtier","lucien-debray","chateau-renaud","beauchamp","eugenie-danglars","mercedes","albert-de-morcerf","fernand-mondego","andrea-cavalcanti","valentine-de-villefort","maximilien-morrel","hermine-danglars"]},{"number":105,"title":"The Cemetery of Père-Lachaise","volume":5,"summary":"At Valentine's funeral the count keeps his eyes on Maximilian, follows him home, and stops him from shooting himself by revealing that he is Edmond Dantès, the Morrel family's secret benefactor. He makes Maximilian promise to live one more month.","details":["Valentine is buried at Père-Lachaise in a vault marked “The families of Saint-Méran and Villefort,” as her mother Renée had wished, and a huge crowd follows the coffin. The count arrives and searches anxiously for Morrel, then spots him slipping between the yew trees. Twice he checks whether Morrel is carrying a weapon. After the speeches, the count hides behind a tomb as Morrel kneels at the grave and murmurs, “Oh, Valentine!” Morrel calmly turns down a ride and walks home to the Rue Meslay, and the count follows.","Julie, watching Penelon graft roses in the garden, greets the count happily. Upstairs Morrel's door is locked, so the count smashes its glass panel with his elbow and pretends he slipped. He sees the pistols and the letter Morrel was writing, and accuses him of planning suicide. Morrel furiously blames the count for his false promises. The count replies that he alone has the right to forbid it: he saved Morrel's father, sent the purse and the Pharaon, and he is the Edmond Dantès who once held little Maximilian on his knees.","Morrel calls Julie and Emmanuel. The count stops him before he can say the name, but they embrace the count as their unknown benefactor, and Julie fetches the treasured silk purse. The count says he will leave France within a week.","Alone with Morrel, the count urges him to hope. Morrel makes him promise that if he is not cured in a month, on 5 October, the count will give him pistols and a painless poison. It is 5 September, ten years to the day since the count saved Morrel's father. Morrel swears not to harm himself before then and agrees to live with the count. Haydée, the count says, left last night to wait for him."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","maximilien-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","penelon","chateau-renaud","beauchamp","lucien-debray","valentine-de-villefort","villefort","renee-de-saint-meran","pierre-morrel","haydee"]},{"number":106,"title":"Dividing the Proceeds","volume":5,"summary":"Madame Danglars learns that her husband has fled, and Debray coldly hands over her share of their stock-market profits and drops her. Upstairs in the same plain house, Mercédès and Albert, now poor, plan their futures: she will go to Marseilles, and he has enlisted as a soldier in Africa.","details":["Debray keeps a secret apartment in the Rue Saint-Germain-des-Prés, where a veiled lady visits him. The morning after the count's visit to Danglars, Madame Danglars rushes there: her husband left last night. His mocking letter says he paid out five million and is running from a second demand. He suspects she has hidden away much of his fortune, and he leaves her “as I took you,—rich, but little respected.”","She hopes Debray will now stand by her. Instead he coolly advises her to travel, then settles their partnership. From her stake of 100,000 francs, their speculations since April have made 2,400,000, and he hands her 1,340,000 in notes, bonds and a cheque. She waits in vain for one kind word, then leaves without saying goodbye. Debray, keeping 1,060,000 francs for himself, reflects that he would have married Valentine had she lived.","In a bare room upstairs live Mercédès and Albert, who moved here after giving up their fortune. Mercédès has lost her spark, and Albert tells her they have no money. His plan: she will go to the little house in the Allées de Meilhan in Marseilles, where 3,000 francs are buried in the garden. He has sold his watch and signed up for the Spahis (French cavalry serving in Africa) as a paid substitute for another man, for 2,000 francs, half paid now. He means to earn a name of his own. In six months he will be an officer, or dead.","On the stairs they run into Debray. Albert introduces him as “once a friend” and politely refuses his help. Debray, with a million in his pocket, is shaken by the contrast between the two women. Next day Mercédès leaves by coach. From a window at Lafitte's bank, a hidden man watches and asks God how he can restore the happiness he has taken from these innocent people."],"characters":["lucien-debray","hermine-danglars","danglars","mercedes","albert-de-morcerf","edmond-dantes","fernand-mondego","valentine-de-villefort","eugenie-danglars","maximilien-morrel"]},{"number":107,"title":"The Lions’ Den","volume":5,"summary":"In La Force prison, Benedetto still plays the Cavalcanti prince, sure that a secret protector will rescue him. His visitor is Bertuccio, the count's steward and his own foster-father, who promises to tell him the next day who his real father is.","details":["The “Lions' Den” is the section of La Force prison that holds the most dangerous inmates. Andrea, his fine clothes torn but carefully brushed, begs a keeper for twenty francs and boasts of his millionaire relatives. The keeper laughs and lets the convicts close in to beat up “the prince.” Andrea saves himself with a secret bandits' sign that Caderousse taught him, and the convicts accept him as one of their own.","Then he is called to the visitors' room. He has stayed silent since his arrest, convinced that the powerful protector who gave him money, a noble father and a brilliant marriage will save him, and he does not want to offend that person. The visitor is Bertuccio, who greets him as “Benedetto” and “unhappy child” and arranges for them to talk in a private room.","Andrea calls Bertuccio his adopted father. But he insists that someone else gave him 100,000 francs, invented an Italian father for him, and got him invited to the dinner at Auteuil, and he hints that his real father must be the Count of Monte Cristo. Bertuccio sternly forbids him to say so: the count is too favoured by Heaven to have such a son. Andrea has fallen into “terrible hands,” he warns, hands that can save him or crush him. Andrea demands to know his father, and Bertuccio says that is why he came. Just then the examining magistrate (the judge who questions suspects before trial) sends for the prisoner, and Bertuccio promises to return tomorrow."],"characters":["andrea-cavalcanti","bertuccio","gaspard-caderousse","edmond-dantes","villefort"]},{"number":108,"title":"The Judge","volume":5,"summary":"On the morning of Benedetto's trial, Villefort confronts his wife with her four murders. To spare his name a public scandal, he orders her to take her own poison before he comes back from court.","details":["Ever since the abbé's vigil, Noirtier has been strangely calm. Villefort has replaced every servant and shut himself away to prepare the case against Caderousse's murderer, even though the main evidence is a few words written by a dying convict. Once, in the garden, he sees Noirtier glaring with hatred at Madame de Villefort as she plays with Edward, and then at him, as if reminding him of his oath. “Have patience but one day longer,” Villefort answers.","He works through the night. In the morning his wife sends him a cup of chocolate, and he swallows it in one gulp, almost hoping it is poisoned. It is not. When she asks to come to the trial, he sends word that she should wait for him in her room. There he sends Edward out and bolts the door.","He asks her where she keeps the poison she uses, and accuses her of murdering M. and Madame de Saint-Méran, Barrois and Valentine. She cannot deny it. He reminds her that the scaffold awaits poisoners, but says he will not let his wife dishonour his name and his son. Surely, he says, she has kept some of her deadliest poison for herself. She begs for her life for Edward's sake. He replies that if she lived, she might one day kill the boy too. If he finds her alive when he returns, he warns, she will sleep in prison that night. He says farewell, and as she faints he double-locks the door behind him."],"characters":["villefort","heloise-de-villefort","noirtier","edouard-de-villefort","edmond-dantes","andrea-cavalcanti","gaspard-caderousse","bertuccio","valentine-de-villefort","marquis-de-saint-meran","marquise-de-saint-meran","barrois","dr-davrigny"]},{"number":109,"title":"The Assizes","volume":5,"summary":"All fashionable Paris crowds into the court for Benedetto's trial. While they wait, Beauchamp, Debray and Château-Renaud gossip about the deaths at Villefort's house, the absent count, and a veiled lady who looks like Madame Danglars.","details":["“The Benedetto affair” is the sensation of Paris. Many who knew the charming “Prince Cavalcanti” think he must be the victim of some plot, and some expect his father to reappear. On a brilliant September day the courtroom fills early.","Debray reports that the president of the court thinks Benedetto is just a commonplace rascal. Beauchamp spots a veiled lady he is sure is Madame Danglars, only ten days after her daughter's flight and three after her husband's bankruptcy. Debray blushes and says she is probably some foreign princess. They notice Madame de Villefort is not there. Debray likes her, while Château-Renaud dislikes her by instinct.","Beauchamp repeats a story from a servant who left Villefort's house: little Edward has supposedly been poisoning anyone who displeased him, using a drug from his mother's laboratory. The others call it absurd. Debray says the count is keeping out of sight because the Cavalcantis tricked him out of 100,000 francs. Beauchamp points out that the count is involved anyway: Caderousse was killed as he left the count's house, and the bloodstained waistcoat holding the letter that stopped the marriage contract lies on the evidence table. Château-Renaud has called on Morrel three times without seeing him. Then the court enters."],"characters":["beauchamp","lucien-debray","chateau-renaud","hermine-danglars","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","andrea-cavalcanti","villefort","edmond-dantes","bartolomeo-cavalcanti","gaspard-caderousse","maximilien-morrel","julie-morrel","eugenie-danglars","valentine-de-villefort"]},{"number":110,"title":"The Indictment","volume":5,"summary":"At his trial Benedetto reveals that he was born at Auteuil in 1817 and buried alive by his father, and that his father is the prosecutor himself, M. de Villefort. Villefort, broken, admits it before the court and walks out, and Madame Danglars faints in the crowd.","details":["Villefort's indictment is powerful, but Benedetto stays calm and stares straight back at him. Asked his name, he asks to answer the questions in his own order. He gives his age: born on the night of 27 September 1817. He gives his birthplace: Auteuil. Villefort turns livid. As for his profession, he was a forger, then a thief, and lately an assassin.","He cannot give his own name, he says, but he can give his father's: the king's attorney, Villefort. The court erupts. He explains that he was born at No. 28 Rue de la Fontaine in a room hung with red damask. His father told his mother the baby was dead, wrapped him in a napkin marked with an H and an N, and buried him alive in the garden. A man with a vendetta against his father stabbed him, dug up the box and took the living baby to the foundling hospital. Later a woman from Rogliano in Corsica claimed him. The reader knows these two from Bertuccio's own story: Bertuccio and his sister-in-law Assunta. His adopted father once told him the crime was his father's, not his.","Asked about his mother, Benedetto says she thought him dead and is innocent. A veiled lady in the crowd shrieks and is carried out; her veil falls, and it is Madame Danglars. When the president demands proof, Benedetto says, “Look at M. de Villefort.” Villefort staggers forward, says no proof is needed, admits that everything is true and that he is “in the hands of an avenging God,” and leaves, placing himself under the authority of whoever succeeds him. The case is adjourned, and Benedetto leaves calm and more fascinating than ever."],"characters":["andrea-cavalcanti","villefort","hermine-danglars","bertuccio","assunta","beauchamp","chateau-renaud","lucien-debray","valentine-de-villefort","fernand-mondego"]},{"number":111,"title":"Expiation","volume":5,"summary":"Villefort rushes home meaning to save his wife and flee with her, but finds that she has poisoned herself and taken Edward with her. The Abbé Busoni, visiting Noirtier, reveals himself as Edmond Dantès. Villefort shows him the bodies and loses his mind.","details":["The crowd parts silently for Villefort. In his carriage, his wife's forgotten fan reminds him of the sentence he passed on her. Now that his own guilt is public, he decides she must live: they will flee together, she will repent and raise Edward, and no one will know her crimes. He orders the coachman to hurry.","Edward's room is empty and his wife's door is bolted. Villefort breaks it open. Héloïse stands rigid, says “It is done,” and falls dead, clutching a crystal bottle. The servants say she sent for Edward half an hour ago. Beyond her body Villefort finds the boy on a sofa, dead, with a note in her hand: “A good mother cannot depart without her son.”","Shattered, Villefort goes down to his father and finds the Abbé Busoni there. The abbé says Villefort has paid his debt and that he will pray for him. Then he pulls off his wig, and Villefort sees the Count of Monte Cristo. The count tells him to go back further, to his wedding day in Marseilles twenty-three years ago: “I am Edmond Dantès!” Villefort drags him upstairs to the bodies and cries, “Are you well avenged?”","The count turns pale, feeling that he has gone beyond the bounds of vengeance. He carries Edward into Valentine's old room and tries for a quarter of an hour to revive him, but no skill can do it. He lays the boy beside his mother. Outside, Villefort is digging wildly in the garden, crying that he will find the child: he has gone mad. Doubting for the first time whether he had the right to do what he did, the count resolves to “save the last.” He leaves Paris with Morrel the next day. Haydée has gone ahead with Ali, and Bertuccio stays behind with Noirtier."],"characters":["villefort","heloise-de-villefort","edouard-de-villefort","edmond-dantes","noirtier","maximilien-morrel","baptistin","haydee","ali","bertuccio","valentine-de-villefort","renee-de-saint-meran"]},{"number":112,"title":"The Departure","volume":5,"summary":"The count says goodbye to Julie and Emmanuel and leaves Paris with Maximilian. In Marseilles they see Albert sail for Africa, and the count visits Mercédès in old Dantès' house for a last, sorrowful talk.","details":["Julie and Emmanuel talk about the sudden falls of Morcerf, Danglars and Villefort, as if some avenging spirit were at work. The count arrives to collect Maximilian. They will go first to Marseilles, and he must be in Rome in five days. He tells his friends they will probably never see him again, and as he leaves he asks Julie whether she still trusts “Sinbad the Sailor.” Outside, Ali reports that he placed the count's letter before Noirtier, who signalled “yes.”","From the hill of Villejuif the count looks back over Paris and declares his mission complete. He says he leaves without pride or hatred, though with many regrets. They travel swiftly by carriage and steamboat to Marseilles. On the quay where Morrel's father once greeted the Pharaon, they see Albert, now in uniform, sailing for Algiers, while a veiled woman waves goodbye. Morrel goes to visit his father's grave.","The count goes to the little house in the Allées de Meilhan, his father's old home, which he has given to Mercédès. He finds her weeping in the garden. He admits he caused her misfortunes, but she cannot hate the man who spared her son. Instead she blames herself: she was weak, she married Fernand, and she let him die. The count says he was only an instrument of God, driven on like an avenging angel. She asks only for Albert's happiness and refuses money for herself, saying she will live on the sum he left buried in the garden and spend her days in prayer. She promises they will meet again in heaven. Alone at the window, she murmurs, “Edmond, Edmond, Edmond!”"],"characters":["edmond-dantes","maximilien-morrel","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","ali","noirtier","albert-de-morcerf","mercedes","pierre-morrel","louis-dantes","fernand-mondego","valentine-de-villefort"]},{"number":113,"title":"The Past","volume":5,"summary":"Shaken by doubt, the count revisits the Château d'If, hears his own story told as legend, and regains his conviction when he finds the Abbé Faria's manuscript. He then arranges to meet Maximilian on the island of Monte Cristo on 5 October.","details":["Since Edward's death the count has feared that his vengeance went astray. To look his past in the face, he hires a boat to the Château d'If, retracing the journey he made as a prisoner twenty-four years ago. The fortress has held no prisoners since the revolution of 1830.","A guide shows him his old cell and tells a local legend: a dangerous prisoner dug a tunnel to the cell of a “mad” priest, took the dead priest's place in his burial sack, and was thrown into the sea, surely to drown. He was known only as “No. 34.” Alone in the dark, the count finds the marks of his old despair and the words he scratched on the wall: “Oh, God! preserve my memory!” In Faria's cell, No. 27, he weeps. The guide sells him Faria's great manuscript, written on strips of cloth. Its epigraph, “Thou shalt tear out the dragons' teeth, and shall trample the lions under foot, saith the Lord,” seems an answer to his doubts. He gives the guide 10,000 francs. Passing the Catalans on the way back, he murmurs a woman's name, and it is Haydée's.","At the cemetery he finds Morrel by his parents' graves. The count never found his own father's grave. He tells Morrel about a man who lost his father and his bride and spent fourteen years in a dungeon. That man's father died in poverty, and his bride married one of his persecutors, yet he has found peace and hopes to be happy. Morrel renews his promise. The count tells him that a yacht, the Eurus, will wait at Bastia on 4 October to bring him to the island of Monte Cristo on the 5th. Then the count leaves by steamer for Italy."],"characters":["edmond-dantes","abbe-faria","villefort","mercedes","louis-dantes","haydee","maximilien-morrel","pierre-morrel"]},{"number":114,"title":"Peppino","volume":5,"summary":"Danglars reaches Rome and cashes the count's receipt at Thomson & French, unaware that the bandit Peppino is shadowing him. Leaving the city, he is kidnapped and taken to Luigi Vampa's hideout in the catacombs of Saint Sebastian.","details":["A Frenchman wearing the ribbon of the Legion of Honour, who knows no Italian beyond musical terms like “Allegro!” and “Moderato!”, arrives in Rome and checks in at Signor Pastrini's hotel. It is Danglars. A man follows him to the bank of Thomson & French. This is Peppino, and the bank clerk is clearly in league with the bandits. They had been told in advance that Danglars was coming to draw about five million francs on the Count of Monte Cristo's receipt.","Danglars leaves delighted, with a letter of credit in his pocketbook, and sleeps with it under his pillow. Next afternoon he sets off on the Ancona road, planning to collect his money in Venice and Vienna. He dozes off. At a stop, fresh horses are harnessed, and a strong hand pushes him back when he tries to get out. Riders gallop on both sides, and a voice orders, “Dentro la testa!” (Heads in!). At first he fears he has been arrested. Then he realizes they have circled back past Rome onto the Appian Way.","Forced out of the carriage, he is led by Peppino through tall weeds into a hole in the rock and along the catacombs to the bandit chief, who is reading Plutarch's Life of Alexander. Danglars recognizes Luigi Vampa from Albert's stories, and he is locked in the same cell Albert once occupied. Since they have not killed him, he reasons, they mean to ransom him, perhaps for 8,000 crowns, twice Albert's price, leaving him over five million. Comforted, he falls asleep."],"characters":["danglars","peppino","luigi-vampa","signor-pastrini","edmond-dantes","albert-de-morcerf","eugenie-danglars","louise-darmilly","hermine-danglars"]},{"number":115,"title":"Luigi Vampa’s Bill of Fare","volume":5,"summary":"Danglars wakes to find his money untouched, but his captors will sell him food only at outrageous prices: a hundred thousand francs for a chicken. Starving, he signs a draft on his own fortune.","details":["Danglars wakes and finds his purse and his letter of credit for 5,050,000 francs untouched. Puzzled by these “singular bandits,” he decides to wait for them to make the first move. Hours pass with no food. Through the cracks in the door he watches his guards eat bread, cheese and onions, which start to look surprisingly appetizing. When he asks the giant red-haired guard for dinner, the man ignores him.","Then Peppino comes on duty with a pan of chickpeas and bacon, a basket of grapes and a flask of Orvieto wine. He politely offers Danglars anything he wants, as long as he pays. A youth brings in a roast fowl on a silver dish. Danglars tosses down a louis (a gold coin worth 20 francs), but Peppino says he still owes 4,999 more, a total of 100,000 francs. Danglars thinks it is a joke and refuses, and the fowl is taken away.","Half an hour later he asks for dry bread. It costs the same, Peppino explains, because they have “a fixed price for all our provisions.” When Danglars protests that he does not carry 100,000 francs, Peppino reminds him that he carries 5,050,000, enough for fifty fowls and a half. At last Danglars understands. He writes a draft on Thomson & French and eats a chicken that seems very thin for its price."],"characters":["danglars","peppino","luigi-vampa","albert-de-morcerf"]},{"number":116,"title":"The Pardon","volume":5,"summary":"Vampa tells Danglars that his ransom is all five million, and that a higher chief forbids killing him. Starved until he repents, Danglars is confronted by the count, who reveals himself as Edmond Dantès, forgives him, and sets him free.","details":["The next day Danglars is thirsty. Wine is 25,000 francs a bottle, and water is “even more scarce.” He demands to see the chief. Vampa tells him the ransom is simply the five million he carries, and that they are forbidden to shed his blood by a chief above Vampa himself. Danglars swears he will never sign again, but after two days he pays a million for a supper.","Twelve days later, after many splendid meals, he has only 50,000 francs left. Suddenly he clings to that last sum and chooses hunger instead. He prays and weeps for the first time in years. In his delirium he sees an old man dying of hunger on a pallet. By the fifth day he is chewing the matting on the floor. He crawls to the door and begs Vampa to take his last gold and just let him live.","Vampa answers that others have suffered more: men who died of hunger. A man in a cloak asks Danglars whether he repents, and Danglars says he does. The man steps into the light. He is not the Count of Monte Cristo, he says, but Edmond Dantès, whom Danglars sold and dishonoured, and whose father Danglars condemned to starve. He forgives Danglars and lets him keep the 50,000 francs. The five million Danglars stole from the hospitals has been returned to them by an unknown hand. Of Danglars' accomplices, he says, one is mad and the other dead. Danglars is fed and left free by the roadside. At dawn he bends over a stream to drink and sees that his hair has turned completely white."],"characters":["danglars","peppino","luigi-vampa","edmond-dantes","louis-dantes","villefort","fernand-mondego"]},{"number":117,"title":"The Fifth of October","volume":5,"summary":"On 5 October Maximilian comes to Monte Cristo to die. The count gives him what seems a deadly drug, but as he sinks, Valentine appears, alive. Haydée confesses her love, and the count leaves the young couple his fortune and a farewell letter and sails away with her.","details":["At sunset Maximilian's yacht reaches the island. He wades ashore, and the count greets him. Morrel is calm and still determined to die: he waited and hoped for a month, in vain. In the underground palace the count offers him nearly a hundred million francs to live, but Morrel holds him to his word. At half past eleven the count gives him a greenish substance from a jewelled golden box. Morrel stops the count from taking some himself, swallows it, and feels himself slipping away, promising to tell Valentine what the count has done for him.","As his sight fades, a door opens and a beautiful young woman appears. It is Valentine, alive. The count tells her that Morrel chose death to find her, and that he himself has “vanquished death.” He hopes God will accept the saving of these two lives as his atonement. Valentine speaks of Haydée, whom she loves as a sister, and the count asks her to protect Haydée, whom he means to free tomorrow and restore to her father's name and riches. Haydée overhears. She says she would die without him and confesses that she loves him. The count accepts her love as God's pardon and leads her away.","An hour later Morrel revives. Thinking the count has cheated him, he reaches for a knife, and then he sees Valentine. At dawn, walking by the sea, she tells him how the count revealed the crime and let her fake her death. Jacopo, the yacht's captain, brings a letter. Noirtier waits in Leghorn (Livorno) to bless their marriage. The grotto, the house in the Champs-Élysées and the château at Tréport are wedding gifts from Edmond Dantès, and he asks Valentine to give her father's fortune to the poor. The letter also tells her, for the first time, that her father is mad and her brother dead. It ends: all human wisdom is summed up in two words, “Wait and hope.” On the horizon, a white sail carries the count and Haydée away."],"characters":["maximilien-morrel","edmond-dantes","valentine-de-villefort","haydee","ali","jacopo","noirtier","villefort","edouard-de-villefort","heloise-de-villefort","julie-morrel","emmanuel-herbaut","pierre-morrel"]}]`),characters:JSON.parse(`[{"id":"captain-leclere","name":"Captain Leclere","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"The Pharaon's late captain, who died at sea and left Dantès an errand on Elba."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"The Pharaon's brave old captain, who died of brain fever after leaving Naples. Before dying he asked Dantès to deliver a packet on Elba."},{"from":7,"text":"On his deathbed he gave Dantès a ring and the packet for the grand-marshal, calling it a matter of great importance."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"danglars","name":"Danglars","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Baron Danglars","from":27,"note":"his title; Caderousse tells the abbé he is now a millionaire baron"}],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"The Pharaon's scheming supercargo, who envies and hates Dantès."},{"from":4,"text":"Author of the anonymous letter denouncing Dantès as a Bonapartist agent."},{"from":27,"text":"Dantès' betrayer, now a millionaire baron and banker in Paris."},{"from":46,"text":"Vain, vulgar Paris banker who has opened vast credit for the Count."},{"from":95,"text":"A banker on the edge of ruin, desperate for Andrea's millions."},{"from":104,"text":"Ruined banker fleeing Paris with money owed to the hospitals."},{"from":114,"text":"Fugitive banker held prisoner and starved by Luigi Vampa's bandits."},{"from":116,"text":"Released by Dantès, penniless and repentant, his hair turned white."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Danglars is the supercargo (the officer in charge of the cargo and accounts) of the Pharaon, about twenty-six years old. He fawns on his superiors, sneers at those below him, and is disliked by the crew. He resents Edmond Dantès, who is about to be made captain, and has been spying on him."},{"from":4,"text":"A cunning schemer who prefers to let others do his dirty work, Danglars gathers Dantès' rivals at La Réserve. He suggests prison as a way to part Dantès from Mercédès, and writes an anonymous denunciation with his left hand so that nothing can be traced back to him, then tosses it aside as a ‘joke’ within Fernand's reach."},{"from":5,"text":"After the arrest he fakes surprise, privately blames Fernand, and frightens Caderousse into silence. He also wins the trust of the shipowner Morrel."},{"from":13,"text":"Alone among the conspirators he sleeps peacefully: a cold calculator to whom a man's life matters less than a number. Fearing Dantès' revenge when Napoleon returns, he leaves the sea and goes to Madrid to work for a Spanish merchant."},{"from":21,"text":"In prison, the Abbé Faria helps Dantès work out that Danglars wrote the denunciation, disguising his hand. Dantès remembers, too, the inaccuracies in his accounts. He becomes one of the three men Dantès swears to punish."},{"from":27,"text":"According to Caderousse, Danglars became a cashier at a Spanish bank on Morrel's recommendation, made a fortune supplying the army, and married a second time, to a widow, Madame de Nargonne. He is now a millionaire baron living in the Rue du Mont-Blanc in Paris, and refused even to see the poor Caderousse."},{"from":30,"text":"Said to be worth six to eight million francs, he refused to help Morrel, the man to whom he owed the start of his fortune."},{"from":40,"text":"He is a baron ‘of a new creation’: Charles X gave him the title in 1829 for arranging a loan. He sits as an opposition deputy and plays the stock market, where he seems to get government news early."},{"from":47,"text":"Past fifty, with dyed black hair and a cunning face, he flaunts his wealth. He calls himself a man of the people but likes his servants to call him ‘my lord’. He suspects the Count's unlimited credit is a hoax, but is crushed by the Count's display of money. At home he sells his wife's favorite horses behind her back for profit."},{"from":62,"text":"Acting on false news, he panics and sells his Spanish bonds, losing about a million francs. Pale and surly afterwards, he is rude to his wife and eager to hear that the Cavalcanti are fabulously rich."},{"from":65,"text":"His marriage is a cold business arrangement. For sixteen years he has known of his wife's affairs, including her past with Villefort, and tolerated them for his own advantage; the couple have lived apart as husband and wife for four years. He cares only for his money."},{"from":66,"text":"His fortune is shakier than it looks: he has lost about 1,700,000 francs in a month. He now fancies the rich young Andrea Cavalcanti for Eugénie instead of Albert, resents the Morcerfs' older nobility, and reveals that the Count de Morcerf was once the fisherman Fernand Mondego. He writes at once to his correspondent in Yanina."},{"from":81,"text":"Cowardly but insolent, he puts off Morcerf's proposal for the marriage without explanation, then gloats over the newspaper paragraph about Yanina, saying it will make any explanation unnecessary. He gladly accepts Andrea as Eugénie's suitor once he hears of the young man's supposed millions, and promises a 500,000-franc dowry."},{"from":87,"text":"Challenged by Albert, he admits writing to Yanina, calls it a father's duty, and says the Count suggested it. He clearly has no intention of fighting."},{"from":95,"text":"In private he is a brutal husband and a domineering father. He admits to Eugénie that he is nearly ruined: he needs Andrea's three million, or at least the rumor of it, to cover a huge railway deposit, while he holds five and a half million belonging to charitable institutions that could be withdrawn at any time."},{"from":97,"text":"When soldiers burst in to arrest Andrea at the contract signing, Danglars shows abject terror, and is left humiliated and questioned by the police. By the end of the night his daughter has gone too."},{"from":106,"text":"Facing bankruptcy, he hands over to the Count the five million francs that belong to the charities in return for a receipt, then burns his papers and flees Paris by night, leaving his wife a letter accusing her of hiding away part of his fortune."},{"from":115,"text":"He reaches Rome and cashes the Count's receipt at Thomson & French, planning to settle in Vienna. On the road out of Rome he is kidnapped and shut in Luigi Vampa's catacomb hideout. Finding his money untouched, he expects to be ransomed, but the bandits give him nothing to eat except at ruinous prices, and hunger soon forces him to pay them."},{"from":116,"text":"Starved down to his last 50,000 francs, he prays for the first time in years and repents. His captor reveals himself as Edmond Dantès, the man he betrayed, and forgives him. Danglars is released with what little money he has left, and finds his hair has turned white."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Tried to turn Morrel against Dantès over the stop at Elba."},{"chapter":3,"text":"Muttered that the letter from Elba gave him ‘a capital idea’."},{"chapter":4,"text":"Wrote, with his left hand, an anonymous letter denouncing Dantès as a Bonapartist agent."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Frightened Caderousse into keeping quiet about the letter."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Left the sea for Madrid to work for a Spanish merchant."},{"chapter":30,"text":"Refused to help Morrel, his old benefactor."},{"chapter":46,"text":"Agreed to give the Count credit of six million francs for the year."},{"chapter":47,"text":"Sold his wife's horses behind her back, only to see them pulling the Count's carriage."},{"chapter":61,"text":"Sold his Spanish bonds in a panic on false news and lost about a million francs."},{"chapter":64,"text":"Offered Major Cavalcanti a seat in his carriage and arranged a business meeting."},{"chapter":65,"text":"Demanded his wife pay a quarter of his Spanish loss, and revealed he knew of her past with Villefort."},{"chapter":66,"text":"Revealed that Morcerf was once Fernand Mondego, and wrote to his correspondent in Yanina."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Stalled Morcerf and gloated over the Yanina article."},{"chapter":81,"text":"Accepted Andrea Cavalcanti as Eugénie's suitor."},{"chapter":87,"text":"Told Albert that Monte Cristo had advised him to write to Yanina."},{"chapter":95,"text":"Confessed to Eugénie that he was on the edge of ruin."},{"chapter":104,"text":"Took the charities' five million to the Count for a receipt, burned his papers and prepared to flee."},{"chapter":106,"text":"Fled Paris by night toward Fontainebleau."},{"chapter":114,"text":"Was kidnapped on the road out of Rome and locked in Luigi Vampa's hideout."},{"chapter":115,"text":"Signed a draft for 100,000 francs to buy a single chicken."},{"chapter":116,"text":"Was forgiven and released by Edmond Dantès, and found his hair had turned white."}]},{"id":"edmond-dantes","name":"Edmond Dantès","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Number 34","from":14,"note":"his prisoner number in the Château d'If"},{"name":"the Maltese sailor","from":21,"note":"the cover story he gives the smugglers who rescue him"},{"name":"Lord Wilmore","from":25,"note":"the name on his English passport"},{"name":"the Englishman from Thomson & French","from":30,"note":"the clerk of the Roman bank who visits Boville and Morrel"},{"name":"Sinbad the Sailor","from":30,"note":"the name signed on the letter that saves the Morrels"},{"name":"the Count of Monte Cristo","from":34,"note":"Franz recognises the Count in Rome as Sinbad"},{"name":"Lord Ruthven","from":34,"note":"the Countess G——'s nickname for him, after Byron's vampire"},{"name":"Abbé Busoni","from":43,"note":"his priest disguise, first named here; the abbé of ch 26–27"},{"name":"M. Zaccone","from":69,"note":"the false identity his disguises feed to Villefort's police inquiry"},{"name":"Giacomo Busoni","from":94,"note":"the full name the abbé uses to rent the house next to Villefort's"}],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Young first mate of the Pharaon, about to be made captain and marry Mercédès."},{"from":5,"text":"Young sailor arrested at his own wedding feast on a Bonapartist charge."},{"from":8,"text":"Innocent young sailor locked away without trial in the Château d'If."},{"from":17,"text":"Prisoner in the Château d'If, the Abbé Faria's pupil, who now knows who betrayed him."},{"from":21,"text":"Escaped after fourteen years in prison, now sailing with smugglers and sworn to revenge."},{"from":24,"text":"Escaped prisoner turned secret master of the Spada treasure of Monte Cristo."},{"from":25,"text":"Immensely rich and unrecognised, back in Marseilles in disguise to learn the truth."},{"from":30,"text":"Secret benefactor who saved Morrel, now turning from gratitude to vengeance."},{"from":31,"text":"Calling himself Sinbad the Sailor, he lives in a hidden palace on Monte Cristo."},{"from":34,"text":"The pale, mysterious Count of Monte Cristo, dazzling Rome and befriending Albert de Morcerf."},{"from":40,"text":"The fabulously rich Count of Monte Cristo, newly arrived in Paris society."},{"from":47,"text":"The Count, Paris's great sensation, quietly closing in on his old enemies."},{"from":83,"text":"The Count, who has whispered his true name to the dying Caderousse: “One!”"},{"from":92,"text":"The Count, unmasked as Edmond Dantès to Mercédès and Morcerf, pressing his vengeance."},{"from":100,"text":"The Count, secretly guarding Valentine from a poisoner inside the Villefort house."},{"from":105,"text":"The Count, revealed to Maximilian as Edmond Dantès, preparing to leave France."},{"from":111,"text":"Edmond Dantès, shaken by the innocent deaths his vengeance has caused."},{"from":117,"text":"Edmond Dantès, Count of Monte Cristo, sailing away with Haydée, his work done."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"A tall, dark-haired sailor of about nineteen, first mate of the merchant ship Pharaon. Skilled, calm and loved by the crew, he brings the ship home after Captain Leclere's death and is promised the captaincy by the owner, M. Morrel. He is devoted to his poor old father and engaged to the beautiful Mercédès."},{"from":8,"text":"Open and trusting, he offered his hand even to the hostile Fernand and invited Danglars, Caderousse and Fernand to his wedding. Arrested at the feast, he told the deputy prosecutor Villefort the whole truth about the letter he carried from Elba and trusted him as a friend, but was taken without trial to the Château d'If, where he swings between despair and rage."},{"from":17,"text":"Forgotten through the Hundred Days and after Waterloo, prisoner number 34 sank from prayer to rage to a decision to starve himself, until the sound of digging brought him back to life. His neighbour, the Abbé Faria, becomes his friend and teacher; together they work out that Danglars, Fernand and Villefort caused his ruin, and he swears vengeance. Gifted with a prodigious memory, he becomes a cultured man, and loyally refuses to escape without Faria."},{"from":20,"text":"Faria makes him heir to the lost Spada treasure, hidden on the island of Monte Cristo. When Faria dies, Dantès chooses life, revenge on his enemies and rewards for his friends, and boldly takes the dead man's place in his burial sack."},{"from":24,"text":"Now thirty-three, after exactly fourteen years in prison, he was rescued by smugglers, passing himself off as a Maltese sailor. Prison has made him pale, stern and unrecognisable, patient and secretive, though he grows fond of the loyal Jacopo. Following Faria's clues, he finds Cardinal Spada's treasure: an almost unimaginable fortune."},{"from":25,"text":"He returns to Marseilles unrecognised, travelling on an English passport as Lord Wilmore, and learns that his father is dead and Mercédès has vanished."},{"from":30,"text":"Disguised as an Italian priest, he hears from Caderousse exactly how he was betrayed and how his father starved. Then, posing as an English clerk of the bank Thomson & French, he takes Danglars's denunciation from the prison files and secretly rescues the ruined Morrel, signing himself “Sinbad the Sailor.” Having repaid kindness, he calls himself Heaven's substitute and sails off to punish the wicked."},{"from":35,"text":"As Sinbad he lives in a secret palace on Monte Cristo, friendly with smugglers and bandits. In Rome he is the Count of Monte Cristo, a figure of deathly pallor whom the bandit Vampa respects, and he reveals a cold philosophy of revenge: suffering inflicted over years must be repaid with suffering, not a quick death or a duel. He watches young Albert de Morcerf closely."},{"from":41,"text":"Learned, lavishly generous and cynical about gratitude, he rescues Albert from the bandits and asks him to introduce him to Paris, where 'plans long meditated' draw him. He arrives to the minute; Morrel's name moves him, and on meeting the Count and Countess of Morcerf he is paler even than Mercédès."},{"from":48,"text":"The Abbé Busoni who sent Bertuccio into his service was Dantès himself in disguise. He buys the Auteuil house tied to a dark secret of Villefort's, keeps the young Greek Haydée in secret apartments, and outplays Danglars with sheer wealth. To Villefort he calls himself one of the agents of Providence, rewarding and punishing."},{"from":63,"text":"With the grateful Morrels he almost gives himself away, crediting their rescue to an English friend, 'Lord Wilmore'. He leads Madame de Villefort on with talk of poisons, stages a fraud with a bogus 'Major Cavalcanti' and his 'son' Andrea, costs Danglars a fortune with a bribed telegraph operator, and at the Auteuil dinner torments Villefort and Madame Danglars with talk of a buried newborn child."},{"from":83,"text":"He prompts Danglars to look into what Fernand Mondego did at Yanina, and fools Villefort's police inquiry by playing both Busoni and Lord Wilmore. He refuses all food in Mercédès's house. As Busoni he catches Caderousse robbing his house and lets fate take its course, then whispers to the dying man his true name: Edmond Dantès."},{"from":92,"text":"Morcerf's treachery at Yanina is exposed, and Albert challenges the Count, who vows to kill him. Mercédès calls him Edmond and begs for her son; he promises Albert will live and resolves to die in the duel instead. Spared by Albert's apology, he reveals himself to Morcerf as Edmond Dantès, and begins to hope for happiness with Haydée."},{"from":105,"text":"He watched the poisonings in the Villefort house as God's justice, until he learned that Maximilian Morrel loves Valentine; from the house next door he now guards her night after night. He strips Danglars of five million francs, and stops Maximilian's suicide by revealing that he is Edmond Dantès, the man who saved his father."},{"from":113,"text":"He reveals to Villefort that Busoni and Monte Cristo are both Edmond Dantès, but the death of the child Edward makes him doubt his right to vengeance. He takes leave of Mercédès and revisits his cell in the Château d'If, where Faria's manuscript seems to confirm his mission."},{"from":117,"text":"He reveals himself to Danglars and pardons him. Hoping that saving Valentine and Morrel makes amends for the evil he has done, he gives them his property and sails away with Haydée, whose love he accepts as God's pardon."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Brought the Pharaon home after Captain Leclere's death and was promised the captaincy."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Was arrested at his wedding feast just before going to be married."},{"chapter":8,"text":"Was locked in the Château d'If and thrown into an underground dungeon."},{"chapter":15,"text":"Tunnelled through his wall with a saucepan handle and made contact with another prisoner."},{"chapter":17,"text":"Learned who betrayed him and swore a solemn oath of vengeance."},{"chapter":20,"text":"Sewed himself into Faria's burial sack and was thrown into the sea."},{"chapter":21,"text":"Swam to freedom and was rescued by smugglers after fourteen years in prison."},{"chapter":24,"text":"Found Cardinal Spada's treasure in the second grotto on Monte Cristo."},{"chapter":25,"text":"Returned to Marseilles unrecognised and bought his father's old house as Lord Wilmore."},{"chapter":30,"text":"Turned out to be the Italian priest who had learned from Caderousse that Danglars wrote the denunciation and Fernand posted it."},{"chapter":30,"text":"Saved Morrel in secret as “Sinbad the Sailor,” then sailed away vowing to punish the wicked."},{"chapter":31,"text":"Received the blindfolded Franz in his underground palace on Monte Cristo."},{"chapter":37,"text":"Freed Albert from Vampa's bandits without paying any ransom."},{"chapter":40,"text":"Arrived at Albert's breakfast in Paris on the stroke of half-past ten, as promised."},{"chapter":61,"text":"Bribed a telegraph operator to send a false message that cost Danglars a fortune."},{"chapter":69,"text":"Pulled off Lord Wilmore's wig, whiskers, false jaw and scar after the police agent left."},{"chapter":83,"text":"Revealed his true name to the dying Caderousse and said, “One!”"},{"chapter":89,"text":"Promised Mercédès that her son would live."},{"chapter":92,"text":"Revealed himself to Morcerf as Edmond Dantès."},{"chapter":104,"text":"Took five million francs from Danglars in exchange for a receipt."},{"chapter":105,"text":"Stopped Maximilian's suicide by revealing that he was Edmond Dantès."},{"chapter":111,"text":"Unmasked himself to Villefort, then tried and failed to revive Edward."},{"chapter":113,"text":"Revisited his old cell in the Château d'If and bought Faria's manuscript from the guide."},{"chapter":116,"text":"Revealed his identity to Danglars and pardoned him."},{"chapter":117,"text":"Reunited Morrel with Valentine and sailed away, leaving a letter ending “Wait and hope.”"}]},{"id":"louis-dantes","name":"Old Dantès","firstChapter":1,"importance":"supporting","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Louis Dantès","from":25,"note":"his full name"}],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Edmond's poor, proud old father."},{"from":13,"text":"Edmond's father, who died of grief while his son was in prison."},{"from":27,"text":"Edmond's father, who starved himself to death waiting for his son."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Edmond's poor, proud old father, whom Edmond hurries to visit before anyone else."},{"from":2,"text":"A frail old man who loves flowers and his son. He paid off Edmond's debt to Caderousse and nearly starved for three months rather than ask anyone for help."},{"from":5,"text":"Dressed in his best for the wedding, he pleads desperately with the officer who arrests Edmond and collapses at the news of the Bonapartist charge."},{"from":13,"text":"Sick with worry and losing all hope after Napoleon's final fall, he dies while his son is still in prison."},{"from":27,"text":"Caderousse tells how he refused to leave home in case Edmond returned, sold his belongings to live, and finally used a doctor's diet as an excuse to stop eating. Mercédès and M. Morrel were kind to him; he died blessing his son and cursing those who caused his misery."}],"moments":[{"chapter":2,"text":"Was found by Edmond with empty cupboards, having lived on sixty francs for three months."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Collapsed when his son was arrested at the wedding feast."},{"chapter":27,"text":"Died of hunger after nine days, blessing Edmond with his last words."}]},{"id":"marshal-bertrand","name":"Marshal Bertrand","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Napoleon's grand-marshal on Elba, who received Captain Leclere's packet from Dantès."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Napoleon's grand-marshal on Elba, to whom Dantès delivered the packet entrusted to him by the dying Captain Leclere."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"mercedes","name":"Mercédès","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Madame de Morcerf","from":27,"note":"Caderousse reveals she married Fernand, now the Count de Morcerf"},{"name":"Countess de Morcerf","from":27,"note":"her title as Fernand’s wife in Paris"},{"name":"Mercédès Herrera","from":91,"note":"Herrera was her father’s name"}],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Edmond Dantès’ devoted fiancée, a young Catalan girl from near Marseille."},{"from":9,"text":"Dantès’ fiancée, left grieving and alone after his arrest."},{"from":27,"text":"Dantès’ lost fiancée, now married to Fernand and a rich countess in Paris."},{"from":41,"text":"The Countess de Morcerf, Albert’s mother, strangely shaken by the Count of Monte Cristo."},{"from":89,"text":"Dantès’ first love, who knew him at once and begged him to spare her son."},{"from":91,"text":"Mercédès, who has left her husband’s house and fortune with her son Albert."},{"from":106,"text":"Mercédès, now poor, leaving Paris for the little house in Marseille that Edmond has left her."},{"from":112,"text":"Mercédès, living quietly in old Dantès’ house in Marseille, devoted to prayer."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Mercédès is Edmond Dantès’ fiancée, who lives in the Catalan village outside Marseille and has called on Morrel three times for news of his ship."},{"from":2,"text":"She is a beautiful seventeen-year-old with many admirers, often seen with a tall young cousin."},{"from":3,"text":"A poor orphan living on the village’s charity, she is honest and strong-willed. She loves her cousin Fernand only as a brother and has sworn that if Edmond dies, she will die too — a threat that keeps Fernand’s jealousy in check."},{"from":5,"text":"Radiant at the betrothal feast, she is heartbroken when Edmond is arrested and insists he is innocent."},{"from":9,"text":"Brave and dignified, she goes to Villefort herself to plead for Edmond, then spends the night numb with grief while Fernand watches over her."},{"from":13,"text":"Alone and desperate, she is kept from suicide only by her faith. She cares for Edmond’s father until he dies in her arms, and grows fonder of Fernand out of gratitude."},{"from":25,"text":"When Dantès returns to Marseille, she has vanished from the Catalan village."},{"from":27,"text":"Caderousse reveals that after eighteen months of waiting she married Fernand, in the very church where she was to have wed Edmond. She educated herself, has a son named Albert, and is now a rich countess in Paris, though Caderousse believes she is unhappy."},{"from":41,"text":"She is the Countess de Morcerf, mother of Albert. Her portrait shows her as a Catalan fisherwoman gazing at the sea, and she often weeps before it. Meeting the Count of Monte Cristo leaves her pale and shaken, and she questions Albert anxiously about him."},{"from":54,"text":"She quietly but firmly opposes Albert’s planned marriage to Eugénie Danglars, without explaining why, and keeps her distance from Madame Danglars."},{"from":68,"text":"The count fascinates and puzzles her; she talks of little else, imagines him a Cagliostro, and begs Albert to make sure he comes to her summer ball."},{"from":70,"text":"At her summer ball she watches him closely and notices that he will not eat or drink anything under her husband’s roof — and she understands it as deliberate."},{"from":71,"text":"She pleads with him to share food with her as a sign of friendship and asks searching questions about his past, but he refuses even a grape."},{"from":88,"text":"After her husband’s disgrace she is bedridden with shame. When Albert calls the count an enemy she turns pale and begs him to remain his friend, then has her son followed, fearing what he will do."},{"from":89,"text":"She recognized Edmond by his voice from the first moment. Coming to him secretly at night, she blames herself for marrying Fernand, admits she still loves him, and learns only now why he was arrested. She begs him to spare Albert’s life."},{"from":90,"text":"By telling Albert the family secret, she saves both her son and the count from the duel."},{"from":91,"text":"She leaves her husband’s house taking nothing, urges Albert to take her father’s name, Herrera, and accepts the small treasure Edmond buried for her as a dowry for a convent."},{"from":104,"text":"She and Albert give their whole fortune to the hospitals."},{"from":106,"text":"Living in a bare, cold room, she has lost her sparkle but not her dignity. She agrees to go to Marseille, to the little house where Edmond’s money waits for her."},{"from":112,"text":"Living in old Dantès’ house, she blames herself rather than Edmond: she was weak and married Fernand. She asks only for Albert’s happiness and means to spend her life in prayer."}],"moments":[{"chapter":3,"text":"Forced Fernand to shake Edmond’s hand by sheer force of will."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Saw Edmond arrested at their betrothal feast."},{"chapter":9,"text":"Went to Villefort herself to plead for Edmond."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Cared for old Dantès, who died in her arms."},{"chapter":27,"text":"Secretly dropped a purse of money from her window for the destitute Caderousse."},{"chapter":27,"text":"Married Fernand after eighteen months of waiting for Edmond."},{"chapter":41,"text":"Nearly fainted after meeting the Count, blaming the scent of the flowers."},{"chapter":70,"text":"Took the count’s arm and led the guests into the garden at her ball."},{"chapter":71,"text":"Flung the refused grapes into the bushes, crying “Inflexible man!”"},{"chapter":89,"text":"Came secretly at night and begged Edmond to spare Albert."},{"chapter":91,"text":"Left her husband’s house and accepted Edmond’s small treasure."},{"chapter":92,"text":"Left the house on her son’s arm without looking back."},{"chapter":106,"text":"Left Paris by coach for Marseille."},{"chapter":112,"text":"Told Edmond they would meet again in heaven."}]},{"id":"napoleon","name":"the Emperor","firstChapter":1,"importance":"supporting","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[{"name":"Napoleon Bonaparte","from":6,"note":"also called “the usurper” by royalists"}],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"The exiled emperor on Elba, whom Dantès met while carrying his captain's packet."},{"from":11,"text":"The exiled emperor, escaped from Elba and marching on Paris."},{"from":13,"text":"The emperor who returned for the Hundred Days, then fell for good at Waterloo."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Exiled on the island of Elba, he chatted with Dantès there and remembered Morrel's uncle. Morrel warns that any contact with him could be dangerous."},{"from":10,"text":"The royalists mock him as a ruined man, but Villefort fears his followers and warns the king that he is arming ships."},{"from":11,"text":"He left Elba on 26 February, landed near Antibes on 1 March and is marching on Paris."},{"from":13,"text":"He returns to power for the Hundred Days and then falls again after Waterloo, dashing the hopes of Dantès's father and supporters alike."},{"from":75,"text":"An old letter from Elba to the Bonapartist club announced his coming return, promising details on the arrival of Morrel's ship, the Pharaon."}],"moments":[{"chapter":11,"text":"Escaped from Elba and landed near Antibes on 1 March."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Returned for the Hundred Days and fell again at Waterloo."}]},{"id":"pierre-morrel","name":"M. Morrel","firstChapter":1,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[{"name":"Pierre Morrel","from":90,"note":"his full name, given in the Count's will"}],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Kind Marseille shipowner who employs Dantès and means to make him captain"},{"from":27,"text":"Dantès' loyal old employer, now close to ruin"},{"from":30,"text":"Shipowner saved from ruin and suicide by a mysterious benefactor"},{"from":50,"text":"The late father of Maximilian and Julie, remembered for his honesty"}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"M. Morrel owns the Marseille shipping firm Morrel & Son and its ship the Pharaon. He is kind, fair and fond of young Dantès, whom he plans to make captain. He seems to have a quiet sympathy for Napoleon, in whose army his uncle Policar served."},{"from":5,"text":"He honours Dantès' betrothal feast with his presence and, when Edmond is arrested, rushes to help. Convinced of his innocence, he goes to plead with the deputy prosecutor Villefort, and meanwhile gives Danglars temporary command of the Pharaon."},{"from":7,"text":"Villefort receives him coldly, treating him as a suspected Bonapartist. Morrel goes on to appeal to every influential friend he has, and is refused everywhere."},{"from":13,"text":"During Napoleon's brief return to power he petitions again and again for Dantès' release, never suspecting that Villefort is deceiving him. Cautious but loyal, he pays for old Dantès' funeral even though helping a 'Bonapartist' family is dangerous."},{"from":27,"text":"Years later, Caderousse describes how Morrel was persecuted as a Bonapartist for his petitions. Now he has lost five ships and is close to ruin, with only the Pharaon left to save him. He has a wife, a daughter, and a son who is an army lieutenant."},{"from":28,"text":"He has told his creditors he cannot pay unless the Pharaon arrives by the 15th. The prison files show that his 1815 petition for Dantès was later used by Villefort as proof that Dantès was a dangerous Bonapartist."},{"from":29,"text":"Aged by fourteen years of trouble, he is still scrupulously honest and proud that his firm has never failed to honour a signature. Then news comes that the Pharaon has sunk, leaving him 287,500 francs in debt and nothing to pay with; he thanks God that the crew survived."},{"from":30,"text":"Believing a suicide leaves an honest name where bankruptcy would not, and refused help by Danglars, he prepares to shoot himself. At the stroke of eleven he is saved by a purse sent by 'Sinbad the Sailor' holding his settled debt, and a brand-new Pharaon sails into port. The reader knows the mysterious Englishman and 'Sinbad' were Edmond Dantès repaying his old patron; Morrel does not."},{"from":50,"text":"He has since died, leaving his children comfortably off. He always insisted 'It was no Englishman that thus saved us', and his last words were 'Maximilian, it was Edmond Dantès!'"},{"from":51,"text":"In Marseille he is remembered as a deeply honest merchant and a devoted supporter of the emperor."},{"from":105,"text":"The Count finally tells Maximilian that he was the one who saved his father from suicide, ten years ago to the day, on 5 September."},{"from":113,"text":"He died in his children's arms and lies in Marseille beside his wife, who died two years before him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Told Dantès he intended to make him captain of the Pharaon."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Gave Danglars temporary command of the Pharaon after Dantès' arrest."},{"chapter":7,"text":"Pleaded with Villefort for Dantès and was coldly received."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Paid for old Dantès' funeral despite the risk of being seen to help a 'Bonapartist' family."},{"chapter":29,"text":"Learned the Pharaon had sunk and thanked God that the crew was saved."},{"chapter":30,"text":"Was saved at the stroke of eleven by Sinbad's purse and a new Pharaon."},{"chapter":50,"text":"Died saying, 'Maximilian, it was Edmond Dantès!'"}]},{"id":"gaspard-caderousse","name":"Caderousse","firstChapter":2,"importance":"major","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Gaspard Caderousse","from":26,"note":"his full name"},{"name":"M. Pailletin","from":81,"note":"the name he lives under in Paris as a ‘retired baker’"}],"tagline":[{"from":2,"text":"Envious tailor, neighbour of the Dantès family in Marseille."},{"from":4,"text":"Drunken witness to the plot against Dantès, too fearful to speak up."},{"from":26,"text":"Poor innkeeper near Beaucaire, rewarded by the abbé with a diamond."},{"from":45,"text":"Murderer of the jeweller Joannes, sent to the galleys for life."},{"from":64,"text":"Escaped convict blackmailing Andrea Cavalcanti, whom he knew as Benedetto."},{"from":83,"text":"Murdered by Benedetto; died repentant after learning the Count's true name."}],"description":[{"from":2,"text":"Caderousse is old Dantès' neighbour, a bearded tailor of about twenty-five. Friendly on the surface, he is envious and greedy underneath and resents Edmond's good fortune, and he runs straight to Danglars with news of Mercédès' Catalan ‘cousin’."},{"from":4,"text":"At La Réserve he drinks heavily and taunts Fernand. Drunk but not blind, he protests that Dantès is his friend and calls the denunciation an infamous shame, but he is easily steered by Danglars."},{"from":5,"text":"Sober, he remembers the letter and wants to tell the truth, but fear of being called an accomplice wins out. Unable either to act or to forget, he tries to drown his guilt in brandy."},{"from":26,"text":"Years later he has fallen on hard times and keeps a poor inn on the road between Bellegarde and Beaucaire with his sickly wife, La Carconte. Vain, envious and loud about his own honesty, he seems truly sorry about Dantès, but the sight of a diamond loosens his tongue."},{"from":27,"text":"He admits he was present, drunk, when the denunciation was written, and says remorse haunts him. He tells the abbé the whole story of the betrayal and how Danglars and Fernand rose, and receives the diamond as his reward."},{"from":45,"text":"He sells the diamond to a jeweller for 45,000 francs. Egged on by his wife, he stabs the jeweller to death in the night and flees with the money and the diamond. Caught abroad, he confesses and is sent to the galleys for life."},{"from":64,"text":"Now a ragged, wolfish figure, he has slipped away from the prison at Toulon and is hiding in Paris. He knows Andrea Cavalcanti's real name, Benedetto, and his past, and means to live off him."},{"from":81,"text":"Living on the Rue Ménilmontant as a ‘retired baker’ on Andrea's money, he turns out to be Andrea's old galley comrade, with whom he escaped from Toulon. Greedy and resentful, he extorts more, takes Andrea's diamond, and studies a plan of the Count's house."},{"from":82,"text":"He breaks into the Count's house and is caught by the Abbé Busoni, to whom he insists La Carconte was the jeweller's real murderer. When the abbé threatens to expose him, he tries to stab him. Climbing back over the wall, he is stabbed three times by an accomplice."},{"from":83,"text":"Dying, he names Benedetto as his murderer and signs a deposition. He denies God until the Count whispers his real name, Edmond Dantès; then he begs God's pardon and dies."},{"from":96,"text":"His missing waistcoat turns up with a bloodstained letter to Danglars in the pocket, which exposes Andrea at the contract signing."},{"from":108,"text":"His dying accusation is the key evidence against Benedetto."}],"moments":[{"chapter":2,"text":"Reported to Danglars about Mercédès' Catalan ‘cousin’."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Agreed to ‘wait and see’ rather than speak up for Dantès."},{"chapter":26,"text":"Told the abbé that old Dantès died of starvation."},{"chapter":27,"text":"Told the abbé who had denounced Dantès and received the diamond."},{"chapter":45,"text":"Murdered the jeweller Joannes and ran off with the diamond and money."},{"chapter":64,"text":"Ambushed Andrea outside the Count's house and called him ‘Benedetto’."},{"chapter":81,"text":"Extorted more money from Andrea and studied the plan of the Count's house."},{"chapter":82,"text":"Was stabbed three times as he climbed down from the Count's wall."},{"chapter":83,"text":"Named Benedetto as his murderer and died repenting."}]},{"id":"fernand-mondego","name":"Fernand","firstChapter":3,"importance":"major","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Count de Morcerf","from":27,"note":"the title Caderousse says Fernand now bears"},{"name":"General de Morcerf","from":41,"note":"a lieutenant-general and peer of France"},{"name":"Fernand Mondego","from":66,"note":"his full name, first given by Danglars"}],"tagline":[{"from":3,"text":"Mercédès’ tall, fierce Catalan cousin, jealous of her love for Dantès."},{"from":4,"text":"Mercédès’ jealous cousin, who carried off Danglars’ denunciation of Dantès."},{"from":17,"text":"Mercédès’ cousin, one of the men who sent Dantès to prison."},{"from":27,"text":"Dantès’ betrayer, now the Count de Morcerf, a rich general married to Mercédès."},{"from":41,"text":"The Count de Morcerf, Albert’s father: a general and peer of France."},{"from":78,"text":"The Count de Morcerf, publicly accused of betraying Ali Pasha at Yanina."},{"from":86,"text":"The Count de Morcerf, convicted of treason by the Chamber of Peers."},{"from":92,"text":"The disgraced Count de Morcerf, abandoned by his wife and son."}],"description":[{"from":3,"text":"Caderousse describes a strapping, fierce-looking young Catalan of about twenty-one who escorts Mercédès and seems to be courting her. Fernand is Mercédès’ cousin, a Catalan fisherman who has loved her for ten years. Jealous and quick to reach for his knife, he is held back only by her vow to die if Edmond is harmed."},{"from":4,"text":"He openly admits he hates Dantès and will do anything short of killing him. When Danglars writes an anonymous denunciation and throws it aside, Fernand secretly picks it up."},{"from":5,"text":"Pale and tense through the betrothal feast, he slips away just before the arrest and then returns to act as Mercédès’ protector."},{"from":13,"text":"He stays at Mercédès’ side through her grief, brooding over Dantès, until he is called up into Napoleon’s army. Her farewell gives him hope."},{"from":17,"text":"In prison, Dantès works out that Fernand was Danglars’ partner in the plot, driven by his love for Mercédès."},{"from":27,"text":"Caderousse reveals that Fernand posted the letter. Drafted in 1815, he deserted to the enemy with his general, rose under the restored king, and became a count after the Spanish war. As the Count de Morcerf he served Ali Pasha in Greece, came home rich, and is now a lieutenant-general living in Paris, married to Mercédès."},{"from":41,"text":"Albert’s father is a general and peer of France who looks older than his forty-odd years, with white hair and a careworn face. He dislikes his wife’s Catalan portrait, and is easily charmed by the Count of Monte Cristo’s flattery."},{"from":53,"text":"At the Opera he confirms he was inspector-general of Ali Pasha’s troops at Yanina and owes his fortune to the pasha’s generosity. He seems unsettled to hear that the Count’s companion is Greek — and she, seeing him, accuses him of selling her father to the Turks."},{"from":54,"text":"He is eager for Albert to marry Eugénie Danglars."},{"from":66,"text":"Danglars sneers that the count began life as a fisherman named Fernand Mondego, and mentions unpleasant rumours from Greece about Ali Pasha. Danglars writes to Yanina to find out more."},{"from":78,"text":"Proud and irritable, he formally asks for Eugénie’s hand for Albert, but Danglars stalls with hints of slander. A newspaper then claims that the castle of Yanina was betrayed to the Turks by a French officer named Fernand."},{"from":84,"text":"Beauchamp’s inquiries in Yanina prove that Colonel Fernand Mondego, raised by Ali to high command, surrendered the castle for two million crowns — and that he is the Count de Morcerf."},{"from":86,"text":"Named openly in the press, he defends himself before the Chamber of Peers, claiming he was negotiating for Ali when the pasha died. Haydée appears as a witness, accuses him of the false pardon, of stabbing Selim and of selling her and her mother as slaves, and produces the bill of sale. Speechless, he is convicted of treason."},{"from":89,"text":"The count tells Mercédès that Fernand posted the denunciation Danglars wrote, and lists his later betrayals: in Spain, against Ali, and against France itself."},{"from":92,"text":"Believing Albert has avenged him, then learning of the apology, he goes to fight Monte Cristo to the death. When the count reveals himself as Edmond Dantès, Fernand flees home, watches his wife and son leave him, and shoots himself."}],"moments":[{"chapter":3,"text":"Fled Mercédès’ house in despair and was drawn into drinking with Danglars and Caderousse."},{"chapter":4,"text":"Secretly picked up the crumpled denunciation and hurried off towards Marseille with it."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Vanished from the feast just before Dantès was arrested."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Was conscripted into Napoleon’s army."},{"chapter":27,"text":"Was revealed to have married Mercédès and become the Count de Morcerf."},{"chapter":41,"text":"Invited the Count of Monte Cristo to the Chamber of Peers."},{"chapter":53,"text":"Was denounced by Haydée at the Opera as the man who betrayed her father."},{"chapter":66,"text":"Became the target of Danglars’ inquiry to Yanina."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Had his proposal for Albert put off by Danglars."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Was accused in the newspaper of betraying Yanina."},{"chapter":85,"text":"Was named openly in the press as the traitor of Yanina."},{"chapter":86,"text":"Was convicted of felony and treason by a committee of the Chamber of Peers."},{"chapter":92,"text":"Recognized Monte Cristo as Edmond Dantès and fled."},{"chapter":92,"text":"Shot himself as Mercédès and Albert drove away."}]},{"id":"villefort","name":"M. de Villefort","firstChapter":5,"importance":"major","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[{"name":"Gérard de Villefort","from":7,"note":"his first name"},{"name":"the king's attorney (procureur du roi)","from":13,"note":"his title after promotion, first at Toulouse, later in Paris"}],"tagline":[{"from":5,"text":"The ambitious royalist deputy prosecutor handling Dantès' case in Marseilles."},{"from":7,"text":"Ambitious deputy prosecutor who buried an innocent Dantès in prison to protect his career."},{"from":13,"text":"Rising royalist magistrate, married to Renée, who kept Dantès locked away."},{"from":43,"text":"An 'upright' magistrate whom Bertuccio accuses of burying a child at Auteuil."},{"from":48,"text":"Cold, powerful king's attorney in Paris, Valentine's father and Noirtier's son."},{"from":67,"text":"Paris prosecutor haunted by his buried child and terrified Monte Cristo knows his secret."},{"from":73,"text":"King's attorney hushing up suspicious deaths in his own household."},{"from":108,"text":"Prosecutor who has unmasked his own wife as the household poisoner."},{"from":110,"text":"Disgraced prosecutor, exposed in open court as Benedetto's father."},{"from":111,"text":"Former prosecutor, driven mad by the loss of his wife and son."}],"description":[{"from":5,"text":"The deputy prosecutor in Marseilles who takes charge of Dantès' case. He is known as a fierce royalist and an ambitious young man."},{"from":6,"text":"Young, eloquent and proud of the death sentences he wins, Villefort has given up his father's name and politics to get ahead. He is engaged to Renée de Saint-Méran, daughter of a royalist family, and leaves his own betrothal feast to examine the prisoner."},{"from":7,"text":"Gérard de Villefort is twenty-seven, rich, and practises his serious face in the mirror. He sees that Dantès is innocent, but the letter Dantès carried is addressed to his own Bonapartist father, Noirtier. To save his career he burns it, makes Dantès promise silence, and quietly arranges for him to stay locked up."},{"from":9,"text":"Sensing a political storm, he rushes to Paris to win the king's gratitude. He coldly turns away Mercédès, and feels his first pang of remorse, but ambition wins."},{"from":11,"text":"He warns Louis XVIII of Napoleon's escape from Elba, hiding his father's role, and is rewarded with the king's own cross of the Legion of Honour. Then Noirtier himself turns up at his hotel; father and son, on opposite sides, protect each other."},{"from":13,"text":"He survives Napoleon's return thanks to his father's protection, holds back Morrel's petitions, and makes sure Dantès stays buried. After Waterloo he marries Renée and becomes king's attorney at Toulouse."},{"from":28,"text":"Dantès' prison file shows his handiwork: a note in Villefort's writing brands Dantès an inveterate Bonapartist to be kept in strict solitary confinement."},{"from":43,"text":"Years later, he has the reputation of the most upright of magistrates, having served as king's attorney at Nîmes and Versailles. But Bertuccio, recognizing the Auteuil garden, calls him a villain and says he once buried a child there."},{"from":44,"text":"Bertuccio's story: in 1815 Villefort coldly refused him justice for his murdered brother. Later, at his father-in-law's house in Auteuil, Villefort secretly visited a pregnant young woman known as 'the Baroness', and one night buried a newborn in a box in the garden. Bertuccio stabbed him there, then dug up the box and found the baby still alive."},{"from":48,"text":"Villefort survived the stabbing and is now an important king's attorney in Paris, having kept his post through four revolutions. Thin, sallow, cold and rigidly correct, he is feared rather than liked. He lives with his second wife Héloïse, his daughter Valentine, his son Edward, and his paralyzed father, whose condition he believes is God's punishment for some secret sin."},{"from":58,"text":"He is determined to marry Valentine to Franz d'Épinay, and treats his father's furious opposition as an old man's whim. He visits Noirtier rarely and coldly, and darkly hints that the old man knows something about the murder of Franz's father."},{"from":62,"text":"At the Count's dinner at Auteuil his hand trembles as he arrives, and Bertuccio recognizes him as the man he stabbed in that very garden. The pregnant woman of the story was Madame Danglars, and the baby Bertuccio saved grew up to be Benedetto, now passing as Andrea Cavalcanti."},{"from":67,"text":"Alone with Madame Danglars he tells his side: he believed the baby stillborn and buried it himself, was stabbed by a Corsican, and a year later found the grave empty. He traced the child to a foundling hospital and lost the trail. Convinced Monte Cristo knows his secret, he vows to find out who the Count really is."},{"from":69,"text":"Posing as an envoy of the prefect of police, he questions the Abbé Busoni and Lord Wilmore about the Count, without learning anything that alarms him."},{"from":73,"text":"When Madame de Saint-Méran dies and Dr. d'Avrigny suspects poison, Villefort's first concern is to avoid scandal and keep his enemies from triumphing. He persuades the doctor to say nothing, and rushes Valentine's marriage contract through on the day of the funeral."},{"from":75,"text":"The engagement collapses when Noirtier proves he killed Franz's father in a duel. Villefort had always believed the general was assassinated, and Franz's harsh letter wounds his pride."},{"from":80,"text":"After Barrois dies, d'Avrigny proves poison and accuses Valentine. Villefort admits a crime has been committed but begs on his knees not to give his daughter up to justice."},{"from":99,"text":"His household lives in terror, and he admits the deaths may not have been natural. He tells the Count that condemning criminals reassures him he is not uniquely wicked, and swears before a crucifix that the culprits in his house will die, whoever they are."},{"from":102,"text":"When Valentine is found dead, the 'iron heart' of Villefort is overwhelmed by real grief for the first time."},{"from":103,"text":"Noirtier tells him privately who the murderer is. Villefort begs that the family's honour be protected and swears to take terrible revenge within three days."},{"from":108,"text":"He has known since Madame de Saint-Méran's death that a poisoner lived in his house, and after Valentine's death he no longer doubted it was his wife. To save his name from the scaffold, he orders Héloïse to take her own poison before he comes back from court."},{"from":110,"text":"At Benedetto's trial, the accused names the king's attorney as his father. Villefort admits everything, says he is in the hands of an avenging God, and leaves the court a ruined man."},{"from":111,"text":"Humbled, he rushes home resolved to save his wife and flee with her, but finds her and Edward dead. When the Count reveals he is Edmond Dantès, Villefort loses his reason and begins digging in the garden for his lost child."}],"moments":[{"chapter":6,"text":"Left his own betrothal feast to examine the arrested Dantès."},{"chapter":7,"text":"Burned the letter to Noirtier and made Dantès swear silence, while arranging to keep him imprisoned."},{"chapter":9,"text":"Told Mercédès coldly that Dantès was a great criminal beyond help."},{"chapter":10,"text":"Warned Louis XVIII that Napoleon was about to leave Elba."},{"chapter":11,"text":"Received the king's own Legion of Honour cross, then an unexpected visit from his father."},{"chapter":12,"text":"Destroyed his father's disguise and hurried back to Marseilles."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Married Renée de Saint-Méran after the second Restoration."},{"chapter":44,"text":"Was stabbed by Bertuccio while burying a box holding a newborn baby at Auteuil."},{"chapter":48,"text":"Visited the Count to thank him for saving his wife and son, and left shaken by their conversation."},{"chapter":58,"text":"Announced Valentine's marriage to Franz d'Épinay to the furious Noirtier."},{"chapter":62,"text":"Arrived trembling at the Count's dinner in the house at Auteuil."},{"chapter":63,"text":"Whispered to Madame Danglars that he must see her next day in his office."},{"chapter":67,"text":"Vowed to find out within a week who the Count of Monte Cristo really is."},{"chapter":69,"text":"Disguised himself as a police envoy to question the Abbé Busoni and Lord Wilmore."},{"chapter":73,"text":"Persuaded Dr. d'Avrigny to keep his suspicion of poison secret."},{"chapter":74,"text":"Rushed Franz into signing the marriage contract straight from the cemetery."},{"chapter":75,"text":"Fled the room, tempted to smother his father, after Noirtier's revelation to Franz."},{"chapter":80,"text":"Refused to give Valentine up to justice."},{"chapter":99,"text":"Swore before a crucifix that the culprits in his house would die, whoever they were."},{"chapter":103,"text":"Swore to take terrible revenge for Valentine's murder within three days."},{"chapter":108,"text":"Accused his wife of four murders and ordered her to take her own poison."},{"chapter":110,"text":"Confessed in open court that he was Benedetto's father."},{"chapter":111,"text":"Found his wife and son dead, learned the Count was Dantès, and went mad, digging in his garden for his lost child."}]},{"id":"comte-de-salvieux","name":"the Comte de Salvieux","firstChapter":6,"importance":"minor","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"Saint-Méran family friend and chamberlain to the king's brother."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"An old friend of the Saint-Mérans and chamberlain to the king's brother, who reports that Louis XVIII has praised Villefort. His letter of recommendation gets Villefort his audience with the king."}],"moments":[{"chapter":9,"text":"Wrote the letter that got Villefort access to the king."}]},{"id":"louis-xviii","name":"King Louis XVIII","firstChapter":6,"importance":"supporting","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"The restored Bourbon king of France."},{"from":11,"text":"The witty Bourbon king, caught off guard by Napoleon's return from Elba."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"The restored Bourbon king, toasted by the royalists of Marseilles, who reportedly approves of Villefort's marriage."},{"from":10,"text":"A witty, scholarly king who annotates Horace and laughs off warnings. He knows who Villefort's father is but values the son's zeal."},{"from":11,"text":"Humiliated to learn of Napoleon's landing only by telegraph, he turns bitterly on his ministers and rewards Villefort with his own cross of the Legion of Honour."},{"from":13,"text":"He flees during the Hundred Days and is restored after Waterloo."}],"moments":[{"chapter":10,"text":"Received Villefort's warning that Napoleon was about to leave Elba."},{"chapter":11,"text":"Gave Villefort his own Legion of Honour cross."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Returned to the throne after Waterloo."}]},{"id":"marquis-de-saint-meran","name":"the Marquis de Saint-Méran","firstChapter":6,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"Renée's father, an old royalist nobleman."},{"from":51,"text":"Valentine's royalist grandfather, whose fortune she will inherit."},{"from":71,"text":"Valentine's grandfather, who died suddenly on the road to Paris."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"An old royalist nobleman, father of Villefort's fiancée Renée, who toasts Louis XVIII and wants the family's political quarrels forgotten."},{"from":9,"text":"On Villefort's advice he sells his bonds and arranges the letter that gets Villefort an audience with the king. After the Restoration he stands higher at court than ever."},{"from":44,"text":"He owned the house at Auteuil, which was said to have been rented to a young widow known as 'the Baroness'. He has since sold it, to the Count."},{"from":51,"text":"He is Valentine's grandfather on her mother's side, and she is the sole heiress of him and his wife."},{"from":71,"text":"Travelling to Paris to hasten Valentine's marriage, he took his usual lozenges, fell into an unnaturally deep sleep, cried out and died. A doctor called it apoplexy."},{"from":80,"text":"After two more deaths in the household, Dr. d'Avrigny becomes convinced from his symptoms that he too was poisoned."}],"moments":[{"chapter":9,"text":"Arranged the letter of introduction that got Villefort to the king."},{"chapter":71,"text":"Died on the road from Marseilles while coming to Paris for Valentine's wedding."}]},{"id":"marquise-de-saint-meran","name":"the Marquise de Saint-Méran","firstChapter":6,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"Renée's stern, fiercely royalist mother."},{"from":71,"text":"Valentine's proud grandmother, newly widowed, demanding the wedding at once."},{"from":73,"text":"Valentine's grandmother, who died suddenly days after her husband."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"Renée's stern, fiercely royalist mother. She keeps reminding Villefort of his father's revolutionary past and urges him to be merciless to conspirators."},{"from":51,"text":"She is Valentine's grandmother, and Valentine expects to inherit from her."},{"from":72,"text":"She arrives in Paris with the news of her husband's death. Proud and contemptuous of anyone not noble, she adores Valentine, resents Villefort's remarriage, and, feverish and sure she will die soon, demands the wedding to Franz at once. She describes a white figure that entered her room at night and touched her glass."},{"from":73,"text":"She dies after three violent convulsive attacks, her last wish being that Valentine's marriage not be delayed. Dr. d'Avrigny privately tells Villefort she was poisoned, but agrees to keep quiet."},{"from":74,"text":"She was sixty-six. Her will leaves the whole Saint-Méran fortune to Valentine, and society blames her death on grief."}],"moments":[{"chapter":72,"text":"Described a white figure touching her glass at night, then drank her orangeade and demanded a notary."},{"chapter":73,"text":"Died after three violent convulsive attacks."}]},{"id":"noirtier","name":"Noirtier","firstChapter":6,"importance":"major","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[{"name":"M. Noirtier de Villefort","from":48,"note":"his full name"},{"name":"Grandpapa Noirtier","from":52,"note":"what the family calls him"}],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"Villefort's father: a former revolutionary and Bonapartist senator."},{"from":7,"text":"Villefort's Bonapartist father, to whom Dantès' Elba letter was addressed."},{"from":12,"text":"Cool, fearless Bonapartist conspirator who shields his son and slips past the police."},{"from":48,"text":"Paralyzed, speechless old conspirator who speaks only with his eyes."},{"from":59,"text":"Paralyzed patriarch who disinherits Valentine to stop her marrying Franz d'Épinay."},{"from":75,"text":"Paralyzed old Bonapartist who killed Franz's father in a duel in 1815."},{"from":94,"text":"Valentine's silent protector, who has been arming her against poison."},{"from":117,"text":"Valentine's grandfather, waiting at Leghorn to bless her marriage."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"Villefort's father, mentioned at the royalist betrothal feast as a former Girondin revolutionary who became a senator under Napoleon and is perhaps still a Bonapartist. His son has dropped his name to escape his politics."},{"from":7,"text":"The letter Dantès carried from Elba was addressed to M. Noirtier, Rue Coq-Héron, Paris. That is why Villefort destroys it: the link to his father could ruin him."},{"from":11,"text":"He matches the police description of the man who lured the royalist General Quesnel away before his body was found in the Seine. Without warning, he walks into his son's hotel room calling him 'my dear Gérard'."},{"from":12,"text":"Witty, well informed and utterly fearless, he leads the Bonapartist club of the Rue Saint-Jacques and believes that in politics there are no murders, only obstacles removed. He shaves off his whiskers, puts on his son's clothes, and strolls past the police."},{"from":13,"text":"Powerful at Napoleon's court during the Hundred Days, he protects his son's position in return."},{"from":48,"text":"Years later he is paralyzed by a stroke and unable to speak, entirely dependent on his granddaughter Valentine. He was once a fiery Jacobin, senator and conspirator famous across Europe."},{"from":51,"text":"His mind is fully alert. He speaks with his eyes, and only Valentine can read them. He is the one person at home who truly loves her."},{"from":58,"text":"He signals yes by closing his eyes and no by blinking, and his will is as strong as ever. He detests the plan to marry Valentine to Franz d'Épinay; there was once a bitter political hatred between him and Franz's father, and Villefort hints that Noirtier knows something about that man's murder."},{"from":59,"text":"With Valentine as interpreter, he dictates a will leaving his fortune of 900,000 francs to the poor if she marries Franz. He refuses, with something like hatred, to leave anything to his son or grandson Edward."},{"from":73,"text":"He approves of Maximilian Morrel, but forbids an elopement or a duel, insisting that help will come from him alone."},{"from":75,"text":"He stops the contract by showing Franz a signed record of 5 February 1815: as president of the Bonapartist club, Noirtier killed Franz's father, General d'Épinay, in a fair duel, and was wounded three times doing it. When Franz asks who killed his father, Noirtier spells out 'myself'."},{"from":78,"text":"At Madame de Villefort's request, he makes a new will leaving everything to Valentine, on condition she never leaves him."},{"from":80,"text":"His servant Barrois dies after drinking Noirtier's lemonade. D'Avrigny believes the poison was meant for Noirtier himself, who survived only because a year of brucine treatment had accustomed his body to it."},{"from":94,"text":"He has been quietly protecting Valentine by dosing her with his own medicine, building up her resistance. When she collapses, he confirms to d'Avrigny that the hand that killed Barrois struck her too."},{"from":100,"text":"The Count believes Noirtier knows, or at least suspects, who the poisoner is."},{"from":103,"text":"After Valentine's death, he tells Villefort privately who the murderer is, and agrees to keep it secret because his son has sworn revenge."},{"from":104,"text":"After a night in which the Abbé Busoni watches over Valentine's body, Noirtier sleeps calmly, almost smiling, to everyone's surprise. He stays calm and resigned afterwards, silently reminding his son of his oath."},{"from":117,"text":"He is waiting at Leghorn to bless Valentine before her marriage to Morrel."}],"moments":[{"chapter":11,"text":"Walked into Villefort's hotel room calling him 'my dear Gérard'."},{"chapter":12,"text":"Shaved his whiskers, put on his son's clothes and strolled past the police."},{"chapter":58,"text":"Spelled out 'notary' with Valentine's help, determined to act against the marriage."},{"chapter":59,"text":"Dictated a will leaving his fortune to the poor if Valentine marries Franz."},{"chapter":73,"text":"Promised Maximilian the contract would not be signed, and made him swear to wait."},{"chapter":74,"text":"Summoned Franz d'Épinay in the middle of the contract signing."},{"chapter":75,"text":"Spelled out 'myself' when Franz asked who killed his father."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Made Valentine his sole heir."},{"chapter":94,"text":"Revealed that he had been building up Valentine's resistance to poison."},{"chapter":102,"text":"Made Morrel understand with his eyes that something had happened to Valentine."},{"chapter":103,"text":"Revealed the culprit's name to Villefort in private."}]},{"id":"renee-de-saint-meran","name":"Renée de Saint-Méran","firstChapter":6,"importance":"supporting","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"Villefort's gentle young fiancée, daughter of the royalist Saint-Mérans."},{"from":13,"text":"Villefort's bride, married after Napoleon's final defeat."},{"from":48,"text":"Villefort's late first wife and Valentine's mother."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"Villefort's light-haired, tender-hearted fiancée. Frightened by talk of executions, she begs him to show mercy to the prisoner."},{"from":9,"text":"Upset when Villefort suddenly leaves for Paris, she comes to resent the prisoner who took him away, and does not plead for him again."},{"from":13,"text":"Her wedding is postponed during the Hundred Days, then takes place after the king's second return; Villefort soon leaves Marseilles with her."},{"from":48,"text":"She is now dead. She was Villefort's first wife and Valentine's mother, which is why Valentine is rich through her."},{"from":72,"text":"Her mother reproaches Villefort for forgetting her so soon by remarrying."},{"from":105,"text":"It was her last wish that the family vault at Père-Lachaise bear the names of both the Saint-Mérans and the Villeforts."}],"moments":[{"chapter":6,"text":"Begged Villefort to show mercy to the arrested Dantès."},{"chapter":13,"text":"Married Villefort after Waterloo."}]},{"id":"dandre","name":"M. Dandré","firstChapter":10,"importance":"minor","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":10,"text":"Louis XVIII's overconfident minister of police."}],"description":[{"from":10,"text":"The minister of police, who confidently reports that Napoleon is bored and perhaps going mad on Elba. Disgraced when Napoleon lands, he is grateful to Villefort for defending him."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"duc-de-blacas","name":"the Duc de Blacas","firstChapter":10,"importance":"minor","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":10,"text":"Louis XVIII's devoted, anxious adviser, who brings Villefort to the king."}],"description":[{"from":10,"text":"The king's devoted and anxious adviser, who senses a Bonapartist plot and brings Villefort to the king. He is proved right, and the king praises him for persisting."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"general-quesnel","name":"General Quesnel","firstChapter":11,"importance":"minor","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[{"name":"General d'Épinay","from":60,"note":"the Count identifies Franz d'Épinay as the son of General de Quesnel, created Baron d'Épinay"},{"name":"General Flavien de Quesnel, Baron d'Épinay","from":75,"note":"his full name, in Noirtier's account of his death"}],"tagline":[{"from":11,"text":"A royalist general who vanished after visiting a Bonapartist club."},{"from":60,"text":"Royalist general, Franz d'Épinay's father, killed after a Bonapartist meeting in 1815."},{"from":75,"text":"Franz's father, killed by Noirtier in a duel in 1815."}],"description":[{"from":11,"text":"A general loyal to the king who disappeared after visiting a Bonapartist club in the Rue Saint-Jacques and was found in the Seine. The police believe he was murdered."},{"from":12,"text":"Noirtier tells his son the general heard the club's plans, declared himself a royalist, reluctantly swore an oath, and 'lost his way' on leaving. His words strongly suggest the general was killed."},{"from":60,"text":"He was Franz d'Épinay's father, later known as General d'Épinay (see that entry)."}],"moments":[],"mergedInto":{"id":"general-depinay","from":60}},{"id":"abbe-faria","name":"the Abbé Faria","firstChapter":14,"importance":"major","group":"Prisoners & jailers","aliases":[{"name":"No. 27","from":14,"note":"his prisoner number in the Château d'If"},{"name":"the mad priest","from":14,"note":"what the jailers call him"}],"tagline":[{"from":14,"text":"Italian priest in the Château d'If, thought mad for offering a treasure for his freedom."},{"from":17,"text":"Dantès's fellow prisoner, friend and teacher, who uncovers the plot against him."},{"from":18,"text":"Dantès's teacher, who reveals that the Spada treasure is real and makes him his heir."},{"from":19,"text":"Dantès's beloved teacher, who died in the Château d'If and left him the Spada treasure."}],"description":[{"from":14,"text":"An Italian priest born in Rome, for twenty years secretary to Cardinal Spada, arrested at Piombino in 1811 for dreaming of a united Italy. Everyone thinks he is mad because he keeps offering a huge treasure in exchange for his freedom."},{"from":15,"text":"Cautious and patient, he has dug a long tunnel hoping to reach the sea, but miscalculated and broke into Dantès's cell instead. He says he is alone in the world."},{"from":16,"text":"A small, agile man of about sixty: a scholar, chemist and linguist who refuses to shed blood. In prison he has made his own tools, pens, ink and linen 'paper', and written a book on Italian monarchy."},{"from":17,"text":"A brilliant reasoner, he works out who betrayed Dantès and why, and becomes his patient teacher in languages, science and the ways of the world. He suffers from an inherited catalepsy that killed his father and grandfather on a third attack; a second attack leaves him partly paralysed."},{"from":18,"text":"He was never mad: as secretary to the last Count of Spada he inherited the family papers and, in 1807, found Cardinal Spada's hidden letter revealing a treasure on Monte Cristo. He makes Dantès his heir, calling him 'the child of my captivity'."},{"from":20,"text":"He spends his last months making Dantès memorise the treasure letter and dies selflessly, refusing to let him call for help. His homemade knife, needle and thread make Dantès's escape possible."},{"from":27,"text":"Everything he taught proves right: his clues lead Dantès to the treasure, and everything he deduced about Dantès's betrayers is confirmed."}],"moments":[{"chapter":14,"text":"Offered the inspector six million francs for his liberty and was laughed at."},{"chapter":15,"text":"Broke through the floor into Dantès's cell."},{"chapter":16,"text":"Showed Dantès the chisel with which he had dug fifty feet through rock."},{"chapter":17,"text":"Revealed that Villefort's father was Noirtier, then survived a second attack that paralysed his right side."},{"chapter":18,"text":"Made Dantès heir to the Spada treasure."},{"chapter":19,"text":"Died after his third attack, his last words: “Monte Cristo, forget not Monte Cristo!”"}]},{"id":"cardinal-spada","name":"Cardinal Spada","firstChapter":18,"importance":"minor","group":"History & legend","aliases":[{"name":"Cæsar Spada","from":18,"note":"his full name"}],"tagline":[{"from":18,"text":"Renaissance cardinal poisoned by the Borgias, who hid his fortune on Monte Cristo."}],"description":[{"from":18,"text":"A rich Roman noble made a cardinal in 1498, then poisoned at dinner by Pope Alexander VI and Cæsar Borgia. He had foreseen the trap and hid his fortune in the caves of Monte Cristo for his nephew Guido, leaving a secret letter in invisible ink."},{"from":24,"text":"He hid the treasure with great cunning: marks carved in the rocks as a guide, a boulder slid into place, a false wall painted like granite, and a buried chest bearing the Spada arms."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"cesare-borgia","name":"Cæsar Borgia","firstChapter":18,"importance":"minor","group":"History & legend","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":18,"text":"Son of Pope Alexander VI, who poisoned Cardinal Spada for his wealth."}],"description":[{"from":18,"text":"Son of Pope Alexander VI. He and his father poisoned rich cardinals to seize their wealth, but they never found Cardinal Spada's fortune."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"count-of-spada","name":"the Count of Spada","firstChapter":18,"importance":"minor","group":"History & legend","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":18,"text":"The last of the Spada family, Faria's patron, who left him the family papers."}],"description":[{"from":18,"text":"The last of the Spada family and Faria's patron. Poor despite the family legend of hidden wealth, he left Faria his library and the famous breviary in which the treasure letter was hidden."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"captain-baldi","name":"the patron of the tartan","firstChapter":21,"importance":"minor","group":"Sailors & smugglers","aliases":[{"name":"Captain Baldi","from":23,"note":"his name"}],"tagline":[{"from":21,"text":"Cautious old master of the Genoese smugglers' tartan that takes on the escaped Dantès."}],"description":[{"from":21,"text":"The cautious old pilot and master of the Genoese tartan bound for Leghorn. He takes Dantès on after he proves his seamanship."},{"from":23,"text":"Master of La Jeune Amélie, named as Captain Baldi: a shrewd smuggler well known to the coast guards and fluent in many languages. He trusts Dantès, sees him as his natural successor and delays sailing out of concern for him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":22,"text":"Proposed Monte Cristo as the meeting place for a smuggling exchange."}]},{"id":"jacopo","name":"Jacopo","firstChapter":21,"importance":"supporting","group":"Sailors & smugglers","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":21,"text":"Good-hearted sailor who pulled the escaped Dantès out of the sea."},{"from":25,"text":"Loyal Corsican sailor, rewarded with a boat, who serves Dantès as his messenger."}],"description":[{"from":21,"text":"A frank, good-hearted sailor on the Genoese smugglers' tartan who rescues Dantès from the sea and urges that he be paid fairly."},{"from":22,"text":"A Corsican devoted to Edmond: he nurses him when he is wounded, indignantly refuses payment, and learns navigation and astronomy from him."},{"from":23,"text":"Loyal as ever, he offers to give up his share of the profits to stay and nurse Dantès on Monte Cristo."},{"from":25,"text":"Rewarded with a boat of his own, he believes the story of Dantès's inheritance and serves him loyally as a messenger."}],"moments":[{"chapter":21,"text":"Pulled the drowning Dantès out of the sea by his hair."},{"chapter":22,"text":"Began learning navigation and astronomy from Dantès."},{"chapter":25,"text":"Brought back the news that old Dantès was dead and Mercédès had disappeared."},{"chapter":117,"text":"Delivered the Count's farewell letter and pointed out his departing sail."}]},{"id":"carconte","name":"La Carconte","firstChapter":26,"importance":"minor","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Madeleine Radelle","from":26,"note":"her real name"}],"tagline":[{"from":26,"text":"Caderousse's bitter, fever-wasted wife."},{"from":45,"text":"Caderousse's wife, who plotted the jeweller's murder and was shot dead."}],"description":[{"from":26,"text":"La Carconte is Caderousse's wife, born near Arles and once beautiful, now wasted by fever. Bitter and suspicious, she urges her husband to keep quiet because Fernand and Danglars are powerful enough to crush them, and doubts that the abbé's diamond is real."},{"from":45,"text":"Greedy as well as feverish, she pushes Caderousse to sell the diamond, then plans the murder of the jeweller. She is shot dead on the stairs in the struggle; Caderousse later insists she was the real killer."}],"moments":[{"chapter":45,"text":"Was killed by the jeweller's pistol shot."}]},{"id":"albert-de-morcerf","name":"Albert","firstChapter":27,"importance":"major","group":"The Morcerf household","aliases":[{"name":"Albert de Morcerf","from":31,"note":"the Viscount Albert de Morcerf"},{"name":"Viscount de Morcerf","from":31,"note":"his title"},{"name":"Albert Herrera","from":91,"note":"he plans to take his mother’s family name"}],"tagline":[{"from":27,"text":"The young son of Mercédès and Fernand."},{"from":31,"text":"A dashing young Paris viscount on his way to the Roman Carnival."},{"from":37,"text":"Paris viscount rescued from Roman bandits by the Count of Monte Cristo."},{"from":41,"text":"Son of Mercédès and the Count de Morcerf; the Count’s first friend in Paris."},{"from":78,"text":"Fernand’s son, fiercely defending his father’s honour against a newspaper slur."},{"from":90,"text":"Fernand’s son, who learned the truth and apologized to Monte Cristo."},{"from":106,"text":"Albert, who gave up his name and fortune to serve as a cavalryman in Africa."}],"description":[{"from":27,"text":"Caderousse mentions little Albert, the son of Mercédès and Fernand, whom Mercédès was educating herself during the Spanish war."},{"from":31,"text":"The Viscount Albert de Morcerf is a young man of Paris high society travelling to Rome for the Carnival with his friend Franz. Morcerf is the title Caderousse said Fernand now holds — and Mercédès’ son was named Albert."},{"from":33,"text":"Breezy and self-assured, he believes a full purse can do anything, and he is brave to the point of recklessness, laughing off the bandit Vampa as a myth."},{"from":34,"text":"He is vain about his clothes and stung by his lack of romantic success in Italy, but also talented and well off, with a comfortable income. He knows some Greek."},{"from":37,"text":"Kidnapped by Vampa’s bandits, he shows remarkable coolness, sleeping soundly under threat of death. Freed by the Count of Monte Cristo, he feels eternally in his debt."},{"from":38,"text":"Grateful and carefree, he offers the Count his family’s influence in Paris and brushes off all of Franz’s suspicions. His father, the Count de Morcerf, has called him home to arrange a marriage."},{"from":39,"text":"He lives a pampered life in a pavilion in his parents’ courtyard, full of half-abandoned hobbies and a dandy’s pursuits."},{"from":40,"text":"Paris gossip links him to Eugénie Danglars, daughter of the newly made Baron Danglars, a match his father is most anxious for."},{"from":41,"text":"His mother is the Countess de Morcerf — Mercédès. He believes his parents have been happily married for over twenty years, their only disagreement being her portrait."},{"from":54,"text":"Not yet twenty-one, he does not want to marry Eugénie. Torn between his father’s wishes and his mother’s unhappiness, he would rather displease his father, and has stalled the marriage talks."},{"from":68,"text":"He adores his mother — what he really wants is a wife like her — and dreads marrying Eugénie."},{"from":70,"text":"At his parents’ ball he is a witty guide to the guests, and presses the count to eat at his mother’s request without understanding why it matters to her."},{"from":77,"text":"He knows his father made his fortune serving Ali Pasha. Visiting the count, he is enchanted by Haydée and hears her story of her father’s betrayal."},{"from":78,"text":"When a newspaper claims Yanina was betrayed by an officer named Fernand, he furiously takes it as a slander on his father and challenges Beauchamp, the editor, to a duel."},{"from":84,"text":"Beauchamp returns from Yanina with proof that his father really did betray the castle. Albert burns the proofs, grieves for his father and fears for his mother. His engagement to Eugénie is broken off."},{"from":87,"text":"Wanting a living enemy to fight, he works out that Monte Cristo planned every step of his father’s exposure."},{"from":88,"text":"Suspecting his mother of special reasons for defending the count, he insults Monte Cristo publicly at the Opera, leading to a duel."},{"from":90,"text":"After a sleepless night he apologizes on the dueling ground, saying the count had the right to avenge himself — not for Ali’s sake, but for the fisherman Fernand’s treachery toward him."},{"from":91,"text":"His friends receive the apology coldly. He gives up his luxuries, his father’s name and his fortune, and leaves home with his mother, planning to earn his own living."},{"from":106,"text":"He has sold his watch and enlisted in the Spahis, a cavalry regiment in Africa, to make a name of his own and support his mother."},{"from":112,"text":"Now in uniform, he sails from Marseille to Algiers."}],"moments":[{"chapter":31,"text":"Set off with Franz for the Carnival in Rome."},{"chapter":36,"text":"Disappeared with a masked peasant woman on the last night of the Carnival."},{"chapter":37,"text":"Was kidnapped by Vampa’s band and freed by the Count."},{"chapter":38,"text":"Agreed to receive the Count in Paris on 21 May at 10:30 a.m."},{"chapter":40,"text":"Introduced the Count to his friends at breakfast in Paris."},{"chapter":54,"text":"Arranged to take his mother to Tréport during the Count’s Auteuil dinner."},{"chapter":68,"text":"Invited the count to his parents’ summer ball on his mother’s behalf."},{"chapter":77,"text":"Met Haydée and heard the story of her father’s death."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Challenged Beauchamp to a duel, set for three weeks later."},{"chapter":84,"text":"Burned the proofs of his father’s treason."},{"chapter":85,"text":"Learned his father had been publicly named and galloped back to Paris."},{"chapter":87,"text":"Resolved to demand an explanation from Monte Cristo."},{"chapter":88,"text":"Publicly insulted Monte Cristo at the Opera."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Apologized to the count on the dueling ground."},{"chapter":91,"text":"Left his father’s house with his mother."},{"chapter":106,"text":"Enlisted in the Spahis and settled the money on his mother."},{"chapter":112,"text":"Sailed for Africa."}]},{"id":"ali-tebelin","name":"Ali Pasha","firstChapter":27,"importance":"supporting","group":"Greece & Yanina","aliases":[{"name":"Ali Tepelini","from":53,"note":"his full name"},{"name":"Pasha of Yanina","from":53,"note":"his title"}],"tagline":[{"from":27,"text":"The Greek-region ruler whom Fernand served, and who left him a fortune."},{"from":53,"text":"The late pasha of Yanina, whom Morcerf served — apparently Haydée’s father."},{"from":77,"text":"Haydée’s father, the Pasha of Yanina, betrayed by a French officer and killed."}],"description":[{"from":27,"text":"Caderousse says Fernand served Ali Pasha in Greece as instructor-general, and that before Ali was killed he rewarded Fernand with a large sum of money."},{"from":53,"text":"Ali Tepelini, the Albanian chief of Yanina, had Morcerf as inspector-general of his troops, and Morcerf admits he owes his fortune to him. Haydée’s furious outburst suggests Ali was her father."},{"from":77,"text":"Haydée reveals he was her father. Waiting for the sultan’s verdict, he retreated to a fortified kiosk on a lake, with his gold and gunpowder guarded by the faithful Selim, and sent a French officer he trusted to negotiate. A false pardon tricked the guard into putting out the match, and Ali was killed by the sultan’s troops."},{"from":86,"text":"Before the Chamber of Peers, Morcerf produces Ali’s letters and ring, but Haydée declares that Morcerf himself brought back the false pardon."}],"moments":[{"chapter":77,"text":"Was betrayed by the false pardon and killed by the sultan’s soldiers."}]},{"id":"m-de-boville","name":"M. de Boville","firstChapter":28,"importance":"supporting","group":"Prisoners & jailers","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":28,"text":"Inspector of prisons, desperate over money owed him by Morrel."},{"from":69,"text":"Former inspector of prisons, now high in the police."},{"from":104,"text":"Former prison inspector, now collecting funds for the Paris hospitals."}],"description":[{"from":28,"text":"Inspector of prisons for fourteen years, he had invested his daughter's dowry of two hundred thousand francs with Morrel and was in despair. He believes the prisoner Dantès drowned while escaping and finds the story funny. He sells his doubtful claim at full value to the Englishman from Thomson & French and lets him search the prison registers."},{"from":69,"text":"Now risen to a high post in the police, he handles Villefort's request for information about the Count of Monte Cristo."},{"from":104,"text":"Now Receiver-General of the charities, he comes to Danglars for five million francs owed to the hospitals. He does not recognise the Count, who bows to him as if they had met before."}],"moments":[{"chapter":28,"text":"Sold his claim on Morrel at full value and let the Englishman search the prison registers."},{"chapter":104,"text":"Agreed to return the next day at noon for the hospitals' five million from Danglars."}]},{"id":"captain-gaumard","name":"Captain Gaumard","firstChapter":29,"importance":"minor","group":"Sailors & smugglers","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":29,"text":"Brave captain of Morrel's Pharaon"}],"description":[{"from":29,"text":"The Pharaon's captain fought the storm bravely, threatening to shoot any man who left the pumps, and had to be thrown into the lifeboat by Penelon. He stayed behind sick at Palma."},{"from":30,"text":"Recovered, he returns from Palma and brings the new Pharaon into port."}],"moments":[{"chapter":30,"text":"Brought the new Pharaon into Marseille harbour."}]},{"id":"cocles","name":"Cocles","firstChapter":29,"importance":"minor","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":29,"text":"Morrel's devoted one-eyed cashier"},{"from":50,"text":"The Morrels' faithful one-eyed old doorkeeper"}],"description":[{"from":29,"text":"The firm's one-eyed cashier, nicknamed 'Cocles' (cock-eye). Utterly devoted, he is sure the firm can never stop paying because in twenty years it never has, and is crushed when Morrel shows him the true accounts."},{"from":50,"text":"He now keeps the door at the Morrels' house in Paris, and does not recognize the Count."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"emmanuel-herbaut","name":"Emmanuel","firstChapter":29,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[{"name":"Emmanuel Herbaut","from":40,"note":"his surname"}],"tagline":[{"from":29,"text":"Loyal young clerk who stays with Morrel's failing firm for love of Julie"},{"from":40,"text":"Julie Morrel's devoted husband"}],"description":[{"from":29,"text":"A young clerk of twenty-three or twenty-four who stays with Morrel's failing firm because he loves Morrel's daughter Julie. She trusts him above everyone; he escorts her on her errand and brings the news of the new Pharaon."},{"from":40,"text":"Now about thirty, he is Julie's husband, honoured for having stayed loyal to the Morrels when their fortunes fell."},{"from":50,"text":"He ran the family firm until he had earned 250,000 francs to match his wife's inheritance, then closed the business rather than sell the Morrel name. He finds the Count a strange man."},{"from":88,"text":"At Maximilian's request he agrees to serve as the Count's second witness at the meeting with Albert de Morcerf."},{"from":92,"text":"When the affair ends peacefully he is overjoyed and hurries home to Julie."},{"from":105,"text":"When the Count reveals himself as the family's benefactor, Emmanuel gently reproaches him for hiding so long. The Count asks him to watch over Maximilian."},{"from":112,"text":"He believes the falls of Morcerf, Danglars and Villefort were directed by Providence, and calls the Count an angel."}],"moments":[{"chapter":30,"text":"Brought the news that the new Pharaon was entering port."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Served as the Count's second witness at the meeting with Albert."},{"chapter":105,"text":"Gently reproached the Count for hiding himself so long."}]},{"id":"julie-morrel","name":"Julie Morrel","firstChapter":29,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[{"name":"Madame Herbaut","from":40,"note":"her married name as Emmanuel Herbaut's wife"}],"tagline":[{"from":29,"text":"Morrel's devoted young daughter"},{"from":30,"text":"Morrel's daughter, who fetched the purse that saved her father"},{"from":40,"text":"Maximilian's happily married sister, wife of Emmanuel Herbaut"}],"description":[{"from":29,"text":"Morrel's beautiful daughter, sixteen or seventeen and devoted to her father. She blushes when a stranger from Thomson & French hints that she will marry the clerk Emmanuel, and she promises to obey a letter signed 'Sinbad the Sailor'."},{"from":30,"text":"Trusting the letter despite her fears, she fetches a red silk purse from the Allées de Meilhan and reaches her father just in time to save him."},{"from":40,"text":"Nearly nine years on she is twenty-five, married to Emmanuel Herbaut and, as her brother says, 'as happy as it is permitted to a human creature to be'. She lives in the Rue Meslay in Paris."},{"from":50,"text":"She longs to thank the family's unknown benefactor and keeps his purse, letter and diamond as treasured relics, which she shows the visiting Count. She feels she has heard his voice before."},{"from":105,"text":"When she learns that the Count is the benefactor, she brings out the purse from under its crystal globe to honour him and refuses to give it back."},{"from":112,"text":"Sensing her brother is hiding something, she begs the Count to restore his happiness. She still trusts 'Sinbad the Sailor'."}],"moments":[{"chapter":29,"text":"Swore to obey a letter signed 'Sinbad the Sailor'."},{"chapter":30,"text":"Fetched the red silk purse from the Allées de Meilhan and reached her father just in time."},{"chapter":50,"text":"Showed the Count the purse and the letter signed 'Sinbad the Sailor'."},{"chapter":105,"text":"Brought out the silk purse from under its crystal globe to honour the Count."},{"chapter":112,"text":"Begged the Count to bring back her brother's happiness."}]},{"id":"madame-morrel","name":"Madame Morrel","firstChapter":29,"importance":"minor","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":29,"text":"Morrel's faithful wife, mother of Julie and Maximilian"}],"description":[{"from":29,"text":"Morrel's wife, who weeps at the news of the wreck but stays at her husband's side, and fears, watching through the keyhole, that he is writing his will."},{"from":113,"text":"She died two years before her husband and lies beside him in Marseille."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"penelon","name":"Penelon","firstChapter":29,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":29,"text":"Tobacco-chewing old sailor of the Pharaon, loyal to the Morrels."},{"from":50,"text":"Old quartermaster of the Pharaon, now the Morrel family's gardener."}],"description":[{"from":29,"text":"A tobacco-chewing old sailor who survived the wreck of the Pharaon. He and his shipmates would rather wait for their wages than leave Morrel."},{"from":30,"text":"Newly dressed and embarrassed, he seems to have found a new master, and returns aboard the new Pharaon."},{"from":50,"text":"The old quartermaster is now the family gardener and still calls Julie 'Mademoiselle'. Four years ago in Trieste he saw the 'Englishman' of 1829 boarding a yacht."}],"moments":[{"chapter":29,"text":"Described the sinking of the Pharaon and offered to work unpaid."}]},{"id":"maximilien-morrel","name":"Maximilian Morrel","firstChapter":30,"importance":"major","group":"The Morrel family","aliases":[{"name":"Captain Morrel of the Spahis","from":40,"note":"his rank as a cavalry officer in Algeria"}],"tagline":[{"from":30,"text":"Morrel's dutiful son, a young army officer nicknamed 'the stoic'"},{"from":40,"text":"Brave cavalry captain, still seeking the stranger who saved his family"},{"from":51,"text":"Spahi captain secretly in love with Valentine de Villefort"},{"from":73,"text":"Valentine's desperate secret suitor, who has overheard talk of poison"},{"from":102,"text":"Heartbroken over Valentine's death, kept alive only by his promise to the Count"},{"from":117,"text":"Reunited with Valentine on the island of Monte Cristo"}],"description":[{"from":30,"text":"Morrel's son, about twenty-two, trained at the Polytechnic School and now a sub-lieutenant in the 53rd infantry. His strict sense of duty has earned him the nickname 'the stoic', and he has great influence over his father. Told of the family's ruin, he heartbrokenly accepts his father's choice to die and swears to live on and restore the family name, before the mysterious rescue saves them all."},{"from":40,"text":"Years later he is a captain of Spahis (French cavalry in Algeria) and a member of the Legion of Honour, dignified and brave; he once saved Château-Renaud from Arab horsemen. Every 5 September, the anniversary of his father's rescue, he tries to do some good deed, and he is still hunting for the unknown benefactor who acted through Thomson & French. His sister is happily married to Emmanuel Herbaut. The reader knows that benefactor was Dantès."},{"from":50,"text":"When the Count calls at the family home in the Rue Meslay, Maximilian is warm and open. He tells how Julie and Emmanuel wound up the firm with honour, and that his father died convinced their rescuer was Edmond Dantès. He feels the Count 'likes us'."},{"from":51,"text":"He is secretly and passionately in love with Valentine de Villefort, who is engaged to Franz d'Épinay. He has rented the plot behind her garden and poses as a gardener so they can talk through a boarded gate, and he scrupulously respects every limit she sets, hoping Providence will free her."},{"from":57,"text":"He feels a mysterious bond with Monte Cristo and half believes the Count quietly arranges good fortune for him. He keeps his love for Valentine secret even from his sister."},{"from":73,"text":"Now thirty, he is in despair as Valentine's marriage to Franz is hurried forward, and resolves to kill himself if it goes ahead. Hiding in the garden, he overhears Dr. d'Avrigny tell Villefort that Madame de Saint-Méran was poisoned, but says nothing to Valentine. Brought before Noirtier, he swears to wait for the old man's decision."},{"from":78,"text":"When Noirtier's revelation breaks off the engagement to Franz, Valentine tells Maximilian at the gate that they are saved, and he comes to worship the old man."},{"from":79,"text":"Visiting the Villefort house, he witnesses the servant Barrois' collapse and slips away unseen."},{"from":88,"text":"At the Opera he stops Albert de Morcerf from throwing his glove at the Count, then agrees to act as the Count's second, chilled by how coldly the Count speaks of killing Albert."},{"from":90,"text":"On the morning of the duel he learns that the Count's will names him heir to twenty million francs. He admits he loves a young girl more than his life, secures the first shot for his friend, and begs him to spare Albert. Afterwards the Count promises him the use of his power whenever he needs it."},{"from":93,"text":"Now allowed to visit Valentine twice a week, he has learned to read Noirtier's signals, and he is present when Valentine collapses."},{"from":94,"text":"In his fear he tells Monte Cristo what he overheard about poison and confesses that he loves Valentine de Villefort."},{"from":102,"text":"Trusting the Count's promise, he is caught off guard when he finds the house deserted, and rushes upstairs to hear that Valentine is dead."},{"from":103,"text":"He carries Noirtier up to her deathbed, declares himself her betrothed, denounces her murder, and blames himself for never revealing what he overheard."},{"from":105,"text":"He meant to shoot himself after the funeral; he had never loved anyone before he met Valentine at twenty-nine. The Count stops him, revealing that he is Edmond Dantès, the man who saved his father, and makes him swear to wait one month, until 5 October."},{"from":112,"text":"Numb and hopeless, he follows the Count to Marseille and weeps at his father's grave, feeling that leaving Paris is like losing Valentine a second time."},{"from":113,"text":"He renews his promise to wait and agrees to be brought from Bastia to the island of Monte Cristo on 5 October."},{"from":117,"text":"Having waited a month in vain, he comes calmly to die, refusing even the Count's fortune, and swallows what he believes is a fatal drug. He wakes to find Valentine alive beside him; the Count's farewell gifts go to him as the son of his old master."}],"moments":[{"chapter":30,"text":"Agreed, heartbroken, to let his father die and to live on to restore the family name."},{"chapter":40,"text":"Told how he saved Château-Renaud from Arab horsemen in Algeria."},{"chapter":50,"text":"Revealed that his father died believing their rescuer was Edmond Dantès."},{"chapter":51,"text":"Posed as a gardener to meet Valentine through the boarded gate."},{"chapter":57,"text":"Kissed Valentine's hand through the garden gate."},{"chapter":73,"text":"Overheard Dr. d'Avrigny tell Villefort the marquise was poisoned."},{"chapter":73,"text":"Swore to Noirtier to await his decision."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Heard from Valentine at the gate that they were saved."},{"chapter":79,"text":"Witnessed Barrois' collapse and slipped away unseen."},{"chapter":88,"text":"Stopped Albert from throwing his glove at the Count."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Acted as the Count's second at the meeting with Albert."},{"chapter":94,"text":"Confessed his love for Valentine to the Count."},{"chapter":102,"text":"Rushed upstairs just in time to hear that Valentine was dead."},{"chapter":103,"text":"Carried Noirtier to Valentine's deathbed and denounced her murder."},{"chapter":105,"text":"Swore not to take his life before 5 October."},{"chapter":112,"text":"Wept at his father's grave in Marseille."},{"chapter":113,"text":"Agreed to meet the Count on the island of Monte Cristo on 5 October."},{"chapter":117,"text":"Swallowed what he believed was a deadly drug, and woke to find Valentine beside him."}]},{"id":"ali","name":"Ali","firstChapter":31,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Count's household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":31,"text":"Sinbad's devoted mute Nubian servant"},{"from":37,"text":"The Count's devoted mute Nubian servant"}],"description":[{"from":31,"text":"A mute Nubian servant of Franz's mysterious host on Monte Cristo. The Bey of Tunis had his tongue cut out and was about to take his hand and head when 'Sinbad' bought his life with a fine gun and a cutlass, and Ali is utterly devoted to him."},{"from":37,"text":"In Rome, Franz recognizes the Count's driver as the mute servant from the grotto on Monte Cristo."},{"from":40,"text":"Sent a week ahead to Paris, he buys and furnishes a house for the Count and meets him at the city gate with the address."},{"from":42,"text":"He chose the house on the Champs-Élysées and greets his master with sincere joy."},{"from":46,"text":"The Count calls him a slave whose life belongs to him, and Ali happily agrees, though the Count is unexpectedly gentle with him."},{"from":47,"text":"A skilled lasso thrower who says he has hunted lions, he stops Madame de Villefort's runaway carriage single-handed and calms the horses completely."},{"from":49,"text":"He is to escort Haydée whenever she goes out, and is amazed to see his usually stern master happy."},{"from":82,"text":"He serves as the Count's silent, trusted aide: loading his pistols at the shooting gallery, and keeping watch at his side with a hatchet on the night of the burglary."},{"from":90,"text":"He drives the Count's carriage at breathtaking speed, brings his pistols before the duel, and carries his sealed will to the solicitor."},{"from":111,"text":"He goes ahead with Haydée when the Count leaves Paris."},{"from":112,"text":"He carries a letter from the Count to Noirtier and mimes the old man's 'yes' in reply."}],"moments":[{"chapter":40,"text":"Met the Count at the Paris city gate with the address of his new house."},{"chapter":47,"text":"Stopped Madame de Villefort's runaway carriage with his lasso."},{"chapter":82,"text":"Waited beside the Count with a hatchet and brought him the abbé's costume."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Took the Count's sealed will to his solicitor."},{"chapter":112,"text":"Mimed Noirtier's 'yes' after delivering the Count's letter."}]},{"id":"franz-depinay","name":"Franz d’Épinay","firstChapter":31,"importance":"supporting","group":"Paris society","aliases":[{"name":"Baron d’Épinay","from":31,"note":"his title"},{"name":"Franz de Quesnel","from":58,"note":"his family name; his father was General de Quesnel, made Baron d’Épinay"}],"tagline":[{"from":31,"text":"Albert’s sensible friend, who meets the mysterious Sinbad on Monte Cristo."},{"from":34,"text":"Albert’s friend, uneasy about the Count of Monte Cristo after Rome."},{"from":51,"text":"Albert’s friend, engaged to marry Valentine de Villefort."},{"from":78,"text":"Albert’s friend, who broke off his engagement to Valentine."}],"description":[{"from":31,"text":"Baron Franz d’Épinay is a young Parisian aristocrat who has lived in Italy for years and guides Albert there. Cool in danger but very curious, he dines with a mysterious host calling himself Sinbad on the island of Monte Cristo, and senses a man thirsting for revenge."},{"from":34,"text":"In Rome he pieces the clues together and realizes Sinbad is the Count of Monte Cristo. He admires the count but is repelled by his ideas and wary of him."},{"from":37,"text":"Loyal and quick-witted, he turns to the count to rescue the kidnapped Albert. Afterwards he tells Albert all he has seen, but Albert dismisses his suspicions."},{"from":51,"text":"He is Valentine de Villefort’s fiancé, a match her father insists on."},{"from":58,"text":"His mother died at his birth and his father, General de Quesnel, Baron d’Épinay, was assassinated in 1815 when Franz was two, supposedly by Bonapartists."},{"from":74,"text":"Honourable and sincere, he keeps his word to marry Valentine despite her lost inheritance, saying he seeks happiness, not money."},{"from":75,"text":"Summoned by Noirtier, he learns at last the truth about his father’s death: Noirtier killed him in a duel."},{"from":78,"text":"Shaken, he formally breaks off the engagement, writing that Villefort should have told him first. Albert names him as one of the seconds he wants in his quarrel with Beauchamp."},{"from":90,"text":"At Albert’s request he attends the duel with Monte Cristo, and is bewildered when Albert apologizes."}],"moments":[{"chapter":31,"text":"Dined in Sinbad’s hidden palace on Monte Cristo and tried hashish."},{"chapter":32,"text":"Searched the grotto for hours in vain for the hidden entrance."},{"chapter":34,"text":"Overheard the secret meeting in the Colosseum."},{"chapter":37,"text":"Turned to the count for help in rescuing Albert."},{"chapter":75,"text":"Read aloud the account of his father’s death, then learned Noirtier had killed him."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Broke off his engagement to Valentine."}]},{"id":"gaetano","name":"Gaetano","firstChapter":31,"importance":"minor","group":"Sailors & smugglers","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":31,"text":"Captain of Franz's boat, a part-time smuggler who knows the coast's secrets."}],"description":[{"from":31,"text":"Captain of Franz's boat and a part-time smuggler who knows the signals of smugglers and bandits. He has heard rumours of a mysterious chief."},{"from":32,"text":"He tells Franz that Sinbad is on excellent terms with smugglers and bandits all along the coast and owns a yacht faster than any frigate."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"signor-pastrini","name":"Signor Pastrini","firstChapter":31,"importance":"supporting","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":31,"text":"Owner of the Hôtel de Londres in Rome"}],"description":[{"from":31,"text":"Owner of the Hôtel de Londres in Rome, where Franz and Albert have booked rooms for the Carnival."},{"from":32,"text":"An obsequious but practical innkeeper, he warns that no carriages can be had at Carnival time. The rest of his floor is let to a mysterious rich traveller."},{"from":33,"text":"He knew the bandit Luigi Vampa as a child, and was once captured and released by him, receiving a fine Bréguet watch. He tells the friends Vampa's story."},{"from":34,"text":"He arranges his neighbour the Count's offer of carriage seats and windows, and collects execution notices for his guests' convenience."},{"from":36,"text":"He conjures up ready-made peasant costumes for the friends' Carnival outing."},{"from":37,"text":"Alarmed by a messenger who arrives at night, he arranges Franz's late audience with the Count."},{"from":114,"text":"Years later he welcomes Danglars to his hotel and interprets for him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":33,"text":"Told Franz and Albert the story of Luigi Vampa."},{"chapter":37,"text":"Arranged Franz's late-night audience with the Count."},{"chapter":114,"text":"Welcomed Danglars to the Hôtel de Londres."}]},{"id":"carlini","name":"Carlini","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Tragic bandit in Cucumetto's band, from Pastrini's tale"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"One of Cucumetto's bandits. He stabbed his beloved Rita to spare her from the band, swore revenge, and was shot from behind by Cucumetto."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"carmela","name":"Carmela","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"The Count of San-Felice's daughter, whose costume Teresa envied"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"The Count of San-Felice's beloved daughter, whose ball costume Teresa envied. When the villa burned, an unknown peasant carried her out of the flames."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"count-of-san-felice","name":"the Count of San-Felice","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Rich nobleman who employed the boy Luigi Vampa"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"A rich nobleman who employed Vampa as a shepherd boy, rewarded his cleverness, and gave the Carnival ball where Teresa danced. His villa later burned down."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"cucumetto","name":"Cucumetto","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Brutal bandit chief killed by the young Luigi Vampa"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"A notorious and brutal bandit chief from Pastrini's story, who shot his own man Carlini in the back. After Vampa and Teresa hid him from soldiers, he set his sights on Teresa and was shot dead by Vampa as he carried her off."}],"moments":[{"chapter":33,"text":"Was shot dead by Vampa while carrying Teresa off."}]},{"id":"diavolaccio","name":"Diavolaccio","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Bandit in Cucumetto's band, from Pastrini's tale"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"A bandit in Cucumetto's band who won the lot for the captive Rita, only to find that Carlini had stabbed her."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"luigi-vampa","name":"Luigi Vampa","firstChapter":33,"importance":"supporting","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Former shepherd turned feared bandit chief of the Roman countryside"},{"from":34,"text":"Roman bandit chief who owes total obedience to the Count"},{"from":114,"text":"Bandit chief holding Danglars captive in the catacombs"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"A famous bandit of about twenty-two, once a shepherd of remarkable intelligence, pride and marksmanship. Moody and jealous, he burned the San-Felice villa to win his beloved Teresa a fine costume, then shot the brutal chief Cucumetto and took over his band. He kills prisoners whose ransom arrives late, and hides among shepherds, fishermen and islands such as Monte Cristo."},{"from":34,"text":"He pays an informer inside the papal prison and is fiercely loyal to those who serve him. At the Colosseum Franz overhears him pledge total obedience to a cloaked man, the Count, in return for saving his man Peppino from execution."},{"from":37,"text":"When his band kidnaps Albert, the Count comes to the catacombs and Vampa releases him at once, apologizing. He reads Caesar's Commentaries, admires courage, keeps his word like a gentleman, and would punish any man who knowingly harmed the Count's friend."},{"from":40,"text":"The Count explains that he met Vampa as a shepherd boy more than ten years ago, later captured him and his band, and freed them on condition that they always respect him and his friends."},{"from":53,"text":"The Count enters a racehorse in Paris under the name 'Vampa', which gives him away to Albert."},{"from":114,"text":"He still leads his band from the catacombs of Saint Sebastian, now reading Plutarch's Life of Alexander. Danglars is led to him as a prisoner."},{"from":116,"text":"He answers to a higher chief who has forbidden him to shed Danglars' blood. He demands the banker's whole five million as ransom, charged meal by meal, and reminds him that some men suffered worse, dying of hunger."}],"moments":[{"chapter":33,"text":"Shot Cucumetto dead and was chosen captain of the bandits."},{"chapter":34,"text":"Pledged obedience to the cloaked man at the Colosseum in return for Peppino's life."},{"chapter":37,"text":"Released Albert and apologized to the Count."},{"chapter":116,"text":"Reminded Danglars that some men had suffered more, those who died of hunger."}]},{"id":"rita","name":"Rita","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Carlini's beloved, kidnapped by Cucumetto in Pastrini's tale"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"A surveyor's daughter from Frosinone, kidnapped by Cucumetto and killed by her lover Carlini to spare her from the band; her father hanged himself over her grave."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"teresa","name":"Teresa","firstChapter":33,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":33,"text":"Shepherdess loved by Luigi Vampa, who followed him into banditry"}],"description":[{"from":33,"text":"An orphan shepherdess from Valmontone, beautiful, flirtatious and fond of finery. She is the only person who can control Vampa, and eagerly followed him into the bandit life after he shot Cucumetto to rescue her."},{"from":37,"text":"Now Vampa's mistress, she was the masked 'peasant' in the Carnival carriage who flirted with Albert, with Vampa's consent."}],"moments":[{"chapter":33,"text":"Was rescued from Cucumetto by Vampa's shot."}]},{"id":"andrea-rondolo","name":"Andrea Rondolo","firstChapter":34,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":34,"text":"Condemned murderer executed during the Roman Carnival"}],"description":[{"from":34,"text":"Condemned to be killed with a mallet for murdering the priest Don César Torlini, who had raised him. A short, brutal-looking man, he rages when his fellow prisoner Peppino is pardoned, crying that he will not die alone."}],"moments":[{"chapter":35,"text":"Was executed with a mallet in the Piazza del Popolo."}]},{"id":"countess-g","name":"the Countess G——","firstChapter":34,"importance":"supporting","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":34,"text":"Witty Venetian countess who thinks the pale Count is a vampire"}],"description":[{"from":34,"text":"A witty, beautiful Venetian aristocrat whom Franz knew in Paris. Imaginative and superstitious, she is frightened by the pale stranger at the theatre in Rome and calls him a vampire."},{"from":36,"text":"She later decides the Count is merely an eccentric millionaire, and teases Albert about his Carnival romance."},{"from":37,"text":"At the Duke of Bracciano's ball she worries when Albert fails to appear to claim the dance she promised him."},{"from":53,"text":"Now in Paris, she is unnerved when the Count's racing cup is sent to her with a note from 'Lord Ruthven', and fears he holds a grudge over her vampire jokes."},{"from":68,"text":"She still believes the Count is Lord Ruthven, the vampire of the stories."}],"moments":[{"chapter":34,"text":"Declared the pale stranger at the theatre a vampire."},{"chapter":53,"text":"Received the Jockey Club gold cup with a note from 'Lord Ruthven'."}]},{"id":"greek-girl","name":"The Count’s Greek companion","firstChapter":34,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Count's household","mergedInto":{"id":"haydee","from":45},"aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":34,"text":"A mysterious, beautiful young woman in Greek dress seen at the Roman theatre."},{"from":40,"text":"A beautiful Greek girl the Count says he bought in Constantinople."}],"description":[{"from":34,"text":"An exquisitely beautiful young woman in Greek dress who has attended every performance in Rome this season and delights in the ballet. No one knows who she is; she comes with the pale stranger in the box or with a black servant."},{"from":36,"text":"She seems to live in the Count’s apartments: Albert and Franz hear the sound of her guzla, a stringed instrument, but never see her."},{"from":40,"text":"In Paris, the Count says he bought her in Constantinople, that she speaks only modern Greek, and that everyone around him is free to leave him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":34,"text":"Was seen in a box at the Teatro Argentina beside the pale stranger."}]},{"id":"peppino","name":"Peppino","firstChapter":34,"importance":"supporting","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[{"name":"Rocca Priori","from":34,"note":"his nickname on the execution notice"}],"tagline":[{"from":34,"text":"Shepherd condemned to death for helping Vampa's bandits"},{"from":35,"text":"Vampa's man, pardoned at the scaffold thanks to the Count"},{"from":114,"text":"Polite bandit who sells the captive Danglars food at ruinous prices"}],"description":[{"from":34,"text":"A shepherd condemned to the guillotine for supplying food to Vampa's band. Franz overhears the cloaked Count promise Vampa to save him."},{"from":35,"text":"A handsome, sunburned man of twenty-four or twenty-five, he walks to the scaffold with a firm step, watching for his rescuer, and is pardoned at the last moment; afterwards he slips away into the crowd."},{"from":37,"text":"Now serving as Vampa's messenger and fiercely grateful to the Count, he brings the ransom letter for Albert and guides the Count to the catacombs."},{"from":40,"text":"Albert guesses that the 'life of a man' the Pope granted the Count in exchange for an emerald was Peppino's."},{"from":114,"text":"Years later he shadows Danglars through Rome and leads him into the catacombs; the bandits, helped by a clerk at Thomson & French, knew in advance what the banker would draw."},{"from":115,"text":"An epicure who speaks perfect French, he runs the bandits' 'fixed-price' menu with flawless politeness, charging Danglars 100,000 francs for a chicken or a loaf of bread."},{"from":116,"text":"He keeps selling Danglars food and drink at ruinous prices, and finally ignores his pleas."}],"moments":[{"chapter":35,"text":"Was pardoned at the foot of the scaffold."},{"chapter":37,"text":"Delivered the ransom letter and guided the Count to the catacombs."},{"chapter":114,"text":"Shadowed Danglars through Rome and led him into the catacombs."},{"chapter":115,"text":"Charged Danglars 100,000 francs for a fowl, or for a loaf of bread."}]},{"id":"bertuccio","name":"Bertuccio","firstChapter":35,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Count's household","aliases":[{"name":"Giovanni Bertuccio","from":44,"note":"his full name"}],"tagline":[{"from":35,"text":"The Count of Monte Cristo's steward."},{"from":43,"text":"The Count's Corsican steward, haunted by a vendetta killing at Auteuil."},{"from":62,"text":"The steward who recognizes three figures from his past at Auteuil."},{"from":107,"text":"The Count's steward and Benedetto's foster-father."}],"description":[{"from":35,"text":"Bertuccio is the Count's steward, a man of forty-five to fifty. He looks exactly like the smuggler who led Franz into the cave on Monte Cristo, but shows no sign of recognizing him."},{"from":42,"text":"A Corsican, he has been a soldier and a smuggler and has perhaps been in trouble with the police over ‘a stab with a knife’. He is fiercely efficient, but goes pale at the name Auteuil and tries to talk his master out of buying a house there."},{"from":43,"text":"He confessed to the Abbé Busoni in prison at Nîmes and was recommended to the Count by him. In the garden at Auteuil he breaks down and admits that he took a vendetta there: his victim was Villefort, who had just buried a child."},{"from":44,"text":"A smuggler from Rogliano raised by his older brother, he swore vengeance when Villefort refused to pursue his brother's murderer. He stabbed Villefort in the Auteuil garden and saved the newborn Villefort was burying. His sister-in-law Assunta raised the boy, Benedetto, who grew up wicked. Later he witnessed Caderousse's sale of the diamond at the inn near Beaucaire."},{"from":45,"text":"Wrongly arrested for the murders at the inn, he was saved by the Abbé Busoni. Returning to Corsica, he found that Benedetto had tortured Assunta and fled. He sees his misfortunes as punishment, and calls the Villeforts ‘an accursed race’."},{"from":62,"text":"He transforms the Auteuil house, leaving only the red room untouched. At the dinner he recognizes Madame Danglars as the woman of the garden, Villefort as the man he thought he had killed, and Andrea Cavalcanti as Benedetto."},{"from":68,"text":"The Count has him follow Madame Danglars, then sends him, gladly, to Normandy to prepare a house, a boat and relays of horses."},{"from":85,"text":"The Count says Bertuccio is alone in the world and that he holds his life in his hands. He turns pale whenever Benedetto is mentioned."},{"from":107,"text":"He recognized Benedetto long ago on the Champs-Élysées. He visits him in prison, warns him he has fallen into terrible hands, forbids him to claim the Count as his father, and promises to tell him his real father's name."}],"moments":[{"chapter":43,"text":"Broke down in the Auteuil garden and admitted to a killing there."},{"chapter":44,"text":"Stabbed Villefort and revived the newborn child buried in a box."},{"chapter":45,"text":"Was arrested, covered in La Carconte's blood, for the murder of the jeweller."},{"chapter":46,"text":"Bought Danglars' horses for the Count for 32,000 francs."},{"chapter":62,"text":"Recognized Madame Danglars, Villefort and Andrea (Benedetto) at the Auteuil dinner."},{"chapter":107,"text":"Visited Benedetto in prison and promised to tell him his father's name."}]},{"id":"beppo","name":"Beppo","firstChapter":37,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":37,"text":"Teenage bandit who lured Albert into Vampa's trap"}],"description":[{"from":37,"text":"A fifteen-year-old member of Vampa's band who disguised himself as a peasant girl and lured Albert into the ambush."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"duke-of-bracciano","name":"the Duke of Bracciano","firstChapter":37,"importance":"minor","group":"Rome & the bandits","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":37,"text":"Roman banker famous for his balls"}],"description":[{"from":37,"text":"A celebrated Roman banker whose balls are famous across Europe. At his ball he worries about Albert being out in the streets at night."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"beauchamp","name":"Beauchamp","firstChapter":39,"importance":"supporting","group":"Paris society","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":39,"text":"Albert’s friend, a quick-witted journalist on an opposition newspaper."},{"from":78,"text":"Newspaper editor challenged by Albert over the Yanina article."},{"from":84,"text":"The journalist who proved Morcerf’s treason and offered to keep it secret."}],"description":[{"from":39,"text":"One of Albert’s friends, a journalist on an opposition paper who trades friendly political insults with Debray and is always rushing off to the Chamber of Deputies."},{"from":40,"text":"Sceptical by trade, he tries to catch the Count out, then decides the Count is a better story than any speech."},{"from":74,"text":"A cynical philosopher at heart, he doubts the Marquise de Saint-Méran really died of apoplexy."},{"from":78,"text":"As editor of l’Impartial, he refuses to retract the paragraph about Yanina under Albert’s threats, though he did not write it. He asks for three weeks to investigate, after which he will retract or fight."},{"from":84,"text":"He has secretly been to Yanina and back and found the story true. Generously, he hands Albert the only proofs to destroy and promises to keep the secret."},{"from":86,"text":"When another paper names Morcerf, he alerts Albert and watches the Chamber of Peers hearing from a hidden gallery."},{"from":87,"text":"He reveals that the banker in Yanina said Danglars had already made inquiries there, and tries to keep Albert prudent."},{"from":88,"text":"He apologizes on his own account for Albert’s insult at the Opera, then arranges the duel and serves as one of Albert’s seconds."},{"from":91,"text":"Baffled by Albert’s apology, he advises him with thin irony to leave France for a few years."},{"from":104,"text":"His newspaper hints that Villefort would have investigated the deaths in his house more closely had they happened elsewhere."}],"moments":[{"chapter":47,"text":"Gave the Count’s bravery twenty lines in his newspaper."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Agreed to a duel with Albert after three weeks."},{"chapter":84,"text":"Gave Albert the proofs of his father’s guilt to destroy."},{"chapter":85,"text":"Sent Albert’s valet galloping to Normandy with the news."},{"chapter":86,"text":"Watched Morcerf’s hearing in secret from a gallery."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Acted as Albert’s second at the duel that ended in an apology."}]},{"id":"germain","name":"Germain","firstChapter":39,"importance":"minor","group":"The Morcerf household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":39,"text":"Albert de Morcerf’s trusted valet."}],"description":[{"from":39,"text":"Albert’s trusted valet, who runs his small household with an English groom named John, and announces the Count’s arrival at Albert’s breakfast."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"hermine-danglars","name":"Madame Danglars","firstChapter":39,"importance":"major","group":"The Danglars household","aliases":[{"name":"Madame de Nargonne","from":39,"note":"the widow Danglars married, as Caderousse told it in ch 27"},{"name":"Baroness Danglars","from":46,"note":"her title"},{"name":"Hermine","from":47,"note":"her first name"},{"name":"Hermine de Servières","from":47,"note":"her maiden name (also given as de Servieux)"}],"tagline":[{"from":39,"text":"Danglars' wife, a fashionable Paris baroness."},{"from":53,"text":"Danglars' elegant, scornful wife; Debray is her lover and stock-market adviser."},{"from":62,"text":"A baroness with a buried past: the pregnant woman Bertuccio saw with Villefort."},{"from":67,"text":"Mother of Villefort's lost child, who learns the baby may have lived."},{"from":106,"text":"Abandoned by husband and lover, she leaves Paris alone but rich."},{"from":110,"text":"Benedetto's mother, recognized when she collapsed at his trial."}],"description":[{"from":39,"text":"Madame Danglars is the banker's wife, the widow Madame de Nargonne whom Danglars married. She moves in the best Paris circles and sends Albert de Morcerf a perfumed note offering him a seat in her opera box."},{"from":46,"text":"Danglars boasts that she comes from an ancient family, born de Servières, and that her first husband was the Marquis de Nargonne. The Count seems to know more about the couple's private life than he lets on."},{"from":47,"text":"Still handsome, she has refined taste, a temper, and open contempt for her husband. She is close to Lucien Debray, the minister's secretary, and to Madame de Villefort. She is furious to find her beloved dappled grays sold behind her back."},{"from":54,"text":"Debray is openly her lover. She is a daring, willful speculator on the stock exchange, using Debray's inside knowledge of government news to steer her husband's investments. She is eager to meet the Count and to be invited to his house."},{"from":62,"text":"At the Count's dinner at Auteuil, Bertuccio recognizes her as the young, pregnant widow he saw years ago in that very garden, waiting for Villefort, on the night Villefort buried a newborn child there. She looks anxiously over the house from the moment she arrives."},{"from":63,"text":"Every hint the Count drops about the red room, the hidden staircase and a child shakes her more, until she faints."},{"from":65,"text":"Her husband reveals he has known about her affairs for sixteen years, including her past with Villefort, and has tolerated them for his own profit. She has made 500,000 livres this year from Debray's tips. Her first husband, M. de Nargonne, is said to have died of grief or rage on coming home after nine months away to find her six months pregnant."},{"from":67,"text":"Veiled, she secretly visits Villefort. As M. de Nargonne's widow she bore Villefort's child in the red damask room at Auteuil and was told it was born dead; she has mourned it ever since. Villefort tells her the child may have survived, and she is both overjoyed and horrified."},{"from":76,"text":"Still nervous of the Count but charmed whenever he appears, she claims to have given up speculating."},{"from":93,"text":"She proudly announces Eugénie's coming marriage to ‘Prince’ Cavalcanti."},{"from":99,"text":"After Andrea's arrest she fears her clever daughter, who despises her affair and money dealings with Debray. She uses her old intimacy with Villefort to beg him to let Benedetto escape or delay his trial, but he refuses."},{"from":106,"text":"Abandoned by Danglars, she hopes Debray will protect her. Instead he coolly hands over her share of their joint speculations, and she leaves him without a farewell, rich in money but humiliated."},{"from":110,"text":"She attends Benedetto's trial veiled. When he declares that his mother believed him born dead, she cries out and collapses, her veil falls, and she is recognized."}],"moments":[{"chapter":47,"text":"Discovered her dappled grays had been sold and harnessed to the Count's carriage."},{"chapter":63,"text":"Fainted when the Count imagined a man carrying a sleeping child down the hidden staircase."},{"chapter":65,"text":"Turned white when her husband named Villefort among those who ‘trembled’ before him."},{"chapter":67,"text":"Secretly visited Villefort and learned their child might still be alive."},{"chapter":99,"text":"Begged Villefort to let Benedetto escape, or at least delay his trial, and was refused."},{"chapter":106,"text":"Took her 1,340,000 francs and walked out on Debray without a farewell."},{"chapter":110,"text":"Fainted at Benedetto's trial and was carried out of the courtroom."}]},{"id":"lucien-debray","name":"Lucien Debray","firstChapter":39,"importance":"supporting","group":"Paris society","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":39,"text":"Albert’s cool, witty friend, private secretary to the Minister of the Interior."},{"from":65,"text":"The minister’s secretary, Madame Danglars’ lover and stock-market tipster."},{"from":106,"text":"Madame Danglars’ former lover, who left her coldly once she was of no use."}],"description":[{"from":39,"text":"A tall, fair, cool young man, private secretary to the Minister of the Interior. Witty and worldly, he is one of Albert’s friends."},{"from":40,"text":"He doubts the Count exists, then promises to find out through the ministry’s police who the man really is."},{"from":47,"text":"He is a frequent and very much at-home visitor to Madame Danglars, whose boudoir they decorated together; her husband treats this as normal."},{"from":54,"text":"He lets slip that it is Madame Danglars who gambles on the stock market, and is embarrassed when Albert jokes about feeding her government news."},{"from":61,"text":"As the minister’s secretary he sees official telegraph news first, and passes it straight to Madame Danglars."},{"from":65,"text":"He is plainly Madame Danglars’ lover. Danglars accuses him of pocketing the money she made from his inside tips."},{"from":99,"text":"Friends urge him to marry Eugénie for her money, an idea he half likes but knows is hopeless."},{"from":106,"text":"He and Madame Danglars have been secret partners in speculation, turning her 100,000 francs into millions. Once she is no longer useful, he pays her share and drops her coldly, keeping over a million francs for himself."}],"moments":[{"chapter":39,"text":"Arrived first at Albert’s breakfast, after a night writing dispatches."},{"chapter":61,"text":"Rushed to tell the baroness that her husband must sell his Spanish bonds."},{"chapter":65,"text":"Was coolly dismissed from Madame Danglars’ rooms by her husband."},{"chapter":106,"text":"Paid Madame Danglars her share and let her go without a kind word."}]},{"id":"chateau-renaud","name":"M. de Château-Renaud","firstChapter":40,"importance":"supporting","group":"Paris society","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":40,"text":"Albert’s well-born friend, a duellist and amateur soldier."}],"description":[{"from":40,"text":"A handsome, well-born gentleman of about thirty in Albert’s circle. He once wounded Franz d’Épinay in a duel and went to Algeria to fight “as an amateur.” He judges the Count to be a great man."},{"from":62,"text":"A fine horseman with a sharp eye, he is a regular at society gatherings and remarks on everyone."},{"from":74,"text":"He is one of Franz’s witnesses for the marriage contract."},{"from":88,"text":"He backs Albert without question in his quarrel with the count and agrees to act as one of his seconds."},{"from":90,"text":"At the duel he is stunned by Albert’s apology, which he finds either very despicable or very noble."},{"from":109,"text":"He instinctively dislikes Madame de Villefort."},{"from":110,"text":"He remarks that Morcerf’s pistol shot seems kinder than Villefort’s public ruin."}],"moments":[{"chapter":47,"text":"Spread the story of the Count’s bravery at the Jockey Club."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Acted as Albert’s second at the duel."}]},{"id":"eugenie-danglars","name":"Eugénie Danglars","firstChapter":40,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Danglars household","aliases":[{"name":"Claire-Marie-Eugénie Danglars","from":77,"note":"her full name"},{"name":"Léon d'Armilly","from":97,"note":"the name she travels under, disguised as a young man"}],"tagline":[{"from":40,"text":"Danglars' daughter, talked of as Albert de Morcerf's future bride."},{"from":53,"text":"Danglars' proud, gifted daughter, cold toward her intended, Albert."},{"from":81,"text":"Engaged by her father to Andrea Cavalcanti, a match she merely tolerates."},{"from":97,"text":"Fled Paris disguised as a young man, to live as an artist."}],"description":[{"from":40,"text":"Eugénie is Danglars' daughter, spoken of as Albert de Morcerf's intended bride, with a dowry of two million francs. Albert's friends doubt the proud Morcerfs will ever accept a banker's daughter."},{"from":53,"text":"Seventeen, handsome and severe, with black hair and eyes and a habitual frown. She is a gifted linguist, artist and musician. She treats Albert coolly and is frank to the point of rudeness: she bluntly asks the Count whether Haydée is his daughter."},{"from":57,"text":"She dislikes the idea of marrying Albert and prizes her independence; she almost wishes her father would lose his fortune so that she could live as an artist. She makes music every day with her friend Louise d'Armilly."},{"from":76,"text":"Her father now prefers Andrea Cavalcanti as a son-in-law. Cold, beautiful and satirical, she ignores Andrea's sighs, though the two are admired singing together. Albert says the two of them regard each other with mutual indifference."},{"from":84,"text":"She put up with Andrea only to be rid of Albert, and now openly dislikes him."},{"from":95,"text":"She tells her father she loves no one, wants no husband, and believes she could earn a fortune as a singer. She agrees to sign the marriage contract in three days only on condition that she remains entirely free, and keeps her own plans to herself."},{"from":97,"text":"Scornful of all men, she had planned to run away even before Andrea was exposed. That night she cuts off her hair, dresses as a man, and leaves Paris as ‘Léon d'Armilly’ with Louise, meaning to live as an artist in Italy."},{"from":98,"text":"At an inn in Compiègne, Andrea falls into their room; she coldly urges him to kill himself, then has to leave through a jeering crowd."}],"moments":[{"chapter":53,"text":"Bluntly asked the Count whether Haydée was his daughter."},{"chapter":95,"text":"Agreed to sign the marriage contract, on her own terms."},{"chapter":97,"text":"Fled Paris disguised as a young man."},{"chapter":98,"text":"Urged Andrea to kill himself rather than be taken."}]},{"id":"assunta","name":"Assunta","firstChapter":44,"importance":"minor","group":"The Count's household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":44,"text":"Bertuccio's kind, widowed sister-in-law, who raised the foundling Benedetto."}],"description":[{"from":44,"text":"Assunta is Bertuccio's widowed sister-in-law in Rogliano, Corsica. A kind woman, she secretly reclaimed the baby Bertuccio had left at the foundling hospital, named him Benedetto, raised him as her own and spoiled him."},{"from":45,"text":"Benedetto and two companions held her over the fire to make her give up her money. She was found terribly burned and died."}],"moments":[{"chapter":45,"text":"Died after being burned by Benedetto and his friends."}]},{"id":"benedetto","name":"Benedetto","firstChapter":44,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Count's household","mergedInto":{"id":"andrea-cavalcanti","from":62},"aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":44,"text":"The baby Bertuccio rescued from Villefort's garden, raised in Corsica."},{"from":45,"text":"Bertuccio's wicked foster-son, who tortured Assunta and vanished."}],"description":[{"from":44,"text":"Benedetto is the newborn child that Bertuccio, in his story to the Count, saw Villefort bury in a box in the garden at Auteuil. Having stabbed Villefort, Bertuccio dug up the baby and revived it. Its fine linen was marked with an H and an N under a baron's coronet. He left the child at a Paris foundling hospital, but his widowed sister-in-law Assunta reclaimed it, named it Benedetto, and raised it in Corsica."},{"from":44,"text":"Blond, clever and spoiled by Assunta, the boy grew up a liar and a thief. He knew Bertuccio was not his father, was running with older criminals by the age of eleven, and laughed at Bertuccio's plan to send him to sea."},{"from":45,"text":"While Bertuccio was away, Benedetto and two companions held Assunta over the fire to make her give up her money, robbed the house and disappeared. Bertuccio hopes he is dead."}],"moments":[{"chapter":44,"text":"Was buried alive at birth by Villefort and rescued by Bertuccio."},{"chapter":44,"text":"Mocked Bertuccio's offer to take him to sea."},{"chapter":45,"text":"Tortured his foster-mother Assunta for money and disappeared."}]},{"id":"israel-bertuccio","name":"Israel Bertuccio","firstChapter":44,"importance":"minor","group":"The Count's household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":44,"text":"Bertuccio's elder brother, murdered in Nîmes in 1815."}],"description":[{"from":44,"text":"Bertuccio's older brother, who raised him, was a lieutenant in a Corsican regiment of Napoleon's army. He was murdered in Nîmes in 1815 during the royalist massacres, and Villefort's refusal to seek justice set off Bertuccio's vendetta."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"joannes","name":"Joannes","firstChapter":44,"importance":"minor","group":"Marseille, 1815","aliases":[{"name":"Johannes","from":82,"note":"spelling used when the abbé recalls the murder"}],"tagline":[{"from":44,"text":"Paris jeweller who buys Caderousse's diamond."},{"from":45,"text":"The jeweller murdered at Caderousse's inn."}],"description":[{"from":44,"text":"Joannes is a jeweller from the Palais Royal in Paris who bargains Caderousse's diamond down to 45,000 francs. Armed with pistols, he returns to the inn to shelter from a storm."},{"from":45,"text":"He is murdered in his bed at the inn, after fighting back with his pistols."}],"moments":[{"chapter":45,"text":"Was stabbed to death at Caderousse's inn."}]},{"id":"haydee","name":"Haydée","firstChapter":45,"importance":"major","group":"The Count's household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":45,"text":"The lovely young Greek woman who lives secluded in the Count’s house."},{"from":53,"text":"The Count’s Greek ward, who accuses Morcerf of betraying her father."},{"from":77,"text":"Daughter of Ali Pasha of Yanina, sold into slavery and freed by the Count."},{"from":86,"text":"Ali Pasha’s daughter, whose testimony brought down the Count de Morcerf."},{"from":117,"text":"Ali Pasha’s daughter, who chose to stay with the count out of love."}],"description":[{"from":45,"text":"Haydée is the beautiful Greek woman glimpsed with the Count in Rome. She arrives at his Paris house at midnight, kisses his hand with love and respect, and is given hidden apartments, kept apart from the French servants."},{"from":48,"text":"The Count speaks of her as his slave."},{"from":49,"text":"About nineteen or twenty, from Epirus, she is devoted to the Count. She has loved only her late father, a white-bearded man of about sixty, and the Count — and she says her love for him is not a daughter’s. Told she is free, she refuses ever to leave him. He asks her to keep the secret of her birth."},{"from":53,"text":"At the Opera she dazzles the audience, then nearly faints on seeing the Count de Morcerf. When told he served her father, she cries that he sold her father to the Turks and that his fortune was the price of that treachery. Since Morcerf served Ali Pasha of Yanina, it seems her father was Ali himself."},{"from":71,"text":"The count tells Mercédès she is a prince’s daughter he bought as a slave in Constantinople and has adopted, having no one else to love."},{"from":77,"text":"She tells Albert her story: she is the daughter of Ali Tepelini, Pasha of Yanina, and his wife Vasiliki. When she was four, her father was betrayed by a French officer he trusted and killed by the sultan’s troops. She and her mother were sold as slaves, and after her mother’s death she passed through other hands until the count acquired her."},{"from":86,"text":"She has always meant to avenge her father and follows every newspaper in her seclusion. Without telling the count, she appears before the Chamber of Peers with her birth records and the bill of sale proving that Fernand Mondego sold her and her mother, and denounces Morcerf to his face."},{"from":90,"text":"Afraid for the count before his duel, she waits all night outside his door; on reading his will she tears it up, saying she will need nothing if he dies."},{"from":92,"text":"She greets the count with a joy that seems both a daughter’s and a lover’s."},{"from":111,"text":"She leaves Paris ahead of the count to wait for him."},{"from":117,"text":"The count offers to free her and restore her father’s name and wealth, but she says she would die without him, loving him “as one loves a father, brother, husband.”"}],"moments":[{"chapter":45,"text":"Arrived at the Count’s Paris house at midnight."},{"chapter":49,"text":"Promised the Count she would never leave him."},{"chapter":53,"text":"Recognized the Count de Morcerf at the Opera and denounced him as the man who betrayed her father."},{"chapter":77,"text":"Told Albert the story of her father’s betrayal and death."},{"chapter":86,"text":"Testified before the Chamber of Peers and denounced Morcerf to his face."},{"chapter":90,"text":"Tore up the count’s will."},{"chapter":117,"text":"Declared her love for the count and sailed away with him."}]},{"id":"baptistin","name":"M. Baptistin","firstChapter":46,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Count's household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":46,"text":"The Count's discreet French valet in Paris"}],"description":[{"from":46,"text":"The Count's French valet, in his service for a year. He is warned never to gossip about his master and told that loyal servants share a fund to be divided at the Count's death."},{"from":54,"text":"Efficient and perfectly discreet, he recognizes the Cavalcanti visitors from his master's exact descriptions and shows them in. Albert would love to employ him."},{"from":82,"text":"He is sharply rebuked for questioning orders when he delivers an anonymous warning letter."},{"from":90,"text":"He follows his master to the duel with Albert in terror."},{"from":111,"text":"He is the only servant who leaves Paris with the Count and Morrel."}],"moments":[{"chapter":55,"text":"Recognized the Cavalcanti visitors from the Count's description."},{"chapter":82,"text":"Delivered the anonymous warning letter and was rebuked for questioning orders."},{"chapter":111,"text":"Left Paris with the Count and Morrel, the only servant to do so."}]},{"id":"edouard-de-villefort","name":"Edward","firstChapter":47,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[{"name":"Edward de Villefort","from":59,"note":"full name"}],"tagline":[{"from":47,"text":"Madame de Villefort's spoiled, crafty little son."},{"from":51,"text":"Valentine's spoiled half-brother, his mother's idol."},{"from":101,"text":"Villefort's young son, for whose inheritance the poisonings were committed."},{"from":111,"text":"Villefort's young son, poisoned by his own mother."}],"description":[{"from":47,"text":"A small, pale boy of seven or eight with a crafty look, spoiled and willful. Revived by a drop of the Count's elixir after the carriage accident, he refuses to thank Ali because he is 'too ugly'."},{"from":51,"text":"Valentine's troublesome half-brother and the object of his mother's overpowering love. He tortures the parrot and mocks his sister, and is barely scolded."},{"from":57,"text":"He inherits nothing from Valentine's mother, so he is poor beside his half-sister. He idolizes the Count, who brings him presents. Noirtier will not have him in his room and refuses to leave him anything."},{"from":93,"text":"He is still up to mischief, emptying Valentine's water decanter to make a pond for his ducks."},{"from":101,"text":"As Villefort's only son, he would eventually inherit Valentine's fortune, and the Count says the crimes in the house were committed on his account."},{"from":111,"text":"His mother, condemned by Villefort, sent for him half an hour before his father returned home. He was found dead on the sofa in her boudoir."}],"moments":[{"chapter":47,"text":"Was revived by one drop of the Count's red elixir."},{"chapter":74,"text":"Blurted out, 'Grandpapa Noirtier can speak now, then?'"},{"chapter":108,"text":"Was kissed and sent out of the room by his father before the confrontation with his mother."},{"chapter":111,"text":"Was found dead on a sofa in his mother's boudoir."}]},{"id":"heloise-de-villefort","name":"Madame de Villefort","firstChapter":47,"importance":"major","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[{"name":"Héloïse de Villefort","from":47,"note":"her first name"}],"tagline":[{"from":47,"text":"Villefort's young second wife, devoted to her spoiled son Edward."},{"from":51,"text":"Valentine's smiling stepmother, who lives for her own son."},{"from":101,"text":"Valentine's stepmother, revealed as the poisoner killing so Edward will inherit."},{"from":108,"text":"Unmasked poisoner, ordered by her husband to take her own life."},{"from":111,"text":"The poisoner who died by her own hand, taking her son with her."}],"description":[{"from":47,"text":"Villefort's young second wife, who dotes on her spoiled son Edward. After the Count saves them from runaway horses, she is fascinated by him and his case of remedies, and urges her husband to visit him."},{"from":51,"text":"Valentine says her stepmother hides hostility behind constant smiles, adores her own son, and has no fortune of her own."},{"from":52,"text":"She has long been fascinated by Mithridates and poisons, and knows a good deal of botany and chemistry. She keeps the Count talking about drugs and their effects, and asks for, and gets, the recipe for his elixir."},{"from":57,"text":"Valentine believes her stepmother would prefer she never married at all: if Valentine took the veil, her fortune would go to Villefort and in time to Edward. Madame de Villefort admires Monte Cristo enormously."},{"from":59,"text":"She hoped Noirtier would leave his fortune to Edward. Angry when he refuses, she still cannot hide her pleasure that Valentine has been disinherited, and keeps returning to how unfair the will is to her son."},{"from":72,"text":"When her mother-in-law arrives grieving and hostile, her presence seems to distress the old lady, so she withdraws."},{"from":78,"text":"She surprises Noirtier by visiting him, claiming she always opposed the d'Épinay marriage, and asking him to restore his fortune to Valentine."},{"from":93,"text":"When Valentine feels unwell, she tells her to go and drink a glass of water, and says she fears a serious illness."},{"from":101,"text":"The truth comes out: she is the poisoner. She creeps through Edward's room into Valentine's at night to dose her drink. The Count says she has been plotting ever since she asked him about aqua tofana in Perugia, killing so that Edward will inherit everything."},{"from":102,"text":"Standing over Valentine she feels a stir of fear and remorse, but she carefully empties and rinses the glass. She faints when d'Avrigny finds poison in it anyway."},{"from":108,"text":"Confronted by Villefort, she cannot deny the four murders. She begs for her life for Edward's sake and laughs wildly at the idea that she could ever harm him. He orders her to take her own poison before he returns."},{"from":111,"text":"Believing a good mother cannot leave her son behind, she poisoned both herself and Edward, leaving a note for her husband."}],"moments":[{"chapter":47,"text":"Was rescued with her son when Madame Danglars' horses bolted at Auteuil."},{"chapter":52,"text":"Asked for, and received, the recipe for the Count's elixir."},{"chapter":63,"text":"Lent the Count her smelling bottle to revive Madame Danglars."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Asked Noirtier to make Valentine his heir again."},{"chapter":101,"text":"Was seen by Valentine pouring poison into her glass at night."},{"chapter":102,"text":"Rinsed Valentine's glass before dawn, then fainted when d'Avrigny found poison in it anyway."},{"chapter":108,"text":"Was condemned by her husband and locked in to die by her own hand."},{"chapter":111,"text":"Poisoned herself and her son Edward."}]},{"id":"valentine-de-villefort","name":"Valentine","firstChapter":47,"importance":"major","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[{"name":"Mademoiselle de Villefort","from":48,"note":"Villefort's daughter"}],"tagline":[{"from":47,"text":"A young friend of Eugénie Danglars, mentioned in a letter."},{"from":48,"text":"Villefort's daughter, devoted nurse of her paralyzed grandfather."},{"from":51,"text":"Villefort's daughter, engaged to Franz but secretly in love with Maximilian Morrel."},{"from":75,"text":"Villefort's daughter, freed from her engagement to Franz, hoping to marry Maximilian."},{"from":80,"text":"Rich young heiress whom d'Avrigny suspects of a string of poisonings."},{"from":93,"text":"Villefort's daughter, struck down by poison and barely surviving."},{"from":101,"text":"Poisoning target who now knows her stepmother is trying to kill her."},{"from":102,"text":"Villefort's daughter, found dead in her bed and buried at Père-Lachaise."},{"from":117,"text":"Alive after all: her death was staged by the Count; reunited with Maximilian."}],"description":[{"from":47,"text":"First mentioned in a postscript to Madame de Villefort's letter, sending her love to Eugénie Danglars."},{"from":48,"text":"Villefort's daughter by his first wife, nearly eighteen. She looks after her paralyzed grandfather Noirtier."},{"from":51,"text":"She is engaged against her will to Franz d'Épinay and in love with Maximilian Morrel, whom she meets secretly at the garden gate. Neglected by her father and, she feels, quietly hated by her stepmother, her only comfort at home is her grandfather, whose eye signals only she can read."},{"from":52,"text":"Tall and graceful, with chestnut hair and blue eyes, she looks sad and has been crying. She went to Italy for her health two years ago."},{"from":57,"text":"She is rich in her own right and expects inheritances from her grandparents, which is why her stepmother once encouraged her to become a nun. She gave up the convent only because of her grandfather's despairing look. She distrusts Monte Cristo, who seems to avoid her."},{"from":59,"text":"She cares nothing for money, only for her grandfather's love. She interprets his signals for the notary as he disinherits her to block the match with Franz."},{"from":72,"text":"After her grandfather's sudden death she nurses her grandmother through a fever, not daring to mention Maximilian because of the old lady's contempt for commoners."},{"from":73,"text":"Torn between duty and love, she introduces Maximilian to Noirtier and swears by her mother's memory to run away with him rather than sign the contract."},{"from":75,"text":"When Noirtier answers Franz's question about his father's killer, she understands first; she has often seen the two scars on her grandfather's arm. The engagement is broken."},{"from":78,"text":"Freed, she tells Maximilian 'We are saved!' Noirtier makes her his heir, and with the Saint-Méran fortune she can expect a huge income."},{"from":80,"text":"It was she who carried in the lemonade that killed Barrois. D'Avrigny points out that she handled the drinks and medicines in every case and accuses her of the poisonings, though she knows nothing of it."},{"from":93,"text":"Unwell for two weeks and finding everything bitter, she has been taking spoonfuls of her grandfather's medicine on his orders. After drinking a glass of water she collapses into rigid unconsciousness."},{"from":94,"text":"She survives a heavy dose of poison, thanks to the resistance Noirtier built up in her with his medicine."},{"from":100,"text":"Still weak and troubled by night visions, she is visited by Monte Cristo, who tells her that someone has been poisoning her drinks and that he has been watching over her."},{"from":101,"text":"She learns her stepmother is the poisoner and her fortune the motive. She would rather die than denounce her, and her first thought is fear for Edward. She takes a pill from the Count's emerald box, promising to fear nothing, even if she wakes in a tomb."},{"from":102,"text":"She is found cold, rigid and breathless in her bed, and Dr. d'Avrigny pronounces her dead."},{"from":104,"text":"Her death is officially confirmed, and after a great funeral she is buried in the Saint-Méran and Villefort vault at Père-Lachaise, mourned by a crowd of young people."},{"from":117,"text":"She is alive. The Count's drug let her appear dead, and she has been waiting on the island with Haydée, whom she loves as a sister. She learns from his letter that her father is mad and her brother dead, and she is reunited with Maximilian."}],"moments":[{"chapter":51,"text":"Confessed her love and unhappiness to Maximilian through the garden gate."},{"chapter":59,"text":"Interpreted Noirtier's signals as he made his will."},{"chapter":70,"text":"Signalled to Maximilian across the ballroom with her bouquet of forget-me-nots."},{"chapter":71,"text":"Fainted on hearing of her grandfather's death."},{"chapter":73,"text":"Introduced Maximilian to Noirtier and declared she would be his alone."},{"chapter":78,"text":"Told Maximilian 'We are saved!' after the engagement was broken."},{"chapter":80,"text":"Was accused of the poisonings by d'Avrigny, though innocent."},{"chapter":93,"text":"Collapsed in Noirtier's room after drinking a glass of water."},{"chapter":100,"text":"Was visited at night by the Count, who told her that her drinks were poisoned."},{"chapter":101,"text":"Swallowed a pill from the Count's emerald box, promising to fear nothing."},{"chapter":102,"text":"Was found lifeless in her bed and pronounced dead."},{"chapter":104,"text":"Was buried at Père-Lachaise after a crowded funeral."},{"chapter":117,"text":"Appeared alive at Maximilian's side."}]},{"id":"abbe-adelmonte","name":"Abbé Adelmonte","firstChapter":52,"importance":"minor","group":"History & legend","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":52,"text":"A Sicilian ‘terrible chemist’ the Count claims as a friend."}],"description":[{"from":52,"text":"The Abbé Adelmonte of Taormina is a Sicilian chemist the Count tells Madame de Villefort about. He supposedly showed how poison passed along a chain of animals could kill without trace, leaving only signs of apoplexy, and is said to have made the Count's elixir."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"louise-darmilly","name":"Louise d'Armilly","firstChapter":53,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Danglars household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":53,"text":"Eugénie Danglars' schoolfriend, a talented young singer without fortune."},{"from":97,"text":"Eugénie's companion in her flight from Paris."}],"description":[{"from":53,"text":"Louise d'Armilly is a talented young singer without fortune, a schoolfellow of Eugénie Danglars who practices music with her every day. Eugénie envies her independent life as an artist, though she avoids being seen with her in public."},{"from":76,"text":"Small, delicate and fairy-like, she is Eugénie's singing teacher and confidante, often staying at the Danglars house, and will not say a word to Albert. She is going to Italy, and the Count has given her letters of introduction to theatre directors there."},{"from":97,"text":"Timid but devoted, she bought the post-chaise and keeps the money for Eugénie's flight, and travels with her, posing as her sister. When Andrea is cornered at their inn she pities him and urges him to escape."}],"moments":[{"chapter":96,"text":"Thanked the Count for her letters of introduction, saying she meant to use them at once."},{"chapter":97,"text":"Escaped from Paris with Eugénie."},{"chapter":98,"text":"Rang for help at the inn, then urged the cornered Andrea to flee."}]},{"id":"andrea-cavalcanti","name":"Andrea Cavalcanti","firstChapter":54,"importance":"major","group":"Paris society","aliases":[{"name":"Benedetto","from":62,"note":"his real name; Bertuccio recognizes him"},{"name":"Prince Cavalcanti","from":76,"note":"the title Paris society and Danglars give him"},{"name":"Convict No. 59","from":82,"note":"his number in the galleys, as Caderousse tells it"}],"tagline":[{"from":54,"text":"Major Cavalcanti's son, a young man the Count promises to launch in Paris."},{"from":56,"text":"A hired impostor playing Major Cavalcanti's long-lost son."},{"from":62,"text":"Really Benedetto, Bertuccio's foundling: son of Villefort and Madame Danglars."},{"from":81,"text":"Sham prince engaged to Eugénie Danglars, and a former galley convict."},{"from":83,"text":"Caderousse's murderer, still posing as a fashionable prince."},{"from":96,"text":"Exposed as an escaped convict and murderer at his own contract signing."},{"from":98,"text":"Benedetto, in prison awaiting trial for Caderousse's murder."},{"from":110,"text":"Benedetto, who named Villefort as his father in open court."}],"description":[{"from":54,"text":"Andrea is the son of the Italian nobleman Major Cavalcanti, described by the Count as a charming young man of about Albert's age. He is about to enter Paris society backed by his father's millions, and the Count has promised to keep an eye on him."},{"from":56,"text":"The story goes that he was stolen from his father at the age of five. He is tall, with light hair, a red beard and black eyes, quick-witted and a fluent liar who reads legal papers like an expert. In fact he is a hired player: promised 50,000 livres a year to act as the major's son, he starts in fear at the news of his ‘father’, and the Count hints that his youth was ‘stormy’."},{"from":62,"text":"At the dinner at Auteuil, Bertuccio recognizes him on sight: ‘Andrea Cavalcanti’ is Benedetto. He is the newborn that Villefort buried alive in that garden, and that Bertuccio, after stabbing Villefort, dug up and revived. The baby was left at a foundling hospital, reclaimed by Bertuccio's widowed sister-in-law Assunta, and raised in Corsica."},{"from":62,"text":"There the clever, spoiled boy became a liar and a thief who ran with criminals by eleven. In the end, with two companions, he held Assunta over the fire for her money, robbed the house and vanished. The pregnant woman Bertuccio saw with Villefort that night, now recognized as Madame Danglars, is his mother."},{"from":64,"text":"Leaving the dinner, he is ambushed by Caderousse, who knew him in the past as Benedetto. Andrea says he was brought up in Corsica, and agrees to pay Caderousse 200 francs a month to keep quiet. He carries a pistol, and was alarmed to find the king's prosecutor at the table."},{"from":76,"text":"Within a fortnight he is accepted everywhere, rumored heir to the Saravezza quarries and flattered by Danglars as ‘Prince’. Vain and polished, though his low birth shows through, he openly courts Eugénie with her father's blessing, while the Count pointedly refuses to vouch for him."},{"from":81,"text":"He proposes to Eugénie and is accepted. Caderousse was his comrade in the galleys at Toulon, and they escaped together. Andrea claims to believe the Count is his real father and has left him money in his will. Under pressure from Caderousse, he draws him a plan of the Count's house and tells him when it will be empty."},{"from":83,"text":"Caught breaking into the Count's house, Caderousse tells the abbé that Andrea is Benedetto, a Corsican foundling and convict No. 59. Climbing down from the wall, Caderousse is stabbed. Before he dies he says Benedetto gave him the plan hoping one would kill the other, then lay in wait, and signs a deposition naming him as the murderer."},{"from":96,"text":"Boastful and greedy, he frets over the dowry and the three million. At the signing of the marriage contract, when the Count tells of Caderousse's bloodstained waistcoat and a letter found in it, he slips away just before police arrive to arrest him."},{"from":98,"text":"Without remorse and full of resource, he steals jewels from the wedding presents and nearly escapes through the chimneys, but is trapped at an inn in Compiègne, falling into the room of Eugénie and Louise. Cornered, he refuses to kill himself, saying ‘one has friends’, and is taken to prison in Paris."},{"from":99,"text":"Villefort, preparing the case, describes him as a Corsican vagabond of unknown parents, sent to the galleys at sixteen for forgery, who escaped and is now accused of murder."},{"from":107,"text":"In prison he still poses as a Cavalcanti prince, sure a powerful protector will save him, and suspects the Count is his father. Bertuccio, his ‘adopted father’, visits and warns him he has fallen into terrible hands, promising to tell him his true father's name."},{"from":110,"text":"At his trial he declares that he was born at Auteuil, the son of the prosecutor M. de Villefort, who buried him alive, and that his mother believed him born dead. Rescued, left at a foundling hospital and raised in Corsica, he has no name of his own and blames his father for everything he became."}],"moments":[{"chapter":56,"text":"Asked the ‘major’, as soon as they were alone, how much he was being paid to be his father."},{"chapter":64,"text":"Agreed to pay Caderousse 200 francs a month to keep him quiet."},{"chapter":76,"text":"Was invited into Eugénie's music room and sang a Corsican song."},{"chapter":81,"text":"Was accepted as Eugénie's suitor, and drew Caderousse a plan of Monte Cristo's house."},{"chapter":83,"text":"Was named by the dying Caderousse as his murderer."},{"chapter":96,"text":"Vanished just as police came to arrest him at his own contract signing."},{"chapter":98,"text":"Was arrested at Compiègne and imprisoned in the Conciergerie."},{"chapter":107,"text":"Escaped a beating from the convicts with a bandits' secret sign taught him by Caderousse."},{"chapter":110,"text":"Named the prosecutor, M. de Villefort, as his father in open court."}]},{"id":"bartolomeo-cavalcanti","name":"Major Bartolomeo Cavalcanti","firstChapter":54,"importance":"supporting","group":"Paris society","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":54,"text":"An Italian nobleman of ancient family whom the Count expects in Paris."},{"from":55,"text":"A shabby impostor paid to play a Lucca nobleman and Andrea's father."},{"from":76,"text":"Andrea's sham father, gone back to the gaming tables of Lucca."}],"description":[{"from":54,"text":"The Count describes Major Cavalcanti as a polite, modest Italian nobleman of very ancient family whom he met in Florence, Bologna and Lucca."},{"from":56,"text":"In reality he is a man of about fifty-two in shabby, old-fashioned clothes who barely knows the story he is supposed to tell: that he is a rich noble of Lucca and former Austrian major whose son was stolen as a child. He is paid 50,000 francs to play the father, cares mainly about the money, and agrees with Andrea to play the game to the end without asking who is behind it."},{"from":66,"text":"Dressed in a major's uniform covered with medals, he looks every inch the old soldier and wisely says little at the Count's dinner. He turns his banknotes into a large diamond ring, and Danglars, dazzled, opens an account for him and his son."},{"from":76,"text":"His part played, he leaves Paris and goes back to gambling at the baths of Lucca, leaving Andrea the family papers."},{"from":84,"text":"Letters in his name approve Andrea's marriage from Parma and promise a wedding gift; later, too ‘infirm’ to travel, he has a representative sign the contract for him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":55,"text":"Received 8,000 francs on account and was told his ‘lost son’ Andrea was waiting in the next room."},{"chapter":56,"text":"Admitted to Andrea that he was paid to play his father."},{"chapter":63,"text":"Innocently asked at dinner what is done to child-murderers in France."},{"chapter":66,"text":"Cashed a 40,000-franc bond with Danglars and opened a monthly allowance for his son."}]},{"id":"barrois","name":"Barrois","firstChapter":58,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":58,"text":"Noirtier's faithful old servant, loyal to no other master."},{"from":79,"text":"Noirtier's devoted servant, who died after drinking his master's lemonade."}],"description":[{"from":58,"text":"Noirtier's servant of twenty-five years, who recognizes no master but Noirtier and anticipates his every wish, ignoring Villefort when he must."},{"from":73,"text":"He guards Noirtier's door, shows Maximilian out by a hidden passage, and alone knows the secret spring of his master's old desk."},{"from":74,"text":"With unusual firmness he announces to the assembled family that Noirtier demands to see Franz at once."},{"from":79,"text":"About sixty, he made Noirtier's lemonade and left it in the pantry. Hot and thirsty, he drank a glass and died in convulsions."},{"from":80,"text":"D'Avrigny says he died in another's place, poisoned by a dose meant for his master, but publicly gives the cause as apoplexy."}],"moments":[{"chapter":58,"text":"Went off to fetch a notary for his master despite Villefort's objections."},{"chapter":74,"text":"Announced that Noirtier demanded to see Franz immediately."},{"chapter":79,"text":"Died in convulsions after drinking Noirtier's lemonade."}]},{"id":"general-depinay","name":"the elder d'Épinay","firstChapter":58,"importance":"supporting","group":"Royal court & politics","aliases":[{"name":"General d'Épinay","from":60,"note":""},{"name":"General de Quesnel","from":60,"note":"the Count calls Franz the son of General de Quesnel, created Baron d'Épinay; the same general whose disappearance is reported in ch 11"},{"name":"General Flavien de Quesnel, Baron d'Épinay","from":75,"note":"his full name"}],"tagline":[{"from":58,"text":"Franz d'Épinay's father, assassinated in 1815; Noirtier's old political enemy."},{"from":60,"text":"Franz's father, the royalist General de Quesnel, killed after a Bonapartist meeting."},{"from":75,"text":"Franz's father, killed by Noirtier in a fair duel in February 1815."}],"description":[{"from":58,"text":"Franz d'Épinay's father, assassinated in 1815; his killers were never found. He and Noirtier were bitter political enemies."},{"from":60,"text":"He was General de Quesnel, created Baron d'Épinay, who served under Napoleon but kept royalist sympathies. He was killed one evening after leaving a Bonapartist meeting he had been invited to, the same disappearance the police were investigating in 1815. Villefort calls him a friend."},{"from":75,"text":"Noirtier's signed record tells the truth: invited to the Bonapartist club, the general refused to break his oath to Louis XVIII, was made to swear silence, then insulted the club's president, Noirtier. They fought a fair duel on the Quai des Ormes on 5 February 1815; the general was killed and his body thrown into the river."}],"moments":[{"chapter":75,"text":"Was revealed to have died in a duel with Noirtier, not by assassination."}]},{"id":"deschamps","name":"M. Deschamps","firstChapter":59,"importance":"minor","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":59,"text":"The Villefort family notary."}],"description":[{"from":59,"text":"The Villefort family notary, who takes charge of Noirtier's sealed will. He later presides over the signing of Valentine's marriage contract and tells Franz about the will."}],"moments":[{"chapter":74,"text":"Presided over the interrupted signing of Valentine's marriage contract."}]},{"id":"telegraph-operator","name":"the man at the telegraph","firstChapter":61,"importance":"minor","group":"Paris society","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":61,"text":"Gentle signal-tower operator whom the Count bribes to send false news."}],"description":[{"from":61,"text":"A poor, gentle man of fifty-five who has worked the Montlhéry signal tower for fifteen years at 1,000 francs a year. He loves his tiny garden and his war on the dormice, and repeats signals without understanding them. The Count pays him to send a false message."}],"moments":[{"chapter":61,"text":"Sent the Count's false signals, then fainted with emotion."}]},{"id":"baron-de-nargonne","name":"M. de Nargonne","firstChapter":65,"importance":"minor","group":"The Danglars household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":65,"text":"Madame Danglars' late first husband."}],"description":[{"from":65,"text":"M. de Nargonne was Madame Danglars' first husband, a baron. According to Danglars, he died of grief or rage when he came home after nine months away and found his wife six months pregnant."},{"from":67,"text":"His title explains the baron's coronet and the letter H embroidered on Hermine's linen."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"cornelie","name":"Cornélie","firstChapter":65,"importance":"minor","group":"The Danglars household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":65,"text":"Madame Danglars' confidential maid."}],"description":[{"from":65,"text":"Mademoiselle Cornélie is Madame Danglars' trusted maid."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"jacopo-manfredi","name":"Jacopo Manfredi","firstChapter":66,"importance":"minor","group":"The Danglars household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":66,"text":"Trieste banker whose failure costs Danglars about a million francs."}],"description":[{"from":66,"text":"Jacopo Manfredi is a banker of Trieste, long a reliable client of Danglars, who has suspended payments and cost him about a million francs."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"dr-davrigny","name":"M. d'Avrigny","firstChapter":72,"importance":"supporting","group":"The Villefort household","aliases":[{"name":"Doctor d'Avrigny","from":73,"note":""}],"tagline":[{"from":72,"text":"The Villeforts' family doctor and old friend."},{"from":73,"text":"Family doctor who suspects poison in the Villefort house but agrees to keep quiet."},{"from":102,"text":"Family doctor who pronounced Valentine dead and found poison in her glass."}],"description":[{"from":72,"text":"A brilliant doctor and old friend of the family who was present at Valentine's birth. He has a daughter, Madeleine. He finds the marquise's symptoms strange."},{"from":73,"text":"He is convinced Madame de Saint-Méran died of brucine or strychnine, poisons that mimic tetanus; he has been giving Noirtier brucine as treatment. He agrees to keep silent but warns Villefort that the evil may not stop."},{"from":79,"text":"When Barrois dies, he questions everyone and proves with syrup of violets that the lemonade was poisoned, linking the death to the marquise's."},{"from":80,"text":"He believes the poison was meant for Noirtier, protected by his brucine treatment, and accuses Valentine because she gains from every death. When Villefort refuses to act, he agrees to keep silent but vows never to return to the house."},{"from":94,"text":"He comes back when Valentine collapses, is astonished she survives, and works out how Noirtier has been protecting her. He orders that no one give her anything without his instructions."},{"from":102,"text":"He declares Valentine dead and, testing her drink, identifies a new poison. He regrets that his earlier silence encouraged the murderer, but agrees to keep the secret and arranges the formalities."}],"moments":[{"chapter":73,"text":"Agreed to stay silent about the poisoning but warned Villefort to watch carefully."},{"chapter":79,"text":"Proved Barrois had been poisoned."},{"chapter":80,"text":"Accused Valentine of the poisonings, then withdrew from the house."},{"chapter":94,"text":"Returned to treat Valentine and took charge of everything she drank."},{"chapter":102,"text":"Declared Valentine dead and found poison in her glass."}]},{"id":"kourchid","name":"Kourchid","firstChapter":77,"importance":"minor","group":"Greece & Yanina","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":77,"text":"The sultan’s commander sent to capture Ali Pasha."}],"description":[{"from":77,"text":"The Seraskier Kourchid was the sultan’s commander sent against Ali Pasha. His soldiers killed Selim and Ali, and he handed Vasiliki and Haydée over to their new master."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"selim","name":"Selim","firstChapter":77,"importance":"minor","group":"Greece & Yanina","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":77,"text":"Ali Pasha’s faithful soldier, who guarded his gold and gunpowder."}],"description":[{"from":77,"text":"Ali Pasha’s favourite soldier, who guarded a cavern of gold and gunpowder with a lit match, ready to blow it all up on Ali’s signal. He was stabbed as soon as he put out the match."},{"from":86,"text":"Haydée accuses Morcerf of having stabbed him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":77,"text":"Put out the match on seeing Ali’s ring and was murdered."}]},{"id":"vasiliki","name":"Vasiliki","firstChapter":77,"importance":"minor","group":"Greece & Yanina","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":77,"text":"Haydée’s mother, Ali Pasha’s beloved Christian wife."}],"description":[{"from":77,"text":"Haydée’s mother and Ali Pasha’s beloved wife, a Christian. After Ali’s death she was handed over to the man most responsible for it, sold to slave merchants, and died at the gates of Constantinople at the sight of Ali’s head."},{"from":86,"text":"The bill of sale shown to the Chamber of Peers proves that she and Haydée were sold together by Fernand Mondego."}],"moments":[{"chapter":77,"text":"Died in Constantinople at the sight of her husband’s head."}]},{"id":"florentin","name":"Florentin","firstChapter":85,"importance":"minor","group":"The Morcerf household","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":85,"text":"Albert’s valet, who rode to Normandy with the bad news."}],"description":[{"from":85,"text":"Albert’s valet, who rides fifteen hours to bring him Beauchamp’s letter and the newspaper naming his father."}],"moments":[{"chapter":85,"text":"Brought Albert the news of the second article."}]},{"id":"el-kobbir","name":"El-Kobbir","firstChapter":86,"importance":"minor","group":"Greece & Yanina","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":86,"text":"The slave merchant who bought Haydée and her mother from Fernand."}],"description":[{"from":86,"text":"A slave merchant who supplied the sultan’s harem. His document records buying Vasiliki and Haydée from Fernand Mondego for 400,000 francs, and Haydée’s later purchase by the Count of Monte Cristo for an emerald."}],"moments":[]}]`)};export{t as default};